<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/29th-joint-glycobiology-meeting-2018> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:contact """Program\r
jgm2018@lists.ugent.be\r
\r
VIB Conference team\r
+32 9 244 66 11\r
conferences@vib.be""" ;
    schema1:description """Theme of the conference\r
Glycobiology is the study of complex carbohydrates in biological and biotechnological context. This is a very broad field that is bound by common methodologies to analyze glycoconjugate structures, to synthesize these structures and to understand their myriad roles in biological systems. The field of glycobiology is growing fast, especially since it was shown that carbohydrate interactions play an important role in many biological processes and are of great interest for biomedical and biomolecular research, and the applications emerging from it.\r
\r
Objective of the conference\r
This conference is organized annually by the working groups on glycobiology of the national associations for biochemistry and molecular biology of Germany, The Netherlands, Belgium and France. In 2018, the 29th edition of the Joint Glycobiology Meeting will be organized in Belgium. The main goal of the conference is to give an opportunity to young researchers to present their glycobiology work to colleagues and principal investigators. Furthermore, a number of distinguished keynote speakers is invited to present their recent data and the state-of-the-art technology in their field of expertise. The meeting provides ample opportunities for informal discussions between all the attendees, to foster new ideas for research and possibilities for collaborations.\r
\r
Poster information:\r
Format: A0 (841 x 1189 mm / 33.1 x 46.8 in), portrait orientation\r
""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2018-10-23 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Gent" ;
                    schema1:addressRegion "Oost-Vlaanderen" ;
                    schema1:postalCode "9000" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Thagaste, 1, Academiestraat" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "51.05921" ;
            schema1:longitude "3.719865" ] ;
    schema1:name "29th Joint Glycobiology meeting 2018" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Conferences" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2018-10-21 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://vibconferences.be/event/29th-joint-glycobiology-meeting-2018> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/2eme-ecole-de-bioinformatique-aviesan> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "EBA 2013 - 2" ;
    schema1:description """La formation est une initiation à l’utilisation des outils bioinformatiques permettant d’aborder la diversité des applications du NGS. Cette école, qui se veut généraliste, sera organisée en deux groupes thématiques principaux: (1) régulation, transcriptome et épigénome et (2) variations génomiques. Elle couvrira une série de techniques dérivées du séquençage à haut débit: RNA-seq, ChIP-seq, identification et annotation de SNP, RAD-seq, assemblage de novo de RNA-seq. Le but de l’école est de couvrir plusieurs technologies largement utilisées, plutôt que de se concentrer sur une seule.\r
L’école sera basée sur des ateliers pratiques sous l’environnement convivial Galaxy.\r
Les participants sélectionnés pourront bénéficier d’un tutorat personnalisé pour discuter de leur plan d’analyse, et effectuer les premières étapes de traitement de leurs propres données ou de celles de leur plateforme (sans avoir la volonté de mener à bien l’analyse complète des données).""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2013-11-22 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "France" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Roscoff" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "48.725582" ;
                    schema1:longitude "-3.982898" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 40 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ABiMS (http://abims.sb-roscoff.fr/), AVIESAN (http://www.aviesan.fr/en), IFB (https://www.france-bioinformatique.fr/), IFB Core (https://www.france-bioinformatique.fr/)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2013-11-17 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "2ème Ecole de Bioinformatique AVIESAN" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "IFB French Institute of Bioinformatics" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.france-bioinformatique.fr> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://aviesan.fr/fr/aviesan/accueil/toute-l-actualite/2eme-ecole-de-bioinformatique-initiation-au-traitement-des-donnees-de-genomique-obtenues-par-sequencage-a-haut-debit/(sort_cat)/3726> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/2nd-advanced-analysis-of-quantitative-proteomics-data-using-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Michael Turewicz (bioinformatician) and Karin Schork (biostatistician) (BioInfra.Prot)\r
\r
Date:\r
Monday, 9th Nov 2020\r
\r
Location:\r
Online\r
\r
Contents:\r
In this course you will learn about using R for the analysis of proteomics data. We will focus on data preprocessing methods and advanced methods for data analysis. In this regard the cpurse will touch upon:\r
    • data normalization\r
    • quality control, handling of missing values\r
    • clustering, heatmaps\r
    • ROC-curves\r
\r
Please be aware that basic knowledge of R and methods for differential analysis of proteomics data are taught in our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” the previous day (Monday, 2nd Nov 2020, http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um)\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
    • Independent usage of R functions for\r
        - Data preprocessing\r
        - Plots and graphs\r
        - Statistical methods for data analysis\r
        - Use of additional R packages\r
    • Deeper understanding of statistical methods applied in differential analyses\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
    • Basic understanding of high-dimensional data sets from quantitative proteomics or other life sciences;\r
    • Basic knowledge of R (e.g. data import, basic plots, t-test, for loop) and basic knowledge of differential analysis of proteomics data. Both can for example be gained from our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” the previous day (Monday, 2nd Nov 2020, http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um).\r
    • Computer with stable internet connection, headset and camera\r
\r
Keywords:\r
R; high-throughput data; omics; proteomics; data analysis, graphics, data preprocessing\r
\r
Tools:\r
download and more information on R here:\r
https://cran.r-project.org/\r
\r
We recommend using an editor such as RStudio, see\r
www.rstudio.com""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2020-11-09 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2020-11-09 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "2nd Advanced analysis of quantitative proteomics data using R" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Germany" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/795-2nd-advanced-analysis-of-quantitative-proteomics-data-using-r> .

