<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/2nd-de-nbi-summer-school-2021-microbial-community-analysis> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Dr. Dirk Benndorf (Magdeburg, BiGi), Prof. Dr. Alexander Goesmann (Giessen, BiGi), Dr. Sebastian Jaenicke (Giessen, BiGi), Prof. Dr. Stefan Janssen (JLU Giessen), Sebastian Jünemann (Bielefeld, BiGi), Dr. Nils Kleinbölting (Bielefeld, BiGi), Kay Schallert (Magdeburg, BiGi), Prof. Dr. Alexander Sczyrba (Bielefeld, BiGi), Dr. Daniel Wibberg (Bielefeld, de.NBI Administration office), Dr. Georg Zeller (Heidelberg, HD-HuB), Dr. Robert Heyer (Magdeburg, BiGi), Jakob Wirbel (Heidelberg, HD-Hub), Dr. Alessio Milanese (ETH Zürich)\r
\r
Date:\r
04. - 08.10.21\r
\r
Location:\r
Online\r
\r
Content:\r
We are pleased to announce that the de.NBI Summer School 2021 on Microbial Community Data Analysis will take place from October 4th to 8th, 2021.\r
\r
The German Network for Bioinformatics Infrastructure (de.NBI) presents the Summer School on Microbial Community Data Analysis that is organized by the Bielefeld-Gießen (BiGi) Center for Microbial Bioinformatics. The BiGi center is specialized in the ﬁeld of Omics data analysis for microbes and is well-known for its software platforms such as EMGB, MetaProtServ, or MGX. During the event, we will cover topics such as sequence data preprocessing, amplicon data analysis (metabarcoding), read- and assembly based metagenomics, and metaproteomics.\r
\r
Program:\r
\r
*All times refere to Central European Summer Time (CEST)! The program includes talks, lectures and hands-on trainings that the participants will conduct on their own computers/laptops.\r
\r
Monday, 04.10.2021\r
\r
10:00-10:30 Welcome note (A. Goesmann)\r
10:30-11:00 Presentation of the de.NBI network (D. Wibberg)\r
11:00-12:00 Keynote by M. Ziegler\r
12:00-13:00 Lunch break\r
13:00-14:00 Keynote: Sequencing technologies (T. Hain)\r
14:00-14:15 Break\r
14:15-16:15 From raw data to feature tables (S. Janssen, S. Jaenicke)\r
16:15-17:00 Break\r
17:00-18:00 Keynote: Advancing microbiome research through Qiita (A. Gonzalez)\r
\r
Tuesday, 05.10.2021\r
\r
10:00-12:00 Assessing biodiversity (alpha/beta) (S. Janssen)\r
12:00-13:00 Lunch break\r
13:00-13:30 Statistical testing (S. Janssen)\r
13:30-13:45 Break\r
13:45-15:00 Tools for taxonomic classification (S. Jaenicke)\r
15:00-16:00 MGX (S. Jaenicke)\r
\r
Wednesday, 06.10.2021\r
\r
10:00-12:00 Metagenome assembly (A. Sczyrba, S. Jünemann, N. Kleinbölting)\r
12:00-13:00 Lunch break\r
13:00-14:00 Binning into MAGs (A. Sczyrba, S. Jünemann, N. Kleinbölting)\r
14:00-14:45 Annotation &amp; evaluation of MAGs (A. Sczyrba, S. Jünemann, N. Kleinbölting)\r
14:45-15:00 Break\r
15:00-16:00 Keynote: Critical assessment of metagenome interpretation - CAMI2 (A. McHardy)\r
\r
Thursday, 07.10.2021\r
\r
10:00-11:00 Keynote: Shinichi Sunagawa\r
11:00-11:15 Break\r
11:15-13:00 Taxonomic profiling of shotgun metagenomes (Georg Zeller, Alessio Milanese)\r
13:00-14:00 Lunch break\r
14:00-14:30 Taxonomic profiling of shotgun metagenomes (Georg Zeller, Alessio Milanese)\r
14:30-15:15 Comparative metagenome analysis (Georg Zeller)\r
15:15-15:30 Break\r
15:30-17:00 Comparative metagenome analysis (Jakob Wirbel)\r
\r
Friday, 08.10.2021\r
\r
10:00-11:00 Introduction in analyzing microbial communities by metaproteomics (Dirk Benndorf)\r
11:00-12:00 Identification of mass spectrometry data using the MetaProteomeAnalyzer (Robert Heyer)\r
12:00-13:00 Lunch break\r
13:00-14:00 Taxonomic and functional comparison of human gut samples using the MetaProteomeAnalyzer (Robert Heyer)\r
14:00-15:00 Biostatistical data evaluation and data visualization (Robert Heyer)\r
15:00-15:15 Break\r
15:15-16:00 Keynote: Metaproteomics: where are we now? A community-driven evaluation of experimental and computational techniques (Thilo Muth)\r
\r
Keynote speakers:\r
\r
    Dr. Maren Ziegler, Systematics and Biodiversity Lab, Justus Liebig University Giessen, Germany\r
    PD Dr. Torsten Hain, Medical Microbiology, Justus Liebig University Giessen, Germany\r
    PhD Antonio González Peña, University of California, San Diego, USA\r
    Prof. Dr. Alice McHardy, Helmholtz Centre for Infection Research, Braunschweig, Germany\r
    Prof. Dr. Shinichi Sunagawa, Department of Biology, ETH Zurich, Switzerland\r
    Dr. Thilo Muth, Bundesanstalt für Materialforschung und -prüfung (BAM), Berlin, Germany\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
Participants need:\r
- Basic knowledge in Linux/CLI\r
- Basic knowledge of executing programs on the command line (shell)\r
- Working knowledge of R\r
\r
Registration:\r
Please fill out the application form to sign up. The number of participants is limited to 20! The application deadline is September 12th.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-10-08 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "2nd de.NBI Summer School 2021 - Microbial Community Analysis " ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-10-04 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/1269-de-nbi-summer-school-2021-part-2-microbial-community-analysis> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/2nd-galaxy-elixir-webinar-series-open-data-infrastructures-to-tackle-covid-19-pandemic> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The Galaxy Community and ELIXIR organise a second webinar series to demonstrate how open software and public research infrastructures can be used in analysing and publishing SARS-CoV2 data.\r
\r
In a series of six webinar sessions, experts from ELIXIR and the global Galaxy community from the US, Australia and Europe will demonstrate how open access and open science are fundamental for fast and efficient response to public health crises. The series follows on the first Galaxy-ELIXIR webinar series organised in April-May 2020. It will introduce the latest Galaxy tools developed for working with SARS-CoV-2 data and discuss some of the challenges in accessing, analysing and interpreting them.\r
\r
The kick-off session of the webinar series takes place on 20 January 2021.\r
\r
More information about Galaxy analyses of COVID-19 data: covid19.galaxyproject.org\r
\r
Programme\r
\r
Session 1: COVID-19 analysis in Galaxy: Lessons learned and introduction to the series\r
\r
\r
	20 January 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	The first session will summarise the developments in Galaxy the past eight months and introduce the latest tools for COVID-19 related research:\r
\r
	\r
		WorkflowHub, Dockstore\r
		Single-tool view, simplified workflow run-form\r
		Scaling and performance improvements over the last 9 month\r
		Paper-Cut event(s)\r
		Galaxy plans for the next years\r
	\r
\r
	Speakers: \r
\r
	\r
		Anton Nekrutenko\r
		Marius van den Beek\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Session 2: Importance of (open) infrastructures in responding to a pandemic\r
\r
\r
	27 January 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	The second session will present some of the data resources used to deposit and access SARS-CoV2 data. You will also discuss the critical importance of open access data to respond to epidemic outbreaks efficiently.\r
\r
	\r
		Data resources\r
		Analysis resources\r
		Interoperability\r
	\r
\r
	Speakers: \r
\r
	\r
		Andrew Lonie\r
		Guy Cochrane\r
		Björn Grüning\r
		Frederik Coppens\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Session 3: Supporting the COVID-19 Data portal: viral data cleaning from human reads and submission to ENA\r
\r
\r
	3 February 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	This session will present the COVID-19 Data Portal and the tools clean and submit data to open access repositories:\r
\r
	\r
		Submission tool to European Nucleotide Archive\r
		Data preprocessing and cleaning\r
	\r
\r
	Speakers: \r
\r
	\r
		Ignacio Eguinoa \r
		Bert Droesbeke\r
		Frederik Coppens\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Session 4: Insights from selection analysis of complete genomes and read-level data\r
\r
\r
	10 February 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	Speakers: \r
\r
	\r
		Sergei Pond\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Session 5: Viral Beacon and Galaxy variant workflows\r
\r
\r
	17 February 2021 | 17.00–18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	Speakers:\r
\r
	\r
		Bjoern Gruening\r
		Beatriz Serrano-Solano\r
		Babita Singh\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Session 6: DRS, long-read-sequencing, proteomics and more — an update to recent COVID-19 workflow developments\r
\r
\r
	24 February 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
	Speakers: \r
\r
	\r
		Wolfgang Maier\r
		Nathan Roach\r
		Pratik Jagtap\r
		Subina Mehta\r
	\r
\r
	Register\r
\r
\r
Acknowledgement\r
\r
The analyses have been performed using the Galaxy platform and open source tools from BioConda. Tools were run using XSEDE resources maintained by the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC ), Pittsburgh Supercomputing Center (PSC), and Indiana University in the U.S., de.NBI,  VSC cloud resources and  IFB cluster resources on the European side, STFC-IRIS at the Diamond Light Source, and ARDC cloud resources in Australia.\r
\r
                                                               """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-02-24 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "2nd Galaxy-ELIXIR webinar series: Open Data Infrastructures to tackle COVID-19 pandemic" ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-01-20 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/2nd-galaxy-elixir-webinar-series> .

