<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/a-practical-introduction-to-bioinformatics-and-rna-seq-using-galaxy> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0102>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3168>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Job seeker" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs)" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Early Career Researchers (ECRs)" ] ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """- No formal training in bioinformatics or genetics is needed. Only basic knowledge of genetics is required, like: What is DNA, RNA? or What is a gene?\r
- Knowledge about sequencing techniques is a plus\r
- There is no need for programming or informatics skills \r
""" ;
    schema1:description """This practical introduction to bioinformatics will explain key concepts of sequencing data analysis as well as a broader overview on bioinformatics data analysis.\r
\r
Data analysis is a job in high demand. Already in 2012 a Harvard business review article declared data science the ‘sexiest job of the 21st century’ and still in ‘2022 and beyond’ it is an excellent career choice. Furthermore, studying the healthcare job market during Covid-19, we can clearly see a shift to analytical skills as a requirement in new job offers.\r
\r
RNA-sequencing revolutionised genomics and health care research, by being a high-throughput and comparatively low-cost method. Together with the development of computational tools to enable their analysis, it has fundamentally improved our understanding of RNA biology. \r
\r
Galaxy is a worldwide open source project with the European Galaxy server being the biggest instance in Europe with more than 70,000 users. The Freiburg Galaxy Team is hosting this server out of Freiburg. Through Galaxy as a gateway, we are offering free access to databases and more than 3,200 bioinformatics tools which can be used through a graphical user interface. \r
\r
Within this workshop you will learn how to use Galaxy and how to perform a full RNA-seq data analysis. However, RNA-seq data is just one of many different kinds of data analysed in bioinformatics. Therefore, the last workshop day will provide an overview of the wide range of bioinformatics databases and data formats.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-09-08 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Co-funded by the European Commission" ],
                [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "101100604 - DIGITAL-2022-TRAINING-02" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "<a href=\"http://biont-training.eu/\">BioNT</a> (BIO Network for Training) " ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-09-04 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Databases",
        "Galaxy",
        "Mapping",
        "Quality Control",
        "RNA-Seq",
        "Sequence Analysis" ;
    schema1:name "A practical introduction to bioinformatics and RNA-seq using Galaxy" ;
    schema1:teaches """By the end of this workshop, you will be able to:\r
\r
- Interact with the data analysis platform Galaxy\r
- Get data from external sources into Galaxy\r
- Run tools on Galaxy and create a workflow\r
- Perform and interpret quality control of sequencing data\r
- Map reads on a reference genome and evaluate the mapping quality\r
- Explain the principle and specificity of mapping of RNA-seq data to an eukaryotic reference genome\r
- Select and run a state-of-the-art mapping tool for RNA-seq data\r
- Estimate the number of reads per gene\r
- Explain the count normalization to perform before sample comparison\r
- Construct and run a differential gene expression analysis\r
- Analyze the DESeq2 output to identify, annotate, and visualize differentially expressed genes\r
- Understanding different bioinformatic data formats\r
- Overview over different bioinformatics data bases""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.cecam.org/workshop-details/1260> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/a-practical-introduction-to-bioinformatics-and-rna-seq-using-galaxy-0485e1cf-80d3-4cd4-8f68-86c15c5967a5> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Pavan Videm, Teresa Müller (RBC)\r
\r
Date:\r
10.09.2024 – 13.09.2024\r
09:00 to 13:00 (CEST)\r
\r
Location:\r
Online\r
\r
Contents:\r
This practical introduction to bioinformatics will explain key concepts of sequencing data analysis as well as provide a broader overview on bioinformatics data analysis.\r
\r
Data analysis is a job in high demand. As early as 2012, a Harvard Business Review article declared data science the “sexiest job of the 21st century”, and it remains an excellent career choice in “2022 and beyond”. Furthermore, a study of the healthcare job market during COVID-19 clearly showed a shift towards analytical skills as a requirement in new job offers.\r
\r
RNA-seq has revolutionised genomics and health care research by being a high-throughput and comparatively low-cost method. Along with the development of computational tools for its analysis, RNA-seq has fundamentally improved our understanding of RNA biology.\r
\r
Galaxy is a global open-source project with the European Galaxy server being the largest instance in Europe, serving more than 70,000 users. The Freiburg Galaxy Team hosts this server out of Freiburg. Through Galaxy as a gateway, we offer free access to databases and more than 3,200 bioinformatics tools which can be used through a graphical user interface. \r
\r
In this workshop, you will learn how to use Galaxy and how to perform a complete RNA-seq data analysis. However, RNA-seq data is just one of many different kinds of data analysed in bioinformatics. \r
\r
To provide an outlook on what is possible in the field of bioinformatics beyond RNA-seq data analysis, this course includes additional training materials that will be linked at the end of the course. You are encouraged to review this material on your own during the week following  the course. To address your questions about the additional materials and other course content, we will provide an open question document where you can submit any course related questions during this week.\r
\r
Learning Goals:\r
By the end of this workshop, you will be able to:\r
\r
Interact with the data analysis platform Galaxy\r
Get data from external sources into Galaxy\r
Run tools on Galaxy and create a workflow\r
Perform and interpret quality control of sequencing data\r
Map reads on a reference genome and evaluate the mapping quality\r
Explain the principle and specificity of mapping of RNA-seq data to an eukaryotic reference genome\r
Select and run a state-of-the-art mapping tool for RNA-seq data\r
Estimate the number of reads per gene\r
Explain the count normalisation to perform before sample comparison\r
Construct and run a differential gene expression analysis\r
Analyse the DESeq2 output to identify, annotate, and visualise differentially expressed genes\r
Understanding different bioinformatic data formats\r
Prerequisites:\r
\r
No formal training in bioinformatics or genetics is needed. Only basic knowledge of genetics is required, like: What is DNA, RNA? or What is a gene?\r
Knowledge about sequencing techniques is a plus\r
There is no need for programming or informatics skills \r
PC/Laptop with an up-to-date browser. Chrome, Safari and Firefox browsers are all supported (some older browsers, including Internet Explorer, may not be)\r
Register a Galaxy EU account before the workshop\r
European Galaxy Server (https://usegalaxy.eu)\r
Verify the confirmation email!\r
If you have any problem please contact: contact@usegalaxy.eu\r
Install the genome browser IGV if you would like you would like to inspect your data outside Galaxy\r
 \r
\r
Keywords:\r
Training, RNAseq, Galaxy \r
\r
Tools:\r
Galaxy""" ;
