<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/advanced-analysis-of-quantitative-proteomics-data-using-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Michael Turewicz (bioinformatician) and Karin Schork (biostatistician) (BioInfra.Prot)\r
\r
Date:\r
Tuesday, 19th Nov 2019\r
\r
Location:\r
Ruhr University Bochum, 44801 Bochum\r
\r
Contents:\r
In this course you will learn about using R for the analysis of proteomics data. We will focus on data preprocessing methods and advanced methods for data analysis. In this regard the cpurse will touch upon:\r
    • data normalization\r
    • quality control, handling of missing values\r
    • clustering, heatmaps\r
    • ROC-curves\r
\r
Please be aware that basic knowledge of R and methods for differential analysis of proteomics data are taught in our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” the previous day (Monday, 18th Nov 2019, http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um)\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
    • Independent usage of R functions for\r
    • Data preprocessing\r
    • Plots and graphs\r
    • Statistical methods for data analysis\r
    • Use of additional R packages\r
    • Deeper understanding of statistical methods applied in differential analyses\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
    • Basic understanding of high-dimensional data sets from quantitative proteomics or other life sciences;\r
    • Basic knowledge of R (e.g. data import, basic plots, t-test, for loop) and basic knowledge of differential analysis of proteomics data. Both can for example be gained from our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” the previous day (Monday, 18th Nov 2019, http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um).\r
    • Please bring your own laptop for the hands-on exercises!\r
\r
Keywords:\r
R; high-throughput data; omics; proteomics; data analysis, graphics, data preprocessing\r
\r
Tools:\r
download and more information on R here:\r
https://cran.r-project.org/\r
\r
We recommend using an editor such as RStudio, see\r
www.rstudio.com""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-11-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Germany" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Bochum" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Arnsberg" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Bochum" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "51.481845" ;
                    schema1:longitude "7.216236" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-11-19 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Advanced analysis of quantitative proteomics data using R" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
