<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/advanced-modeling-with-copasi-icsb-2019> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Sven Sahle (Heidelberg University), Frank Bergmann (Heidelberg University), Stefan Hoops (University of Virginia), Ursula Kummer (Heidelberg University), Pedro Mendes (University of Connecticut), Jürgen Pahle (Heidelberg University), Egils Stalidzans (University of Latvia) (de.NBI-SysBio / de.NBI-ModSim)\r
\r
Date:\r
Thursday, October 31st, 2019\r
\r
Links:\r
http://denbi-modsim.de/icsb_copasi_2019/\r
Registration: https://forms.gle/NepBmMjrXmTzuCpy9\r
\r
Location:\r
Room 3, Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology Graduate University\r
Okinawa, Japan\r
\r
Contents:\r
COPASI is a widely used software tool for creating, simulating and analysing models of biochemical reaction networks. It is open source free software and available for all major operating systems. Its features include deterministic and stochastic simulation, steady state analysis, stoichiometric analysis, optimisation and parameter estimation, time scale analysis, sensitivities and metabolic control analysis, Lyapunov exponents, linear noise approximation, etc.\r
\r
In the workshop we will demonstrate the use of advanced features of COPASI, focusing on parameter estimation and parameter identifiability analysis, and the use of COPASI from scripting languages. For experienced users of the software, we will present an overview of new features added in the latest releases, such as stochastic differential equation models. A special session will demonstrate an optimisation workflow, highlighting how COPASI can be integrated in third party software projects. The developers of COPASI will also be available for the discussion of individual questions from the participants.\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
Laptop\r
\r
Keywords:\r
systems biology, software tools, quantitative models, parameter estimation, model simulation, metabolic networks, model databases, community standards\r
\r
Tools:\r
COPASI, """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2019-10-31 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Japan" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Präfektur Okinawa" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "26.120191" ;
            schema1:longitude "127.702501" ] ;
    schema1:name "Advanced modeling with COPASI - ICSB 2019 " ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2019-10-31 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/658-advanced-modeling-with-copasi-icsb-2019> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/advanced-modelling-with-copasi-2025> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Frank Bergmann (Heidelberg University), Stefan Hoops (University of Virginia), Ursula Kummer (Heidelberg University), Pedro Mendes (University of Connecticut), Jürgen Pahle (Heidelberg University), Sven Sahle (Heidelberg University)\r
\r
Date:\r
Oct 10, 2025, 9:30a - 4p (CEST / UTC+2)\r
\r
Location:\r
BioQUANT, Im Neuenheimer Feld 267, 69120 Heidelberg\r
\r
Contents:\r
COPASI is a widely used software tool for modeling, simulation, and analysis of biochemical reaction networks. Compatible with all major operating systems, it supports a wide range of computational methods—including deterministic and stochastic simulations, steady state analysis, optimization, sensitivity analysis, and more.\r
\r
This workshop focuses on advanced techniques in parameter estimation, including:\r
\r
Integration of heterogeneous data from time course and steady-state experiments\r
Identifiability assessments to determine meaningful parameters\r
Implementation of constraints and weighting strategies to refine models\r
Participants will also learn how to automate and customize analyses through scripting with COPASI, expanding their modeling capabilities beyond the graphical user interface.\r
\r
In the afternoon, participants are encouraged to bring their own models or parameter estimation challenges for a collaborative, hands-on session guided by experts.\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
This workshop is intended for experienced modellers with a solid grasp of COPASI. It builds on foundational skills and explores advanced strategies for analyzing complex biochemical models.\r
\r
Keywords:\r
Parameter Estimation, Scripting, COPASI, Systems Biology\r
\r
Tools:\r
COPASI, basiCO, COPASI R Connector • CoRC""" ;
