<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/application-of-recently-developed-fair-metrics-to-the-elixir-core-data-resources> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable) principles aim to maximize the discovery and reuse of digital resources. Using recently developed​ software and metrics to assess FAIRness, an ELIXIR Implementation Study looked at a subset of ELIXIR Core Data Resources to apply these technologies. \r
\r
In this webinar, the lead of the Implementation Study Michel Dumontier (ELIXIR Netherlands) will discuss their approach, findings, and lessons learned towards the understanding and promotion of the FAIR principles.\r
\r
This webinar is presented jointly by the  Pistoia Alliance and ELIXIR.\r
\r
Dr. Michel Dumontier is the Distinguished Professor of Data Science at Maastricht University and co-founder of the FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable) data principles. His research focuses on the development of computational methods for scalable and responsible discovery science. Previously at Stanford University, Dr. Dumontier now leads the interfaculty Institute of Data Science at Maastricht University to develop sociotechnological systems for accelerating scientific discovery, improving human health and well-being, and empowering communities with ethical data-driven decision making. He is a principal investigator in the Dutch National Research Agenda, the European Open Science Cloud, the NCATS Biomedical Data Translator, and the NIH Data Commons Pilots. He is internationally recognized for his contributions in bioinformatics, biomedical informatics, and semantic technologies including ontologies and linked data.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2019-10-17 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Application of recently developed FAIR metrics to the ELIXIR Core Data Resources" ;
    schema1:startDate "2019-10-17 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-fairness-CDR> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/applications-and-impacts-of-the-brapi-project-on-plant-breeding> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3489>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3810> ;
    schema1:description """Modern genomic breeding methods rely heavily on very large amounts of phenotypic and genotypic data, presenting new challenges in effective data management and integration. The datasets are often large and complex, and the data is often stored on multiple systems, sometimes separated by country and organisation. As the common analyses methods increasingly require aggregation of datasets from diverse sources, data exchange between disparate systems becomes a challenge.

This webinar will be an introduction to The Breeding API (BrAPI) Project. The BrAPI Project began in 2014 when a small group of plant breeding and technology experts came together to try to standardise their data. Since then, BrAPI has become internationally accepted as one of the primary data exchange standards in the plant breeding domain. This webinar will give an overview of what BrAPI is, how it works, what it is capable of, and the impact the project has had so far on the community.

 """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-04-26 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 1000 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-04-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Data standards",
        "Plant breeding",
        "Plant webinar series",
        "data managemnet",
        "data sharing" ;
    schema1:name "Applications and impacts of the BrAPI project on plant breeding" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/applications-and-impacts-brapi-project-plant-breeding> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/applications-of-artificial-intelligence-for-biosciences-in-latin-america> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """This webinar series builds on a newly developed curriculum on concepts, approaches, and applications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in biosciences. The series explores how AI and machine learning are being applied to biosciences research across Latin America. Researchers from Costa Rica, Argentina, and Mexico will share their experiences working with AI tools (such as AlphaFold), and discuss their applications in various fields, including biomedicine, metagenomics, and agriculture.  

This webinar series is part of the [BiotrAIn project](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/our-partnerships/biotrain), a collaboration between [University of Costa Rica](https://www.ucr.ac.cr/),  [CABANAnet](https://cabana.network/) and EMBL-EBI, funded by the Chan Zuckerberg initiative.   
  
**A bilingual series**  
This webinar series includes two webinars delivered in English and three in Spanish. For each webinar, captions in English and Spanish will be provided. All automatically generated captions will be reviewed by bilingual experts in the field, unless stated otherwise.  
  
**BiotrAIn Hub and Spokes course**  
We are currently inviting applications for the next BiotrAIn course “Concepts, approaches, and applications of artificial intelligence in bioscience”. This five-day programme will be delivered simultaneously across seven Latin American classroom locations, introducing AI and machine learning techniques for biological sciences. For more details on the program and how to apply to one of the classroom locations, you can visit the [dedicated page](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdAyOw_eXieXc11thgiXnEdDug1nHcBBN4vet7IGpiMGeegZA/viewform).  
Application deadline: 28 November 2025. """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-11-25 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-10-17 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Applications of artificial intelligence for biosciences in Latin America" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/applications-artificial-intelligence-biosciences-latin-america> .

