<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/applied-machine-learning-for-biological-data> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """Organizer &amp; Educators:\r
Silvia Di Giorgio (ZB MED – Information Centre for Life Sciences - Associated Partner), Sabry Razick &amp; Pubudu Samarakoon (University of Oslo)\r
\r
Date: \r
Module 1: 27-28.05.2025\r
Module 2: 02-06.06.2025 \r
\r
Location: \r
Online\r
\r
Contents:\r
This intensive workshop focuses on applying machine learning techniques to biological and genomic data, combining theoretical foundations with hands-on coding experience. Participants will work through real-world scenarios using Python-based tools and frameworks that are critical for modern bioinformatics.\r
\r
Module 1 (optional) provides a solid foundation in scientific computing with Python. Across two half-day sessions, participants will explore essential data handling techniques using NumPy and Pandas—tools widely adopted for manipulating and analyzing biological data. \r
\r
Module 2 (mandatory) spans five full days and begins by introducing core concepts in machine learning. On the first day, the participants will be introduced to unsupervised learning, and they will implement clustering algorithms and dimensionality reduction techniques using real-world genomics data. The workshop then dives into supervised learning with a focus on classification and regression, including logistic regression and tree-based methods. Participants will construct and evaluate ML models, perform cross-validation, and tune hyperparameters in hands-on sessions tailored to cancer genomics datasets. Later sessions introduce deep learning concepts and the PyTorch framework. Participants will learn to build and train simple neural networks and explore a deep learning-based bioinformatics tool used in genomic variant calling. The final day introduces accelerated genomics through GPU-powered workflows. Participants will learn about GPU technology and how to use containerized bioinformatics tools. They will also implement high-performance, GPU-accelerated pipelines using Parabricks.\r
\r
Please note: Registration is only required for participation in Module 2.  Module 1 is optional and does not require registration.\r
\r
This workshop offers a comprehensive, practical journey through the machine learning landscape in bioinformatics, from data wrangling to deep learning and scalable genomic workflows.\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
By the end of this workshop, you will be able to:\r
\r
Apply data manipulation techniques using NumPy and Pandas.\r
Define essential machine learning terminology and differentiate between supervised and unsupervised learning approaches.\r
Implement and evaluate regression and classification models on biological datasets through hands-on coding exercises.\r
Apply regularization techniques and hyperparameter tuning to optimize model performance while preventing overfitting.\r
Analyze biological questions to determine the most appropriate machine learning approach (regression, classification, clustering).\r
Interpret and evaluate machine learning models using appropriate metrics and cross-validation techniques to ensure reliability.\r
Develop scripts using PyTorch to build and train simple neural networks and implement deep learning based bioinformatics tools using genomics datasets. \r
Design end-to-end machine learning workflows for biological applications, from data preprocessing to model deployment.\r
Implement containerization using Docker to enhance reproducibility and scalability in bioinformatics workflows.\r
Compare CPU-native versus GPU-accelerated approaches for genomic data processing and identify computational bottlenecks.\r
Prerequisites:\r
\r
A life scientist, bioinformatician, or data analyst working with biological or genomic data\r
Curious about how machine learning can be applied to biological research questions\r
Looking to strengthen your Python skills for data handling and analysis\r
Interested in implementing classification, regression, or clustering models on real-world datasets\r
Exploring the use of deep learning techniques, in bioinformatics\r
Involved in next-generation sequencing (NGS) workflows and want to optimize them with GPU acceleration\r
Committed to building reproducible and scalable analysis pipelines using container technology\r
Eager to understand and apply best practices in model evaluation, tuning, and validation\r
New to machine learning and seeking a hands-on, structured introduction\r
Keywords:\r
Machine Learning \r
\r
Tools:\r
Python""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-06-06 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Germany" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "50.56912" ;
            schema1:longitude "10.449144" ] ;
    schema1:name "Applied Machine Learning for Biological Data" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI & ELIXIR-DE" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-05-27 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training-courses-2025/1881-applied-machine-learning-for-biological-data> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/applied-metaproteomics-workshop-2020> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Dirk Benndorf, Stephan Fuchs, Robert Heyer, Kay Schallert (BiGi/MetaProtServ)\r
\r
Date:\r
23.11.2020 – 27.11.2020\r
\r
Location:\r
Magdeburg, Germany (Universitätsplatz 2, Building 28.205)\r
\r
Contents:\r
Microbiome research is rapidly increasing. Meta-Omics data provide insights into microbial community influencing human health and biochemical cycle. Metaproteomics focusses on proteins representing the expressed genes and functions. In contrast to metagenomics or metatranscriptomics, the extraction and the analysis of proteins from human or environmental samples is challenging.\r
The de.NBI course on Applied Metaproteomics trains the participants to apply a complete workflow starting from design of experiments via sample preparation to measurement and bioinformatics analysis of high-resolution MS data. Maximum 10 participants are strongly encouraged to analyze their own samples to start their own metaproteomics project.\r
The workshop takes place at chair of bioprocess engineering at Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg. The team of microbial communities head by Dr. Dirk Benndorf is well experienced in metaproteomics. The samples will be measured on newly purchased timsTOF Pro MS. His group devellopes the MetaProteomAnalyzer service facilitating the analysis of of metaproteomic data. The provision of service is supported by the German Network for Bioinformatics Infrastructure - de.NBI.\r
\r
Monday\r
09:00-10:00   Arrival\r
10:00-11:00   Introduction to metaproteomics (lecture: Dirk Benndorf)\r
11:00-12:00   Design of experiments and sample preparation (lecture: Robert Heyer)\r
12:00-13:00   Lunch\r
13:00-18:00   Sample preparation I (protein extraction, protein quantification: Robert Heyer)\r
during the afternoon short presentations of participant´s about their samples (each participant max. 10 min)\r
\r
Tuesday\r
08:00-11:00   Sample preparation II (FASP digest, SDS-PAGE: Robert Heyer)\r
11:00-13:00   Introduction to high resolution mass spectrometry (lecture: Dirk Benndorf)\r
13:00-14:00   Lunch\r
13:00-14:00   Sample preparation II (peptide extraction: Robert Heyer)\r
14:00-16:00   Introduction to the MPA 2.0 (lecture: Kay Schallert)\r
non-stop: measurement of participant’s samples I\r
\r
Wednesday\r
non-stop: measurement of participant’s samples I: Dirk Benndorf, Patrick Hellwig\r
09:00-11:00   MPA 2.0 exercise I (working with a small reference dataset, db searching, metaprotein concept: Kay Schallert, Robert Heyer)\r
11:00-13:00   MPA 2.0 exercise II (loading user db, BLAST search of unknown hits, quantitative comparison of datasets, visualization and data export: Kay Schallert, Robert Heyer)\r
13:00-14:00   Lunch\r
14:00-17:00   Prophane workflow (lecture and training: Stefan Fuchs, external speaker)\r
\r
Thursday\r
09:00-13:00   MPA 2.0 exercise III (analyzing own data: Kay Schallert, Robert Heyer)\r
13:00-14:00   Lunch\r
14:00-17:00   Training at MS (calibration, setup of LC-MS methods, solvent exchange, trouble shooting, Patrick Hellwig)\r
\r
Friday\r
08:00-10:00   MPA 2.0 exercise IV (visualization of data: Robert Heyer)\r
10:00-12:00   Individual discussion of results and preparation of presentations (Dirk Benndorf, Robert Heyer, Kay Schallert)\r
12:00-13:00   Short presentations of participant‘s results and conclusion\r
13:00   Lunch and departure\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
To apply a complete workflow starting from design of experiments via sample preparation to measurement and bioinformatics analysis of high-resolution MS data.\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
Fee: 30 €\r
\r
Keywords:\r
Microbiome, MPA, Metaproteomics, MetaProteomeAnalyzer,\r
\r
Tools:\r
MetaproteomeAnalyzer""" ;
