<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/assertive-communication> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "Creating a culture in which employees at all levels feel comfortable speaking up is, to say the least, a challenge. It’s not just about learning an effective method for delivering an assertive message, but also about knowing when your voice is needed and having the courage to use it. It involves making the unwritten rules explicit, establishing clear agreements and communication, and developing self-awareness about your own challenges—so that speaking up becomes easier. Only then can you begin to train the right skills and truly master the art, both individually and as a team.In this training, we start with you and explore what holds you back from addressing someone directly. You’ll also discover how both you and others can be triggered by certain messages — and what the impact of that can be. To navigate around this, we’ll learn (or revisit) how to deliver these messages effectively without damaging collaboration." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-11-06 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Zwijnaarde" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "9052" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Ghent - VIB/UGent FSVM II, Technologiepark 75" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "51.000392" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.71677" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-11-06 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "communication skills",
        "personal effectiveness" ;
    schema1:name "Assertive communication" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/assertive-communication> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/assess-protein-dynamics-post-translational-modifications> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "The breakthrough of AlphaFold2 delivered structural models for nearly all proteins lacking experimental structures, opening new avenues to interrogate both sequence and structure. However, these models capture static snapshots rather than the dynamic conformational ensembles that govern protein behaviour. The Bio2Byte Tools, published as B2BTools, is a suite of sequence-based predictors that easily unveil the biophysical properties hidden in the amino acid language: backbone dynamics, early folding events, disordered positions, among other features. Developed by the lab led by Prof. Dr. Wim Vranken, this online platform is part of the ELIXIR Belgium node services. In parallel to this suite of tools, Scop3P, another ELIXIR Belgium node service, will allow you to explore and understand the impact of phospho-sites on human protein structure and function. It can serve as a springboard for researchers seeking to analyse and interpret a given phosphorylation site or phosphoprotein in a structural, biophysical, and biological context.This course is free for academic attendees thanks to specific sponsoring by ELIXIR Belgium." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2026-01-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Belgium" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Zwijnaarde" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "9052" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Ghent - VIB/UGent FSVM II, Technologiepark 75" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "51.000392" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.71677" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2026-01-19 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "ELIXIR",
        "advanced bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:name "Assess protein dynamics & post-translational modifications" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches "Able to correlate biophysical properties to post-translational modifications (PTMs)",
        "Calculate and analyse protein biophysical profiles for sequence-based data",
        "Compare the impact on protein dynamics by introducing mutations with B2BTools",
        "Perform exploratory structural analysis combining the AlphaFold predictions with biophysical profiles generated by the B2BTools" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/assess-protein-dynamics-post-translational-modifications> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/assessing-ethical-impacts-of-nanomaterials-in-dentistry> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """On Thursday 18th November 2021, the NanoCommons, NanoSolveIT, and RiskGone teams, in a joint initiative with the NanoSafety Cluster, will offer an online workshop/hackathon on their Ethic Impacts Assessment Tool.\r
\r
Ethical Impacts Assessment (EIA) targets nanomaterials applied to improve properties of components of dental materials and dental hygiene products. The dental materials include composites, adhesive systems, impression materials and dental implants. The dental hygiene products include toothpaste, toothbrushes, and mouth rinse. In literature, innovative applications of nanotechnology in nanobiosensors for detecting oral cancer and nano-drug delivery are also mentioned. Identified ethical issues are health related, liberty and sustainable development (instead of environmental). A small-scale EIA was performed by Ineke Malsch. On this case study the freely available EIA tool (http://enaloscloud.novamechanics.com/riskgone/thresholdanalysis/) will be applied and its functionality presented. Attendees will be able to get hands-on experience during this 1.5 h workshop/hackathon.\r
\r
Expectation: Willing to learn new things and active participation in the interactive webinar.\r
\r
The webinar will take place on Thursday November 18th from 15:00 till 16:30 h CET.\r
\r
Feel free to join and please do not forget to register here: https://ukri.zoom.us/meeting/register/tJUvf-qqpzItHNQUvsU3L3FAd5rEyXk13Ul4 \r
\r
After registering, you will receive a confirmation email containing information about joining the workshop.\r
\r
Please, be aware that some personal information may be collected for statistical purposes, however, in a blinded and anonymous way. The workshop will be partially recorded for educational purposes.\r
\r
You will find updates with more information about NanoCommons and training materials here.\r
\r
Feel free to redistribute this information within your projects and networks. We are happy to have as many participants as possible and to help the community with the FAIRification of their data by offering insight into fully exploring and linking available data resources in a more straightforward way.\r
\r
The NanoCommons team will be happy to answer your questions and to guide you in your requests!""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2021-11-18 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "RiskGone" ],
                [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NanoCommons" ],
                [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NanoSolveIT" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 80 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Martin Himly, PLUS, Chair of EU NanoSafety Cluster WG-A" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2021-11-18 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "concern assessment" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Ethical Impacts Assessment Tool" ;
            schema1:url <http://enaloscloud.novamechanics.com/riskgone/thresholdanalysis/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
