<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/bioinformatics-tools-and-data-retrieval> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/data_0867>,
        <http://edamontology.org/format_1333>,
        <http://edamontology.org/operation_0346>,
        <http://edamontology.org/operation_2403>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0769> ;
    schema1:description """The Job Dispatcher service at the EMBL-EBI provides access to various sequence analysis tools and a database retrieval system (Dbfetch).

This virtual course is split into two sessions running over two days. 

During the first session, you will learn how to access and use our sequence analysis tools (Eg: NCBI BLAST+, Clustal Omega, EMBOSS); and retrieve various database entries at the EMBL-EBI using Dbfetch. This session will also explain how to use sequence alignment and search effectively to find relevant results.

The second session focuses on alternative ways to access these services beyond the website. This session will introduce the Job Dispatcher RESTful API, show how to access our tools programmatically, and integrate them into workflows""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-11-09 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 60 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2022-11-08 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics tools",
        "Dbfetch",
        "Job dispatcher" ;
    schema1:name "Bioinformatics tools and data retrieval" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/bioinformatics-tools-and-data-retrieval> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/bioinformatics-tools-for-analyzing-clinical-metaproteomics-samples-of-the-human-gut-gcb-2021> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Robert Hayer, Kay Schallert, Dirk Benndorf (BiGi / MetaProtServ), Thilo Muth (Associated Partner), Stephan Fuchs (Associated Partner)\r
\r
Date:\r
09.09.2021\r
\r
Location:\r
GCB 2021 - Online\r
\r
Contents:\r
Metaproteomics analyzes the entirety of proteins from whole microbial communities such as complex microbiomes from medical and technical applications, e.g., in fecal diagnostics and the operation of biogas plants or wastewater treatment plants. A precondition for successful metaproteomics studies is, in addition to experimental knowledge, comprehensive knowledge about the bioinformatics data evaluation (Heyer et al., 2017). This workshop aims to train people by a hands-on workshop in the required bioinformatics tools and skills required for the complete workflow of metaproteomics data analysis. It starts with identifying peptides and inferring proteins from mass spectrometry data using the MetaProteomeAnalyzer (Muth et al., 2015, Muth et al., 2018, Heyer et al., 2019) and the taxonomic and functional annotation using Prophane (Schiebenhoefer et al. 2020). Subsequently, we will illuminate the biostatistical data analysis and data visualization. As a use case, we selected fecal samples from patients with inflammatory bowel disease.\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
In this workshop, we want to bring together bioinformaticians and researchers working in meta-omics and microbiome-focused disciplines.\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
Registration on GCB 2021.\r
\r
Keywords:\r
Microbiome, Metaproteomics, MetaProteomeAnalyzer, Prophane\r
\r
Tools:\r
MetaProteomeAnalyzer, Prophane""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-09-09 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:name "Bioinformatics tools for analyzing clinical metaproteomics samples of the human gut - GCB 2021" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-09-09 14:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/1270-bioinformatics-tools-for-analyzing-clinical-metaproteomics-samples-of-the-human-gut-gcb-2021> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/bioinformatics-tools-to-predict-protein-properties-from-sequence> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "In this workshop, we address how to predict features of proteins from their sequence only, using Python scripts to access APIs of web services available online. We will introduce single-sequence based predictions of biophysical characteristics of proteins (e.g. DynaMine for backbone dynamics), methods that use multiple sequence alignments (e.g. I-TASSER), and methods that determine the difference between a protein and its mutant form (e.g. DEOGEN2). We will also show how the results of these predictions can be combined and analysed using a protein case study." ;
    schema1:endDate "2019-09-10 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Brussels" ;
                    schema1:postalCode "1050" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "U-Residence Brussels" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "50.824343" ;
            schema1:longitude "4.394176" ] ;
    schema1:name "Bioinformatics tools to predict protein properties from sequence" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Bioinformatics Core" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2019-09-10 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/product/115> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/bioinformatics-tools-to-predict-protein-properties-from-sequence-b96402e2-4dce-49fd-bfc1-c8cd31836bdb> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "There are now well over 130 million protein sequences available from Uniprot, and for only a small fraction of these (about 2%) structural information is available from the Protein Data Bank. Uniprot provides a mix of manual and automatic functional and structural annotations for these sequences, but when working on a particular research question you might want to obtain more detailed insights into the possible behavior of the protein(s) you are interested in." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
