<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/bioinformatics-workflow-management> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0769> ;
    schema1:alternateName "A 3-day virtual workshop with tutorials lead by the developers of CWL, Snakemake, Nextflow, nf-core, openEBench, workflUX and WESkit" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "bioinformaticians" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "post-docs" ] ;
    schema1:description """Reproducibility is a cornerstone of good scientific practice. Thanks to funding provided by ELIXIR Training Platform Task 2, we have brought together the biggest names in bioinformatics workflow management for a 3 day practical workshop. We have presentations from the development teams of CWL, Snakemake, Nextflow, nf-core and more. If you want to learn more about the workflow management concepts that will be addressed in this workshop, check out these articles:\r
 - [Workflow systems turn raw data into scientific knowledge](https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-019-02619-z)\r
 - [Methods Included: Standardizing Computational Reuse and Portability with the Common Workflow Language](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.07028)\r
 - [Sustainable data analysis with Snakemake](https://f1000research.com/articles/10-33/v1)\r
 - [Nextflow enables reproducible computational workflows](https://doi.org/10.1038/nbt.3820)\r
 - [The nf-core framework for community-curated bioinformatics pipelines](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41587-020-0439-x)\r
 - [Lessons Learned: Recommendations for Establishing Critical Periodic Scientific Benchmarking](https://doi.org/10.1101/181677)\r
\r
## Details\r
\r
**Organizers**: \r
- [Naveed Ishaque, *Berlin Institute of Health at Charité University Hospital*](naveed.ishaque@charite.de)\r
- Sina Barysch, *EMBL*\r
- Franziska Mueller, *Berlin Institute of Health at Charité University Hospital*\r
- Daniel Wibberg, *CeBiTec, Univerity of Bielefeld*\r
\r
**Target audience:** PhD/postdoc-level bioinformaticians familiar with omics analysis and scripting in python/R. You should already be familiar with some data analysis workflows and ready to take your first steps in trying out a modern workflow management tool.\r
\r
**Places:** 30 attendees - apply soon to avoid disappointment!\r
\r
**Location: virtual via Zoom**. The practical sessions will require use of your own laptop, HPC etc. Please note that you will be expected to set up software environments for the practical sessions before hand. Details on how to do this will be provided in due course.\r
\r
**Dates:** Monday 29th November – Wednesday 1st December.\r
\r
**Contact: for more information email Naveed Ishaque, [naveed.ishaque@bih-charite.de](naveed.ishaque@bih-charite.de)**\r
\r
## Registration ##\r
\r
Please register via [surveymonkey](https://www.surveymonkey.de/r/GSSG3YY)\r
\r
![qr](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/92855176/138662567-2dc6d014-32dd-409f-b760-ad5ad0486ced.png)\r
\r
