    schema1:coursePrerequisites """ You should be able to navigate the file tree in a terminal session and edit text files in the terminal. This Linux shell crash course (video: https://youtu.be/56p6xX0aToI) contains the essentials\r
 We assume you already use some programming or scripting language but we do not require knowing a particular programming language.  We try to keep this course programming language-independent.\r
Read how to attend a livestream course (https://coderefinery.github.io/manuals/how-to-attend-stream/) \r
""" ;
    schema1:description """Do you write code for your research? Do you manage data? Most importantly, are you doing it properly? Has it ever happened to you to have to rewrite or simply copy-paste codes from one folder to another, possibly losing things during the process? Have you ever opened an old code you wrote and had no clue what it does? Maybe you feel you waste a lot of time doing a lot of manual work to process your data and would like to automate it? Or maybe you also are not doing any of this yet, but would like to avoid these issues from happening in the future and do things properly from the beginning?\r
\r
If you answered yes to any of the previous questions, then you are strongly encouraged to participate in the CodeRefinery workshop in Uppsala on May 9-11, 2023.\r
\r
Topics covered in the workshop include:\r
\r
* Using Git to maintain your codes and collaborate with others\r
* Principles of reproducible research and FAIR\r
* Good coding practices in general (writing documentation, testing, how to choose an appropriate license)\r
\r
The Workshop will not teach you to code, but all the "good practice skills" that are needed to write good maintainable software and achieve reproducible research.\r
\r
The workshop will alternate between type along tutorials and hands-on exercises. You may register for parts of the workshop.""" ;
