<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-carpentry-workshop-2021> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Renato Alves (HD-HuB) \r
\r
Date:\r
26/01/2021 - 28/01/2021\r
\r
Location:\r
Online\r
\r
Contents:\r
The missing step between data collection and research progress is a lack of training for researchers in crucial skills for effectively managing and analysing large amounts of data. This hands on workshop teaches researchers the fundamental data skills they need to conduct their work, and provides high-quality, domain-specific training covering the full lifecycle of data-driven research. The domain-specific approach allows participants to tailor the data, content, and tools to reflect the specific data and analysis needs of different areas:\r
\r
- Data organisation and cleaning\r
- Introduction to R\r
- Data analysis and visualisation in R\r
- Interacting with databases from R\r
- Data analysis with applications to genomics\r
\r
 Learning goals:\r
\r
You will learn...\r
- how to organise data and some practices for more effective data handling\r
- to effectively clean and format data and automatically track any changes\r
- to use R to handle, filter, and summarise tabular data\r
- to use the split-apply-combine concept for data analysis\r
- to visualise the results of these analyses in R using the ggplot2 library\r
- to apply all of the concepts in an exploratory analysis of a typical biological dataset\r
\r
Prerequisities:\r
The course is aimed at graduate students and other researchers in the life sciences who would like to learn good practices in data management and analysis. The course is ideally suited to students without prior experience in computational research, but some familiarity with working with tabular data on a computer is welcome.\r
\r
Keywords:\r
Data Carpentry, R\r
\r
Tools:\r
R""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2021-01-28 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2021-01-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Data Carpentry Workshop 2021 " ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Germany" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/1014-data-carpentry-workshop-2021> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-carpentry-workshop-2023> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Data Carpentry workshops are for any researcher who has data they want to analyse, with no prior computational experience required." ;
    schema1:contact "training@earlham.ac.uk" ;
    schema1:description """This hands-on workshop teaches fundamental data skills needed for anyone who deals with data and datasets. You will learn basic concepts, skills, and tools for working more effectively with data. The aim is to get researchers doing more in less time, and with less pain!\r
\r
We will cover: \r
\r
data organisation in spreadsheets\r
data cleaning\r
SQL (Structured Query Language)\r
the command line\r
R for data analysis\r
visualisation using biological examples\r
By the end of the workshop, learners should be able to more effectively manage and analyse data and apply the tools and approaches directly to their ongoing research.\r
\r
This four-day workshop will be delivered in person at Earlham Institute. \r
\r
Who is this training for?\r
Researchers in the life science and computational science disciplines. We particularly encourage PhD students and postdoctoral scientists to attend, but the course is open to everyone, at any stage of their career. \r
\r
The course is aimed at complete beginners; trainees are expected to have no prior knowledge of the tools or computational experience before attending. However, we will cover a lot of material and the training is quite fast-paced, so be prepared to fully immerse yourself in the world of data management and analysis!""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2023-03-16 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "Earlham Institute" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Norwich" ;
                    schema1:addressRegion "Norfolk" ;
                    schema1:postalCode "NR4 7UZ" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Earlham Institute" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "52.628569" ;
            schema1:longitude "1.292305" ] ;
    schema1:name "Data Carpentry Workshop 2023" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Earlham Institute" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2023-03-13 10:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.earlham.ac.uk/events/data-carpentry-workshop-2023> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-carpentry-workshop-5b2d17cb-f74b-4613-be3a-9cf0e744f1ab> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Data Carpentry workshops are for any researcher who has data they want to analyse, and no prior computational experience is required. This hands-on workshop teaches basic concepts, skills, and tools for working more effectively with data. We will cover data organisation in spreadsheets, data cleaning, SQL, the command line, and R for data analysis and visualisation using examples from biology. Participants should bring their laptops and plan to actively participate. By the end of the workshop, learners should be able to more effectively manage and analyse data and apply the tools and approaches directly to their ongoing research.\r
\r
Data Carpentry's aim is to teach researchers basic concepts, skills, and tools for working with data so that they can get more done in less time, and with less pain.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
