<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-management-human-data-and-infectious-diseases-opportunities-for-engagement> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """This is the first joint webinar between ELIXIR, Asia Pacific Bioinformatics Interaction &amp; Networking Society (APbians) and RSG India. The aim is to provide an overview of ELIXIR data management and human data activities, as well as opportunities for engagement for Indian scientists. The webinar is openly accessible and we encourage anyone interested in the programme to register!\r
\r
This event will take place from 11:00 –13:30 (CEST) / 14:30 –17:00 (IST).\r
\r
The full programme is available on Google Drive and is as follows:\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			Time\r
			Presentation\r
		\r
		\r
			11:00 / 14:30\r
			Andrew Smith, ELIXIR Head of External Relations\r
				ELIXIR Europe: overview and opportunities \r
		\r
		\r
			11:20 / 14:50\r
			\r
				Frederik Coppens, ELIXIR Belgium\r
					\r
					Research data management (RDM) in ELIXIR and insight into the RDM Toolkit\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			11:40 / 15:10\r
			\r
				Shandar Ahmad, Professor, Asia Pacific Bioinformatics Interaction &amp; Networking Society (APbians)\r
\r
				Data accessibility,  integration and management challenges for a Bioinformatics research lab in India\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			12:00 / 15:30\r
			Break\r
		\r
		\r
			12:10 / 15:40\r
			Anamika Chatterjee, ELIXIR Scientific Officer\r
				\r
				Human genomic data-sharing infrastructure: challenges and opportunities\r
		\r
		\r
			12:30 / 16:00\r
			Katharina Lauer, ELIXIR Senior Scientific Coordinator for Infectious Diseases\r
				\r
				ELIXIR COVID-19 and infectious diseases activities\r
		\r
		\r
			12:50 / 16:20\r
			Arfa Jabin, President of RSG India\r
				\r
				Data accessibility and management challenges for bioinformatics students in India\r
		\r
		\r
			13:00 / 16:30\r
			Open Discussion\r
		\r
		\r
			13:30 / 17:00\r
			Close\r
		\r
	\r
""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2022-08-17 11:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Data management, human data and infectious diseases: Opportunities for engagement" ;
    schema1:startDate "2022-08-17 11:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/data-management-human-data-and-infectious-diseases-opportunities-engagement> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-management-in-a-bioimage-informatics-data-flow> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/data_4040>,
        <http://edamontology.org/operation_4042>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3383> ;
    schema1:description "Making image data and their analysis output public aims at supporting transparency, auditability and reproducibility of a research project. However crucial contextual information is often lost along the way between image acquisition and publication resulting in a dramatic loss of value of the published data. In this webinar, we will follow the flow of bioimage data from acquisition to analysis focusing on data management practices in an open science context. We will review points to consider and provide tips and examples for managing bioimage data and their metadata through a project’s life." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-06-04 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-06-04 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Data management in a bioimage informatics data flow" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/data-management-bioimage-informatics-data-flow> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-management-in-medicinal-chemistry> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """None.\r
\r
However, you should specify your experience in the listed fields in the registration form. Participants are encouraged to get familiar with the Galaxy platform and available training materials. \r
\r
""" ;
    schema1:description """The development of medicinal chemistry is advancing very rapidly. Big pharmaceutical companies, research institutes and universities are working on ground-breaking solutions to help patients combat all kinds of diseases. During that development process, tons of data are generated – not only from the lab environment but also from clinical trials. Since discovering more potent, safer and cheaper drugs is the ultimate goal of all research bodies, we should all focus on making the data we gather FAIR: Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable to push the boundaries of drug development even further.\r
With the currently available methods such as artificial intelligence, machine learning, many toolkits, software and access to various databases, managing big data is now inherently linked to medicinal chemistry and helps to make this area as efficient as it can be.\r
In this training session, we will explore some concepts related to FAIR data principles and medicinal chemistry, explore the available chemical and pharmacological databases, and perform some basic data analyses using the Galaxy interface.\r
Participants are encouraged to familiarise themselves with the Galaxy platform and available training materials.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
