<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-visualization-in-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """People with all expertise-levels in R and plotting are encouraged to apply. 
The course materials can be organized to match your level and the TAs would be happy to help you to attain your needs.

This course is designed to help researchers in visualizing their data through various techniques using R and Quarto. Participants will learn how to create publication-ready visualisations with R. Additionally, the course also includes introductions to Quarto and dynamic plotting using tools like plotly, OJS, etc. The last day of the course includes interactive plotting that can be hosted on a web server, enabling researchers to share and explore visualizations of their data.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-05-15 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Sweden" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Lund" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress """Medicon Village
Scheelevägen 2
Lund, Sweden""" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "55.70293" ;
                    schema1:longitude "13.192945" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-05-13 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:name "Advanced Data visualization in R" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "NBIS" ;
            schema1:url <http://nbis.se> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://nbisweden.github.io/workshop-data-visualization-r/2505/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/data-visualization-using-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Markus Stepath, Karin Schork, Nils Hoffmann (BioInfra.Prot / LIFS)\r
\r
Date:\r
Monday, 7th Dec 2020\r
\r
Location:\r
Online training\r
\r
Contents:\r
In this course you will learn how to use the ggplot2 package in R to create informative and beautiful figures to communicate your omics data and analysis results.\r
\r
We will cover the following topics:\r
- Usage of the tidyverse for data preprocessing\r
- Usage of the ggplot2 R package\r
- Presentation of different types of graphics and when to use them\r
- Customization of graphics\r
\r
Please be aware that basic knowledge of R is taught in our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” on Monday, 2nd Nov 2020 (http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um).\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
- Using the ggplot2 R package to create graphics for omics data\r
- Decide which type of graph is appropriate for the given data\r
- Ability to costumize the graphics using ggplot2\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
- Basic understanding of high-dimensional data sets from quantitative proteomics or other life sciences;\r
- Basic knowledge of R (e.g. data import, basic plots. This can for example be gained from our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” (Monday, 2nd Nov 2020, http://goo.gl/forms/mpKHnbT1Um).\r
- Computer with stable internet connection, headset and camera\r
\r
Keywords:\r
R; tidyverse; ggplot2; high-throughput data; omics; proteomics; data analysis, graphics, data preprocessing\r
\r
Tools: \r
Download and more information on R here:\r
https://cran.r-project.org/\r
\r
We recommend using an editor such as RStudio, see\r
www.rstudio.com""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2020-12-07 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "de.NBI" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2020-12-07 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Data visualization using R" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Germany" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/training/965-data-visualization-using-r> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/databases-and-computational-tools-on-phase-separation> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Training School" ;
    schema1:description """After four successful editions, the PhasAGE consortium is pleased to announce the fifth Training School on “Databases and computational tools in phase separation”.\r
\r
The event will take place at a PhasAGE member institution, in Brussels (Belgium) and the lead organizer of the Training School is Prof. Peter Tompa. \r
\r
Target competences in brief: For members of the PhasAGE consortium and the selected external participants, the target of this Training School is on applying computational methodologies to study liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) and aggregation using in silico tools, databases and other resources.\r
\r
More detailed overview: Participants will receive multidisciplinary training to gain deeper insights and practical experience in applying databases, in silico resources and computational tools to query relevant biological data and analyze it in context. Using integrative methodologies for studying both structured and intrinsically disordered proteins, trainees will investigate the LLPS and aggregation propensities of wild-type and mutant proteins in physiology and disease. The program integrates practical sessions with lectures by leading experts in the field from within and beyond the organizing consortiums, while an additional dedicated slot is assigned to career development in and outside academia. The schedule allows for plenty of networking and socializing opportunities for trainees in coffee breaks, at lunch and during the social dinner.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-02-28 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Ixelles" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "1050" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Boulevard de la Plaine,  Boulevard de la Plaine" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "50.820411" ;
                    schema1:longitude "4.393203" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VUB and VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2024-02-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "bioinformatics",
        "biological processes",
        "computational biology",
        "databases",
        "disordered proteins",
        "liquid-liquid phase separation",
        "protein aggregation",
        "protein condensates",
        "protein function",
        "protein interactions",
        "protein structure",
        "sequence-based prediction" ;
    schema1:name "Databases and computational tools on phase separation" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VUB" ] ;
    schema1:url <https://phasage.eu/training-school-5/> .

