<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/demonstration-on-data-curation-and-creation-of-pre-reasoned-datasets-in-the-openrisknet-framework> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0219>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_2840> ;
    schema1:description """In the process of utilising the analysis tools available in the OpenRiskNet e-infrastructure, data needs to be accessed from different sources and various formats.\r
\r
In this webinar we will use examples to introduce attendees to the OpenRiskNet data handling and curation process. This will include methods for data access, upload, and extraction for further downstream analysis.\r
\r
Some specific examples we will be exploring include:\r
\r
* A workflow using the text mining capability for data extraction from a public database;\r
\r
* Demonstration of extraction of data from a public database for a specific endpoint (e.g. drug induced liver injury) and combine with other data (e.g. omics data) to get a better grouping for target prediction.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-03-18 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-03-18 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Demonstration on data curation and creation of pre-reasoned datasets in the OpenRiskNet framework" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ;
            schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/events/58/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/demonstration-on-openrisknet-approach-on-modelling-for-prediction-or-read-across-modelrx-case-study> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """ModelRX support similarity identification in other case studies by providing tools for calculating theoretical descriptors of substances. Also, aims to fill gaps in incomplete datasets and use in silico predictive modelling approaches (read across, QSAR) to support final risk assessment.\r
The ModelRX case study contributes provides computational methods to support  suitability assessment of existing data and identification of analogues and also provides predictive modelling functionalities, which are essential in the field of final risk assessment.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-06-11 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-06-11 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Demonstration on OpenRiskNet approach on modelling for prediction or read across (ModelRX case study)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ;
            schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/events/67/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/demystifying-elixir-everything-you-ever-wanted-to-know-and-more> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """This interactive event will help participants understand ELIXIR's structure and operations. It is particularly suitable for people who have been part of ELIXIR for less than two years, but is open to all.  Those involved in onboarding new people to Nodes might also find this webinar useful.\r
\r
By the end of the session, attendees should have a clearer picture of how they fit into ELIXIR and how best to apply their knowledge and skills to maximise their impact. They should leave with answers to their questions, and understand where to find further information and how to actively engage with ELIXIR.\r
\r
Speakers\r
\r
 \r
	\r
		\r
			Elaine Harrison\r
				ELIXIR Communications Manager\r
		\r
	\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			Irena Maus\r
				Deputy Head of Node, Node Coordinator at ELIXIR-DE""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2024-07-11 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Demystifying ELIXIR: Everything you ever wanted to know and more" ;
    schema1:startDate "2024-07-11 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/demystifying-elixir-everything-you-ever-wanted-know-and-more> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/deploying-applications-to-an-openrisknet-virtual-environment> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """This webinar will illustrate how you can deploy containerised services to an OpenRiskNet Virtual Environment. Starting from a simple case where you have an existing container image that contains a service, such as one from Docker Hub, we will walk you through the process of deploying that service and configuring it so that it plays nicely with the Kubernetes environment and can be accessed by users and/or other services. Different approaches to deployment will be discussed, as well as some more complex examples where multiple container images are involved.\r
\r
By the end of the webinar it is expected that attendees with have the basic knowledge to get started on deploying their own containerised services.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-06-24 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-06-24 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Deploying Applications to an OpenRiskNet Virtual Environment" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ;
            schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/events/66/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/deploying-nextflow-pipelines-in-the-cloud-a-practical-introduction> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0769> ;
    schema1:description """Modern biological research generates vast amounts of data that require complex computational workflows to process efficiently and reproducibly. As data volumes continue to grow, so does the demand for computing infrastructure to process them. Deploying these workflows in the cloud is becoming increasingly common. This webinar will introduce the fundamental concepts of cloud deployment for Nextflow pipelines and demonstrate the steps required for setting up a fully functional pipeline in the cloud.

We will begin by describing the purpose of Workflow Management Systems (such as Nextflow) and why they have become indispensable in modern computational biology. The session will cover both the benefits and potential limitations of cloud-based workflow execution, helping you understand when and why to leverage cloud resources for your computational workloads.

We will use the "Biome-specific Shallow-shotgun Inference of Functional Traits through Read-mapping" ([BioSIFTR](https://github.com/ebi-metagenomics/biosiftr)) pipeline as our case study. This pipeline, developed by the Microbiome Informatics team at EMBL-EBI as part of the [Finding Pheno project](https://findingpheno.eu/), serves as an excellent example of a complex computational workflow on a public cloud platform using Nextflow.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-07-30 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-07-30 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "MGnify",
        "nextflow",
        "pipeline development",
        "workflow management" ;
    schema1:name "Deploying Nextflow pipelines in the cloud: a practical introduction" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/deploying-nextflow-pipelines-cloud-practical-introduction> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/deploying-web-services-for-covid-19-research-in-the-de-nbi-cloud> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Martin Braun, Sven Twardziok (HiDiH)\r
\r
Date:\r
25.06.2020, 14:00 to 16:00\r
\r
Location:\r
Online lecture\r
\r
Contents/Learning goals:\r
The de.NBI cloud provides priority access to their services for projects involved in COVID-19 research. With this, the de.NBI cloud supports hosting of COVID-19 related web services. Web services can improve collaboration of spatially distinct researchers; e.g. by providing a shared workspace for data analysis or as an application user interface to access data and tools. In our online lecture we will cover the whole process from applying a project in the de.NBI cloud to the deployment of a web service in the de.NBI cloud infrastructure. Thereby, we will focus on scalable deployment via docker swarm. As an example we will create and deploy a small R-Shiny application.\r
\r
The lecture is intended for scientists from undergraduate to postdoc level. It will be a general overview about the involved steps with examples and links to detailed information.\r
\r
Keywords:\r
Docker, Traefik, R-Shiny, OpenStack, SSH\r
""" ;
