<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-cloud-systems-for-multi-omics-analysis> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """\r
\r
While genomics and transcriptomics are well established fields, multi-omics is a developing area with the potential to unite assays and analyses across the many points of molecular biology’s Central Dogma. Applying these new, large scale multi-omics approaches is critical to understanding novel biological mechanisms and developing new knowledge about complex and intricate biological systems. Harnessing multi-omics data requires significant compute power to enable analysis at scale, and cloud systems can offer that power. \r
\r
Annalisa Pawloski and Hatem Nawar from Google Cloud will be disscussing how Cloud systems can be used for multi-omics analysis. They will lead a detailed exploration of every touch point in analysis from:\r
\r
\r
	automating data from experimental Windows instruments into the Cloud,\r
	automating analysis functions within the Cloud,\r
	using Cloud Functions to report back and interact with scientists,\r
	building custom analysis,\r
	importing public data sets.\r
\r
\r
The webinar is free of charge.\r
\r
Programme:\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			Welcome to the event\r
			\r
				Katharina Lauer (ELIXIR)\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			How cloud systems can be used for multi-omics analysis (45min)\r
			\r
				Annalisa Pawlosky and Hatem Nawar (Google Cloud) \r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			Panel discussion &amp; Q&amp;A (30-45min)\r
			\r
				Dirk Van Hyfte (BioStrand)\r
\r
				Claire O’Donovan (EMBL-EBI)\r
\r
				Annalisa Pawlosky (Google Cloud)\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-05-19 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum:  Cloud systems for multi-omics analysis" ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-05-19 16:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-cloud-systems-multi-omics-analysis> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-commercialisation-of-research-in-bioinformatics> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """\r
\r
The ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum webinar on commercialisation of research in bioinformatics will discuss the interface between academic research, technology transfer, business and public-private partnerships. With a particular focus on the data-driven life science sector, we will explore the motivations behind commercialisation, and discuss opportunities and lessons learnt.\r
\r
Alessandro Riccombeni (AWS) and Gareth Conduit (Intellegens) will not only guide us through the practicalities and technical aspects of commercialising your research but also immerse us into exploring the transition on a more personal level. \r
\r
This event will be video recorded.\r
\r
 """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-10-29 17:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum: Commercialisation of Research in Bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:startDate "2020-10-29 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/EBIF-commercialisation-research-bioinformatics> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-enabling-ecosystems-for-machine-learning-in-the-life-sciences> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum (EBIF) is a one-day event and aims to bring together the community of bioinformaticians to discuss visionary ideas, bottlenecks and solutions to some of the major challenges in the data-driven life science sector.\r
\r
This year’s EBIF focus is on making Machine Learning robust and reproducible for the Life Sciences. The programme includes a mixture of presentations from industry and academia that will lead to panel discussions on the following themes:\r
\r
\r
	Interoperable data and workflows to enable reproducible Machine Learning in the life sciences and the role of Open Science in the value chain;\r
	Challenges and solutions in Machine Learning for the life sciences - Federated Learning and Synthetic Data;\r
	Innovation, collaboration and security in the Machine Learning Ecosystem.\r
\r
\r
\r
	Watch the event live\r
\r
	The live streaming has now ended.\r
\r
\r
\r
Format: hybrid event\r
\r
We encourage physical attendance for participating in roundtable discussions and networking activities, and for engaging with the speakers.\r
	Virtual participants will only access live stream and ask questions via SliDo.\r
\r
Target audience\r
\r
Bioinformaticians and technical specialists with an interest in the industry perspective of the topic.\r
\r
Aims\r
\r
\r
	Give companies the chance to present technological advances in the field of Machine Learning in life sciences;\r
	Provide a forum for knowledge exchange and collaboration in the pre-competitive space;\r
	Create networking opportunities with bioinformatics opinion leaders, academic experts in ELIXIR and other industry members.\r
\r
\r
Programme\r
\r
\r
	Two Sessions of presentations from academia and industry\r
	Flash talk session and round table discussions (only for physical attendees)\r
	Panel discussions\r
	Keynote speech\r
""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2022-10-11 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "London" ;
                    schema1:postalCode "NW1 2BE" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Wellcome Collection" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "51.52555" ;
            schema1:longitude "-0.134873" ] ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum:  Enabling Ecosystems for Machine Learning in the Life Sciences" ;
    schema1:startDate "2022-10-11 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-enabling-ecosystems-machine-learning-life-sciences> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-exploiting-semantics-web-technology-for-a-service-cross-talk-between-companies> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """\r
\r
In this Webinar, we will be taking an industry case example as a seeding scenario in a question of connecting different bioinformatics resources for an analytical workflow pipeline. The scope of discussion may include (but is not limited to) metadata and standards to allow cross-database data integration, machine-assisted data management by semantic web framework, and schematic mark-up for data and tools discoverability. For additional details of the ELIXIR Interoperability Service Framework, please see https://elixir-europe.org/platforms/interoperability.\r
\r
 """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-05-05 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum: Exploiting semantics web technology for a service cross-talk between companies" ;
    schema1:startDate "2020-05-05 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-exploiting-semantics-web-technology-service-cross-talk> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-large-scale-cloud-computing-in-the-life-sciences> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """\r
\r
ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum: Large scale cloud computing in the life sciences\r
\r
This is our last, but not least, webinar as part of the ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum Series.\r
\r
Cloud computing has already reshaped the way we work and collaborate. Momentum is building to take cloud computing in the life sciences to the next level through the ELIXIR Compute Platform and in projects such as the European Open Science Cloud and Global Alliance for Genomics and Health. Cloud computing opens the door to new partnerships, makes us technologically agile, more secure and fosters innovation.\r
\r
In this webinar, we will show some real-life use cases, discuss successes and bottlenecks, and explore what the future holds for cloud computing in the life sciences. Our presenters have significant experience in the field, ranging from academic, biomedical, industry including both suppliers and service providers.\r
\r
 \r
\r
This event will be recorded and made available to the public.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-06-23 17:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum: Large scale cloud computing in the life sciences" ;
    schema1:startDate "2020-06-23 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-large-scale-cloud-computing-life-sciences> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-open-source-software-in-the-life-sciences> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """\r
\r
PROGRAMME:\r
\r
1. Introduction to the ELIXIR tools platformand development of software FAIR metrics\r
\r
2. How We Are Supporting Essential Open Source Scientific Software: CZI’s EOSS Program\r
\r
The majority of open source software for science is undervalued and lacks funding for maintenance, growth, development, and community engagement—especially after the initial phase when it’s linked to original research. At the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative we aim to support the people that work on this software to ensure long-term utility and value. The Essential Open Source Software for Science program supports domain-specific and foundational tools essential to conducting biomedical research. We will cover the current and future status of the program and our plans for community building and support beyond funding. \r
\r
 """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-05-28 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum: Open source Software in the Life Sciences" ;
    schema1:startDate "2020-05-28 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-open-source-software-life-sciences> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/elixir-bioinformatics-industry-forum-trusted-research-environments-for-sharing-data-in-life-sciences> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The annual ELIXIR Bioinformatics Industry Forum is a one-day event that brings together bioinformaticians and technical specialists to explore solutions to major challenges in the data-driven life science sector. \r
\r
This year’s Forum theme is "Trusted research environments for sensitive data in the life sciences". The programme features a variety of presentations from industry and academia, offering the participants a chance to learn more about relevant initiatives and research breakthroughs in the area of TREs. The forum aims to stimulate discussions among solution providers and enable potential collaborations.\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			08:30\r
			\r
				Registrations and welcome coffee\r
				First floor-south wing\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			09:30 - 9:40\r
			\r
				Welcome to the event by ELIXIR (Despoina Sousoni) &amp; EMBL-EBI (Matthew Hall)\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			9:40- 11:00\r
			\r
				Session 1: How partnerships can help us tackle big challenges and seize big opportunities in Trusted Research Environments for sensitive and restricted data\r
\r
				The session will feature presentations from different initiatives and organisations that are working on developing and using Trusted Research Environments, and share their experiences in building collaborative efforts to achieve big goals and challenging tasks in this domain.\r
				Part one: ELIXIR Introduction by Harald Wagener, Juan Arenas, Peter Maccallum\r
				\r
				10min presentations, 10min common Q&amp;A\r
				\r
				Part two Speakers:\r
				\r
					BBMRI-ERIC (Radovan Tomašík): Federated Platform - Data querying and analysis\r
					Norwegian Trusted Research Environment (NORTRE) (Gard Thomassen): How a multi-tenant TRE infrastructure serves research projects, biobanks, clinics and SMEs at the same time\r
					HDR UK (Emily Jefferson): Examples of partnership models to tackle the big challenges and seize big opportunities in TREs: data discovery, federated analytics, reference TRE implementations and standards\r
				\r
				\r
				10min presentations, 10min common Q&amp;A\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			11:00 - 11:30\r
			\r
				Coffee break\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			11:30 - 13:00\r
			\r
				Session 2: Technical solutions for enabling data access and analysis in Trusted Research Environments for life sciences\r
\r
				The presentations will showcase the benefits of the service and identify the current gaps/challenges for advancing the application. This aims to trigger discussions among solution providers and foster potential collaborations.\r
\r
				10min each presentation, 10min common Q&amp;A\r
\r
				Part one\r
				\r
					Intel (Markus Leberecht): Confidential Computing in Bioinformatics and Healthcare\r
					Amazon (James Grant): Trusted Research: A perspective\r
					Zetta Genomics (Marta Bleda): Technical solutions for enabling data access and analysis in Trusted Research Environments for life sciences\r
				\r
Part two\r
\r
					ITTM (Andreas Kremer): Data Services for Real World Data\r
					BC platforms (Paul Smith &amp; Philip Quinlan): TRE for sharing Data in Life Sciences\r
					Unison (Rory Popert): Solving the cross-Biobank data heterogeneity problem, a novel machine learning empowered approach\r
					Daset Labs (Guy Hill): Challenges of bringing datasets together to form valuable resources\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			13:00 - 14:00\r
			\r
				Lunch break\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			14:00 - 14:45\r
			\r
				Panel discussion on Interoperability, privacy, and sustainability in Trusted Research Environments for life sciences: challenges and opportunities for innovation\r
\r
				The panel discussion will explore how to address the issues of interoperability and reusability of solutions, privacy and ethics, commercial use of data, management and governance challenges, and rewarding systems in Trusted Research Environments for life sciences.\r
\r
				Panellists:\r
\r
				\r
					Oxford University (Andrzej Rys)\r
					The Hyve (Wouter Franke)\r
					IQVIA (Ricarda Gaentzsch )\r
					Genomics England (Kate Witkowska​​​​)\r
					Centre for Genomic Regulation (Jordi Rambla​​​​)\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			14:40 - 16:55\r
			\r
				Round-table discussions\r
\r
				3-min Flash talk introductory presentations of the topics\r
\r
				Two rounds of 40 min discussions\r
\r
				\r
					Security of data-in-use in federated environments (Pavel Nikonorov from GenxT &amp;  Michael Bursell from Confidential Computing Consortium)\r
					Serverless Cloud Data Processing: Private, FAIR, and Federated (Jonas De Almeida from NIH &amp; Ana T Freitas from IST/BioData.pt)\r
					FAIR Data Management in Federated TREs (Sven Olaf Twardziok from Bih Charite &amp; Miikka Kallberg from CSC, both from ELIXIR Compute Platform)\r
					User Perspectives for International Standards for Data Processing (Alex Kanitz from SIB and ELIXIR Compute Platform)\r
					A framework for running analyses across multiple TREs (Tom Giles &amp; &amp; Philip Quinlan from University of Nottingham)\r
					Research Environments Can Be Built, But Trust Must Be Earned - The Patient Perspective (Richard Stephens from useMYdata and Eric Vermeulen from BBMRI-ERIC Stakeholder Forum)\r
					Privacy-first data sharing and collaboration (Viktoria Paroczi from Xtendr)\r
				\r
\r
				3min wrap up presentations of the discussions\r
			\r
		\r
		\r
			16:55-17:00\r
			Wrap-up session\r
		\r
		\r
			1h Networking drinks\r
		\r
	\r
\r
\r
Theme and objectives of the event\r
\r
\r
	Theme summary: Data-centric research relies on accessing and combining large-scale, sensitive data sets for analysis. These data sets require Trusted Research Environments (TREs) that offer data access and analysis with robust data management, governance and technology. However, the current landscape of TREs for sensitive or restricted data is very fragmented and lacks a common reference architecture for connectivity and interoperability.\r
\r
	To overcome this fragmentation and promote collaboration among different stakeholders, we need a framework for large-scale partnerships on sensitive and restricted data through a network of TREs. ELIXIR is hosting a Bioinformatics Industry Forum on this topic with the aim of fostering a collaborative approach to address these challenges.\r
\r
	Objectives\r
\r
	\r
		Learn from best practices and experiences of established players\r
		Understand the relevant ecosystem\r
		Discover existing gaps and potential research business opportunities\r
		Gain insights into research and technological breakthroughs\r
		Encourage discussions among solution providers and foster potential collaborations\r
	\r
\r
\r
Target audience\r
\r
\r
	Professionals from:\r
\r
	\r
		Large, medium and small enterprises\r
		Funding bodies\r
		Academia\r
		Nonprofit and public organisations\r
		Patients’ and clinicians’ associations\r
	\r
\r
	With research interests in:\r
\r
	\r
		Health and life sciences\r
		Data management\r
		Innovative services in the above areas\r
	\r
\r
\r
Programme Committee\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
\r
			\r
				"Looking forward to a day of sharing what works, how to build partnerships and what not to do."\r
					- Joanne Hackett, Vice President Genomic &amp; Precision Medicine, IQVIA\r
\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
\r
			\r
				"To continue to impact on health and society, bioinformatics needs to be applied to combined molecular and clinical datasets. How can the ELIXIR approach of partnership and federation be applied in the world of trusted research environments?"\r
					- Peter Maccallum, Chief Technical Officer, ELIXIR Hub\r
\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
\r
			\r
				“This day will facilitate the innovation required to maximise the impact of data: accessing data securely and ensuring solutions across different data spaces are interoperable will be key."\r
					- Serena Scollen, Head of Human Genomics and Translational Data, ELIXIR Hub\r
\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
\r
			\r
				“This event will bring together academic and industrial stakeholders to discuss the current state of affairs regarding the existing (and expected) infrastructures for enabling distributed processing of sensitive data."\r
					- Salvador Capella, Team Lead, Barcelona Supercomputing Center\r
\r
				\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
\r
\r
Travel information\r
\r
\r
	Address: First floor-south wing, 200 Aldersgate, London EC1A 4HD\r
\r
	By underground\r
\r
	St. Paul's - Central line\r
\r
	On exiting the station follow St Martin Le-Grand north towards Aldersgate Street and the Museum of London. When you arrive at the Museum of London roundabout our venue is located on the left. Look for the circular artwork in the window and go through the revolving doors.\r
\r
	Barbican - Circle, Metropolitan and Hammersmith &amp; City lines\r
\r
	On exiting the station turn right on to Aldersgate Street. Follow Aldersgate Street south towards London Wall and the Museum of London. As you arrive at the roundabout for Museum of London our venue is on the right. Cross over the zebra crossing and look for the circular artwork in the window and go through the revolving doors to reception.\r
\r
	By train\r
\r
	Aldersgate Station is located near Moorgate and Farringdon railway stations, and is around a 10 minute walk from both.\r
\r
	By bus\r
\r
	The closest bus stop is at the Museum of London and is served directly by the number 4 and 56. Other services also serve Barbican and St. Paul's and our venue is a short walk from both these locations.\r
\r
	Parking\r
\r
	The closest car park is located next to the venue at 158 – 170 Aldersgate Street. Visit www.ncp.co.uk for more details.\r
\r
	See also the venue travel information.\r
\r
	Accommodation\r
\r
	Participants and speakers are expected to make their own accommodation arrangements. The recommended hotel is Premier Inn London Blackfriars, which is located 13 min walking distance from the venue.\r
\r
\r
Background information\r
\r
\r
	See the Bioinformatics Industry Forum page.\r
\r
	\r
		70\r
			participants per event, on average\r
\r
		60\r
			companies joined our two F2F events\r
\r
		92%\r
			of participants would recommend the event to their colleagues\r
	\r
\r
	\r
		A very useful forum to connect with people from ELIXIR who are deeply embedded in the European space of bioinformatics while learning about latest advances in the field.\r
			- Sebastian Schwarz (Data Platform Strategy Lead, Owkin)\r
\r
		A wonderful event, really excellent talks covering a range of relevant challenges which our community needs to think carefully about. And networking is always invaluable!\r
			- Anonymous\r
\r
		Thank you providing such a brilliant forum for discussion. I have many actions and people to follow up with.\r
			- Anonymous""" ;
