<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/fair-in-a-nutshell> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """On the 16th of February 2023, ELIXIR-Estonia will be holding an online data management course in English: FAIR in a Nutshell. This lecture will be a short overview of the FAIR principles and is part of a bigger data management course package aimed at DMP writers and grant applicants.\r
\r
In recent years, more attention has been put on what researchers do with the data they produce. Especially in Europe (think GDPR), but also everywhere else. The main idea is that when researchers use taxpayers' money, the taxpayers themselves should also have access to the results free of charge. This means that the research should be published in open-access journals, and data should be made publicly available.\r
\r
Good data management helps to make the whole process of making data reusable easier. In general, you should think about how to manage your data at the beginning and during the project and know what you plan to do with it at the end of the project. However, what is “good data management” exactly is up for debate. The FAIR Principles concentrate on making your data findable, accessible, interoperable and reusable, so this is a good start. And let’s be honest, some of these things you probably are already doing.\r
\r
In this course, we will be going over all the FAIR Principles and how they are applied in real life. This way, you will already know what to consider while writing a grant, filling out your data management plan or doing your research.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-02-16 12:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-02-16 10:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "CONVERGE",
        "Data management",
        "ELIXIR-CONVERGE",
        "FAIR" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "FAIR in a Nutshell" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7660008> ] ;
    schema1:name "FAIR in a Nutshell" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <http://elixir.ut.ee> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University of Tartu, Institute of Computer Science" ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* Understands the importance of a good data management\r
* Knows what are the FAIR principles and what they mean\r
* Knows how to implement FAIR principles throughout the research project\r
* Knows where to get more information about the FAIR Principles\r
""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/node/465> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/fair-in-biological-practice-or-how-to-work-with-your-data> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """You don’t need to have prior knowledge of data management or programming skills. You do need to be willing to learn how to apply FAIR principles in your daily research life.\r
\r
We expect you to:\r
- have a bio/medical background\r
- know the basics of scientific communication, publications, citations and their importance for careers\r
- familiarity with spreadsheets (Excel)\r
- familiarity with online bioscience data resources (in general, not any particular resource); for example: searching publications\r
- know your way around files and directories on your own machine\r
""" ;
    schema1:description """The way we disseminate scientific results is changing to include Open\r
Science and Findable Accessible Interoperable Reusable (FAIR)\r
principles. Well-organised data (and code) is not only the best\r
preparation for this, but it can also increase productivity. With the\r
introduction of Narrative CVs, both you and your research can be\r
recognised for these new research outputs.\r
\r
In this hands-on, interactive workshop, we will teach how to:\r
- organize your data to work more efficiently\r
- make your outputs reusable\r
- build reputation and presence in the scientific community\r
\r
Join Carpentries-style Workshop: FAIR in (Biological) Practice.\r
\r
Understanding good data management principles is key to practising it.\r
This hands-on workshop is aimed at non-PI researchers in biomedical\r
sciences (PhD students, postdocs, technicians…) who are interested in\r
Open Science, FAIR principles and how to practically apply them to their\r
own workflows.\r
\r
Across four half-day sessions, you will learn how to plan and use the\r
the correct set of tools to prepare your research outputs for public sharing\r
and reuse.\r
""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-03-31 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "University of Edinburgh" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-03-28 14:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "FAIR in Biological practice or how to work with your data" ;
