<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/galaxy-elixir-webinar-series> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_2258>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3332> ;
    schema1:alternateName "FAIR data and Open Infrastructures to tackle the COVID-19 pandemic" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "bioinformaticians" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "software developers, bioinformaticians" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biologists, Genomicists, Computer Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Computational biologists" ] ;
    schema1:description """The Galaxy-ELIXIR webinar series will demonstrate how open software and public research infrastructures can be used in analysing and publishing SARS-CoV2 data.\r
\r
In a series of five webinar sessions, experts from ELIXIR and the Galaxy community in the US and Europe will demonstrate how open access and open science are fundamental for fast and efficient response to public health crises. The focus will be on research reproducibility and transparency, using exclusively open source tools and the Galaxy platform.\r
\r
The goal of the series is to demonstrate publicly accessible infrastructure and workflows for SARS-CoV-2 data analyses. The webinar sessions will guide participants step-by-step through setting up and executing the SARS-CoV-2 data analyses workflows developed by the global Galaxy community. After completing the series, participants will be able to fully reproduce the workflows and conduct their own analyses of SARS-CoV-2 data.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2020-05-28 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 500 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Galaxy Community and ELIXIR" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2020-04-30 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ] ;
    schema1:name "Galaxy-ELIXIR webinar series" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-galaxy-elixir-covid19> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/galaxy-elixir-webinar-series-fair-data-and-open-infrastructures-to-tackle-the-covid-19-pandemic> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The Galaxy Community and ELIXIR organise a webinar series to demonstrate how open software and public research infrastructures can be used in analysing and publishing SARS-CoV2 data.\r
\r
In a series of five webinar sessions, experts from ELIXIR and the Galaxy community in the US and Europe will demonstrate how open access and open science are fundamental for fast and efficient response to public health crises. The focus will be on research reproducibility and transparency, using exclusively open source tools and the Galaxy platform.\r
\r
The goal of the series is to demonstrate publicly accessible infrastructure and workflows for SARS-CoV-2 data analyses. The webinar sessions will guide participants step-by-step through setting up and executing the SARS-CoV-2 data analyses workflows developed by the global Galaxy community. After completing the series, participants will be able to fully reproduce the workflows and conduct their own analyses of SARS-CoV-2 data.\r
\r
The webinar series will start on 30 April 2020 with the first introductory session. Subsequent sessions will take place in weekly intervals.\r
\r
More information about Galaxy analyses of COVID-19 data: covid19.galaxyproject.org\r
\r
Programme:\r
\r
Session 1: Introduction to Galaxy and the Galaxy workflows for SARS-CoV-2 data analysis\r
\r
30 April 2020, 17.00-18.00 CEST (starts at 16.00 BST, 11.00 EDT, 8.00 PDT)\r
\r
The first session will introduce the Galaxy platform and other public research infrastructure to be used throughout the webinar series. It will also explain the motivation behind the Galaxy COVID-19 projects and explain the benefits of open reproducible research and transparent and interoperable analytics.\r
\r
Speakers:\r
\r
\r
	Anton Nekrutenko, Professor of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology at Penn State University (USA) and co-founder of the Galaxy Project.\r
	Sergei Pond, Professor of Biology, Institute for Genomics and Evolutionary Medicine, Temple University, US\r
	Frederik Coppens,  Head of Node of ELIXIR Belgium and Co-Lead of the ELIXIR Galaxy Community\r
	Björn Grüning, Technical Coordinator of ELIXIR Germany and Co-Lead of the ELIXIR Galaxy Community\r
\r
\r
Register\r
\r
\r
Session 2: Genomics/Variant Calling \r
\r
7 May 2020, 17.00-18.00 CEST (starts at 16.00 BST, 11.00 EDT, 8.00 PDT)\r
\r
The second session will present the initial analysis of the SARS-CoV-2 genome, published on bioRxiv. It will guide the participants through accessing and collecting the available datasets, the genome assembly and the analysis of the  within-sample sequence variants. It will also explain how to deploy on a Galaxy instance all the tools and workflows needed to reproduce the analysis.\r
\r
Speakers:\r
\r
\r
	Anton Nekrutenko, Professor of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology at Penn State University (USA) and co-founder of the Galaxy Project.\r
	Wolfgang Maier, PostDoc at the University of Freiburg and member of the European Galaxy team\r
\r
\r
Register\r
\r
\r
Session 3: Cheminformatics: Screening of the main protease\r
\r
14 May 2020, 17.00-18.00 CEST (starts at 16.00 BST, 11.00 EDT, 8.00 PDT)\r
\r
This session will present the Galaxy workflow to identify candidate molecules for COVID-19 drug treatment, using molecular docking simulation of the SARS-CoV-2 main protease. These simulations are used to predict the binding positions of the candidate molecules in the protease binding site, score the quality of each pose, and compare the results with experimental crystallographic data.\r
\r
The computationally intensive workflow was executed through a distributed compute network available via the Galaxy Europe platform. The webinar will present methods and workflows for the identification of potential COVID-19 drug candidates. Special emphasis will be given to the complex methods that have been applied and that have consumed more than 25 years of CPU and GPU time.\r
\r
Speakers:\r
\r
\r
	Tim Dudgeon, Founder and CEO of Informatics Matters\r
	Simon Bray, PhD at the University of Freiburg and member of the European Galaxy team\r
\r
\r
Register\r
\r
\r
Session 4: Evolution of the Virus\r
\r
20 May 2020, 17.00-18.00 CEST (starts at 16.00 BST, 11.00 EDT, 8.00 PDT)\r
\r
Speakers:\r
\r
\r
	Sergei Pond, Professor of Biology, Institute for Genomics and Evolutionary Medicine, Temple University, US\r
\r
\r
Register\r
\r
\r
Session 5: Behind the scenes: Global Open Infrastructures at work \r
\r
28 May 2020, 17.00-18.00 CEST (starts at 16.00 BST, 11.00 EDT, 8.00 PDT)\r
\r
This session will guide the participants how they can use the Galaxy compute capacities to run their own analysis. It will present the Pulsar network that connects data centres and High Performance Computing clusters to share their computation power in support of the Galaxy Europe users and provide examples of how to submit an analysis job from the user’s perspective.\r
\r
Speakers: TBC\r
\r
Register\r
\r
\r
Acknowledgement\r
\r
The analyses have been performed using the Galaxy platform and open source tools from BioConda. Tools were run using XSEDE resources maintained by the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC ), Pittsburgh Supercomputing Center (PSC), and Indiana University in the U.S., de.NBI,  VSC   cloud resources and  IFB cluster resources on the European side, STFC-IRIS at the Diamond Light Source, and ARDC cloud resources in Australia.\r
\r
                                                        """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-05-28 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Galaxy-ELIXIR webinar series: FAIR data and Open Infrastructures to tackle the COVID-19 pandemic" ;
    schema1:startDate "2020-04-30 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-galaxy-elixir-covid19> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/galaxy-elixir-webinars-advanced-features> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """Galaxy is supported by a large and active community distributed across the globe, which leads to rapid developments in every release. However, many of this features remain unknown by most of the users.\r
\r
With this series of four webinars, the global Galaxy community will present such advanced features, touching upon workflows, data processing and management, interoperability and interactive tools, among others. (This is independent of the Galaxy Webinar Series.) \r
\r
These events will run between 3 and 24 March 2021 and will be chaired by Hans-Rudolf Hotz.\r
\r
Programme\r
\r
3 March: Advanced Galaxy workflow features\r
\r
\r
	\r
		3 March 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
		The first webinar will cover recent Advanced Galaxy workflow features, from parameter sweeping to automatic reports:\r
\r
		\r
			Workflow-parameters\r
			Invocations\r
			Workflow-reports\r
		\r
\r
		Speakers: \r
\r
		\r
			Marius van der Beek\r
			Aysam Guerler\r
		\r
\r
		Register\r
	\r
\r
\r
10 March: Processing thousands of datasets simultaneously\r
\r
\r
	\r
		10 March 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
		Processing thousands of datasets simultaneously: From SRA/ENA import and metadata annotation to parallel processing\r
\r
		\r
			Collections\r
			Advanced collection features (tags, rule-builder, etc.)\r
		\r
\r
		Speakers: \r
\r
		\r
			Wolfgang Maier\r
			Anton Nekrutenko\r
		\r
\r
		Register\r
	\r
\r
\r
17 March: Bridging two worlds\r
\r
\r
	\r
		17 March 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
		Jupyter Notebooks and RStudio in combination with Galaxy workflows\r
\r
		\r
			Interactive tools and Galaxy communication with other projects\r
		\r
\r
		Speakers: \r
\r
		\r
			Daniel Blankenberg \r
			Bérénice Batut\r
		\r
\r
		Register\r
	\r
\r
\r
24 March: Speed up your data analysis with Galaxy: Features no one knows about\r
\r
\r
	\r
		24 March 2021, 17.00-18.00 CET (starts at 16.00 GMT, 11.00 EST, 8.00 PST)\r
\r
		Speed up your data analysis with Galaxy: Features no one knows about\r
\r
		\r
			User-preference options\r
			Data privacy, collaboration, and sharing options\r
			Your-own-reference genome\r
			Restart tools and workflows\r
			Visualisations\r
		\r
\r
		Speakers: \r
\r
		\r
			Jennifer Hillman-Jackson\r
			Anika Erxleben\r
		\r
\r
		Register\r
	\r
\r
\r
Acknowledgement\r
\r
The analyses have been performed using the Galaxy platform and open source tools from BioConda. Tools were run using XSEDE resources maintained by the Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC ), Pittsburgh Supercomputing Center (PSC), and Indiana University in the U.S., de.NBI,  VSC cloud resources and  IFB cluster resources on the European side, STFC-IRIS at the Diamond Light Source, and ARDC cloud resources in Australia.\r
\r
                                                               """ ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-03-24 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Galaxy-ELIXIR webinars: Advanced Features" ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-03-03 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
