<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-advances-in-data-driven-biomedicine> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "Professor Meijer is head of research and innovation at the Netherlands Cancer Institute (NKI). He is responsible for the innovation of the department's patient care through translational scientific research. At the research institute of the Antoni van Leeuwenhoek he leads the Diagnostic Translational Oncology section and he is group leader of a research group that conducts research into biomarker development for early detection, prognosis and predicting response to therapy in colon cancer. In addition, he is involved in local and (inter) national research infrastructure programs, especially in the field of biobanking and related areas." ;
    schema1:endDate "2024-12-04 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series:Advances in Data-Driven Biomedicine" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GHGA" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2024-12-04 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-gerrit-meijer-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-how-do-you-train-genomics-ai> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "Dr. Kyle Farh is Vice President at Illumina’s Artificial Intelligence (AI) Lab for Genome Interpretation, where he deciphers the clinical impact of genetic variants in rare and common diseases. Towards this goal, Kyle leads a multidisciplinary research team of deep learning scientists, statistical geneticists, and molecular biologists. Prior to joining Illumina, Kyle was an attending physician at Boston Children’s Hospital. Kyle completed his postdoc fellowship in Medical and Population Genetics at the Broad Institute, and received his PhD in Molecular Biology at MIT, MD at Harvard Medical School, and BS in Computer Science from Rice University." ;
    schema1:endDate "2024-11-20 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series:How do you train genomics AI?" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GHGA" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2024-11-20 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-kyle-farh-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-on-genome-sequencing-for-rare-disease-diagnostics> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "Massively parallel sequencing has transformed clinical medicine by identifying causal variants underlying rare genetic disorders, often through large gene panels or exome sequencing. While these approaches have enabled key diagnostic and therapeutic insights, a majority of patients with rare diseases still lack a molecular diagnosis. Genome sequencing offers broader variant detection capabilities, including structural and deep intronic variants, with improved coverage uniformity. However, the incremental diagnostic yield and clinical impact of genome sequencing remain uncertain. Through the Broad Center for Mendelian Genomics and the Rare Genomes Project, we analyzed over 8,000 families with suspected monogenic diseases, evaluating genome sequencing’s effectiveness where exome sequencing fell short. Our findings, further tested in an independent clinical cohort, provide insights into the specific advantages of genome sequencing and its potential for improving rare disease diagnosis. This talk will explore the key features that drive successful diagnoses and the broader implications for genomic medicine." ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-05-21 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series on Genome sequencing for rare disease diagnostics" ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-05-21 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-rami-abou-jamra-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-on-the-federated-european-genome-phenome-archive-as-a-global-network-for-sensitive-data-discovery-and-access> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "Thomas Keane is the Team Leader for the European Genome Phenome Archive (EGA) and the European Variation Archive (EVA) at EMBL-EBI. He is responsible for strategic planning for EGA and EVA, is a member of the Global Alliance for Genomics and Health (GA4GH) steering committee, and co-leads the Large-scale Genomics workstream. The Federated EGA is the primary global resource for discovery and access of sensitive human omics and associated data consented for secondary use, through a network of national human data repositories to accelerate disease research and improve human health." ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-03-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series on The Federated European Genome-Phenome Archive as a global network for sensitive data discovery and access" ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-03-19 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-thomas-keane-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-research-asset-discovery-in-large-european-networks> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description "Professor Anthony Brookes’s research interests include genome structure/variation in disease, with a big focus on knowledge management to bridge research and healthcare. Recent impact from such efforts include: the GA4GH standard ‘ADA-M’; GWAS Central; and ‘Café Variome’. Previously he revealed the ‘TUF’ phenomenon which prevents genomic DNA sequences from denaturing; collaborative discovered several disease genes (e.g., PSEN1 in Alzheimer’s Disease); invented the ‘DASH’ method for reading DNA sequences by dynamic hybridisation; and devised novel ‘Megaplex-PCR’ and hybridisation-enrichment technologies." ;
    schema1:endDate "2024-10-23 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series: Research Asset Discovery in Large European Networks" ;
    schema1:startDate "2024-10-23 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-anthony-brookes-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-with-alex-wagner> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:alternateName "The Variation Representation Specification (VRS) - enabling consistent variant exchange" ;
    schema1:description """Alex Wagner from the Nationwide Children's Hospital (Columbus, USA) will talk about "The Variation Representation Specification (VRS) - enabling consistent variant exchange"  at the GHGA lecture series "Advances in Data-Driven Biomedicine" on July 16, 2025.\r
\r
\r
Abstract: \r
\r
The Variation Representation Specification (VRS) is an open-source standard developed by the Global Alliance for Genomic Health (GA4GH) to facilitate the exchange and sharing of genetic variation data. It provides a framework for representing genetic variations in a way that is both human-readable and machine-interpretable, enabling more precise and consistent communication of genetic information between different systems and organizations.\r
\r
The Specification consists of a JSON Schema for representing many classes of genetic variation, conventions to maximize the utility of the schema, and a Python implementation that promotes adoption of the standard.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-07-16 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:name "GHGA Lecture Series with Alex Wagner" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GHGA" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-07-16 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ghga.de/events/detail/ghga-lecture-series-alex-wagner-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/ghga-lecture-series-with-antje-keppler> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:alternateName "The Euro-BioImaging BioHub and its imaging services" ;
    schema1:description """Antje Keppler from EMBL Heildelberg will talk about "The Euro-BioImaging BioHub and its imaging services"  at the GHGA lecture series "Advances in Data-Driven Biomedicine" on April 16, 2025.\r
\r
Registration:\r
\r
Please register here.\r
\r
Biography:\r
\r
Antje leads the international Bio-Hub team, which coordinates and operates the Euro-BioImaging services for biological imaging. Her other passion is coordinating the international Global BioImaging network, which promotes Open Science and open access to imaging research infrastructure services. Recently she was elected Chair of ERIC Forum, where all ERICs come together to represent their voice and interests towards European key stakeholders.\r
\r
Abstract:\r
\r
The Euro-BioImaging Bio-Hub is part of the Euro-BioImaging ERIC headquarter and is hosted by EMBL in Heidelberg. Euro-BioImaging is the European landmark research infrastructure for biological and biomedical imaging as recognised by the European Strategy Forum on Research Infrastructures (ESFRI).\r
\r
Through Euro-BioImaging, life scientists can access imaging instruments, expertise, training opportunities and data management services that they might not find at their home institutions or among their collaboration partners.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-04-16 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "GHGA" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
