<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/guiding-industry-in-the-nanoform-registration-process> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "An overview of the requirements by the different regulatory bodies & available infrastructure for safety assessment" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "mixed audience" ] ;
    schema1:description """First Part: The regulatory framework\r
\r
    10:00 – 10:10 h: Introduction: NanoCommons Knowledge Base Infrastructure by Iseult Lynch - University of Birmingham\r
    10:10 – 10:25 h: Nanomaterial definition and key phys-chem properties for RA purposes by Hubert Rauscher - JRC \r
    10:25 – 10:40 h: Key requirements for nanomaterials human and environmental RA along the life cycle by Socorro Vázquez-Campos - LEITAT \r
    10:40 – 11:00 h: Non-nano parameters and nano-specific parameters required by REACH and other regulatory frameworks by Eric Bleeker - RIVM \r
    11:00– 11:30 h: Q/A session \r
    11:30– 11:45 h: BREAK\r
\r
Second Part: Methodology for data/information acquisition \r
\r
    11:50 – 12:10 h: Gathering phys-chem information for regulatory requirements by Hubert Rauscher - JRC \r
    12:10 – 12:25 h: Exposure Assessment - Methodologies and Tools by Araceli Sánchez Jiménez - INSST \r
    12:25 – 12:40 h: Hazard Assessment – Methodologies and Tools (grouping approaches) by Vicki Stone - Heriot Watt University \r
    12:40 – 13:00 h: Usability of NanoCommons Knowledge Base Infrastructure to support industry by Iseult Lynch - University of Birmingham \r
    13:00 – 13:15 h: Virtual lab tour by Nathan Bossa - LEITAT \r
    13.15 – 13.30 h: Q/A session and Closing Remarks""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-06-02 13:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "nanocommons" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Nathan Bossa, LEITAT" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2022-06-02 10:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "grouping of NM",
        "nanoforms",
        "nanosafety assessment",
        "nanotoxicology" ;
    schema1:name "Guiding Industry in the Nanoform Registration Process" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "NanoCommons" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.nanocommons.eu/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://infrastructure.nanocommons.eu/events/64/guiding-industry-in-the-nanoform-registration-process-an-overview-of-the-requirements-by-the-different-regulatory-bodies-available-infrastructure-for-safety-assessment/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/h3abionet-10-year-symposium> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "Through its various activities across its 28 nodes, H3ABioNet has developed human capacity through training and support for data analysis, and facilitated access to informatics infrastructure by providing access to pipelines and tools for human, microbiome, and pathogen genomic data analysis. H3ABioNet has played a pivotal role in capacity development for Bioinformatics in Africa over the last 10 years and would like to celebrate its various achievements in the form of a showcase (in the form of videos, posters, testimonials, etc.).  The showcase will take place at the Cape Town International Convention Center in Cape Town, South Africa on 22 February 2023 and we invite all who are interested in attending the showcase (in person),  to register for the event. A hybrid option may be available but the event is currently planned to take place in person only.  " ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-02-22 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-02-22 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "10-Year Showcase",
        "H3ABioNet" ;
    schema1:name "H3ABioNet 10-Year Symposium" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ;
            schema1:url <https://h3abionet.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.h3abionet.org/categories/event/conferences/h3abionet-10-year-symposium> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/h3abionet-2018-genotyping-chip-data-analysis-and-gwas-lecture-series> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0769>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3516>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3517>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3796> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Computational requirements for running the H3ABioNet GWAS workflows" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Anyone intersted in GWAS and using the H3Africa genotyping chip" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Anyone who wants to learn more about GWAS" ] ;
    schema1:description """#### Computational requirements for running the H3ABioNet GWAS workflows\r
\r
The first of a series of seven online lectures for Genome Wide Association Studies (GWAS) will cover the technical requirements for setting up a your computational environment for running the H3ABioNet GWAS workflows. In this inaugural lecture of the series, Prof. Hazelhurst will cover the the following topics:\r
    Installing and using Nextflow\r
    Installing and using Github\r
    Use of containers for packaging and running tools\r
    Pulling the GWAS pipeline from Github and running it\r
\r
As this lecture aims to provide attendees with an environment to the run the H3ABioNet GWAS workflow at their own pace, there are some preliminary software requirements:\r
    Either a Linux machine or an Apple running macOS\r
    Ideally you should have machine with at least 2-4 cores and 8GB of RAM.\r
    Java 8\r
    Nextflow installed (see installation instructions at https://www.nextflow.io/)\r
    Python 3\r
\r
Please also install either Docker OR the following dependencies using pip3:\r
Pandas, Matplotlib, Openpyxl, SciPy, NumPy\r
PLINK 1.9\r
\r
[Please also refer to the following documentation to obtain the H3ABioNet GWAS workflow]:\r
https://github.com/h3abionet/h3agwas/blob/master/README.md""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2018-08-20 16:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2018-08-20 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Africa",
        "Cloud computing",
        "Docker",
        "GWAS",
        "GWAS workflow",
        "Genotyping array analysis ",
        "H3ABioNet",
        "H3ABioNet GWAS 2018 Lecture Series",
        "H3Africa genotyping array",
        "High performance computing",
        "Nextflow",
        "Population Genomics",
        "Reproducible Science",
        "Workflows",
        "bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:name "H3ABioNet 2018 Genotyping Chip Data Analysis and GWAS lecture series - Lecture 1" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4_-pxWFUpfU&list=PLcQ0XMykNhCQJPz0amnbz9BPM4Bu0Nkgf> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/h3abionet-2018-genotyping-chip-data-analysis-and-gwas-lecture-series-4a0ce1cc-f48d-4f1e-98b5-2bfc55abce6a> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3056>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3516>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3517>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3796> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Overview of Genome Wide Association Studies and study designs" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Anyone intersted in GWAS and using the H3Africa genotyping chip" ] ;
    schema1:description """### Overview of Genome Wide Association Studies and study designs\r
\r
The second of a series of seven online lectures for Genome Wide Association Studies (GWAS) will provide a basic overview on genome-wide association studies (GWAS). This is timely with the H3Africa genotyping data becoming available for several of the H3Africa research groups. To enable genomic research, the H3Africa Consortium had to build some of its own resources. This has involved sequencing whole genomes from selected under-represented African populations, analyzing the data to identify common genetic variation across major groups and developing a unique GWAS array, enriched for common African variation. The H3Africa SNP genotyping array, is currently the best-suited array available for African genome-wide association studies.\r
\r
What does it take to develop a genome study to understand genetic and environmental contributions to complex disease traits? This GWAS lecture will cover the research process from protocol development to data quality control and GWAS analysis approaches. It will include the two main study designs:\r
\r
    Case : control (e.g. diabetes, hypertension, kidney disease)\r
    Continuous trait (e.g. LDL-Cholesterol, body mass index, height) GWAS.\r
\r
I will discuss the basic building blocks of a GWAS study, the different study designs, power of a study to detect genetic association (based in sample size, allele frequency and expected effect size), and replication studies. The advantages and disadvantages of doing GWAS in African populations will be discussed.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2018-08-22 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2018-08-22 15:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "AWI-GEN",
        "African genome structure",
        "African genomics",
        "African populations",
        "Allelic association",
        "Case Control study",
        "Common and rare variants",
        "Complex traits",
        "GWAS",
        "GWAS study designs",
        "GWAS workflow",
        "Genetic Diversity",
        "Genotype association",
        "Genotypes",
        "H3ABioNet",
        "H3ABioNet GWAS 2018 Lecture Series",
        "H3Africa",
        "H3Africa genotyping array",
        "Populations Genomics",
        "Quantitative traits",
        "Sample size",
        "Study design" ;
    schema1:name "H3ABioNet 2018 Genotyping Chip Data Analysis and GWAS lecture series - Lecture 2" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "H3ABioNet" ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4_-pxWFUpfU&list=PLcQ0XMykNhCQJPz0amnbz9BPM4Bu0Nkgf> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/h3abionet-2018-genotyping-chip-data-analysis-and-gwas-lecture-series-lecture-3> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3516>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3796> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Genotype calling from Illumina files" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Anyone intersted in GWAS and using the H3Africa genotyping chip" ] ;
    schema1:description """### Genotype calling from Illumina files\r
The third of a series of seven H3ABioNet online lectures for Genome Wide Association Studies (GWAS) will introduce genotyping SNP array chips with particular emphasis on the H3Africa genotype chip. The lecture will cover common file formats when obtaining genotyping chip data from a service provider such as Illumina and software used for genotype calling. This lecture will also cover genotype calling, sample and probe quality control, probe viewing and exporting of data using GenomeStudio. Converting the data to PLINK format and some tips on troubleshooting and common pitfalls will be discussed.""" ;
