    schema1:url <https://infrastructure.nanocommons.eu/events/60/instance-maps-breaking-up-your-experiments-into-logical-blocks/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/handle-your-data-the-tidy-way-data-science-using-r-and-the-tidyverse> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:contact """Daniele Parisi\r
dparisi@esat.kuleuven.be""" ;
    schema1:description """The use of R in the data science field has increased enormously the last few years. One very useful toolkit for a wide variety of analyses is the ‘tidyverse’: a collection of R-packages used for data manipulation, exploration and visualization that share a common design philosophy. Tidyverse packages are intended to make statisticians and data scientists more productive by guiding them through workflows that facilitate communication, and result in reproducible work products.\r
\r
In this workshop you will learn:\r
\r
    The difference between tidy and untidy data\r
    How to create tidy datasets and manipulate them using the main tidyr and dplyr verbs\r
    Make your results reproducible by using Rmarkdown and Rstudio\r
    Integrate data visualisation into your workflow using ggplot2\r
    Apply the tidyverse to biological problems\r
\r
LEVEL: A very basic knowledge of the R programming language (or any other programming language) is expected.\r
\r
We expect a little bit of R-experience for this workshop. If you’re already an R-wizard but never did anything using Tidyverse, then this workshop is a great introduction to it! If you’re not an R-wizard but now a little bit of R, then this course is a great way to improve your R-skills fast.\r
\r
If you’re already pretty good with Tidyverse, I guess we can not help you as this is an introduction\r
\r
Please remember to bring your own laptop with the last version of R installed.\r
\r
Location: Campus Gusthuisberg O&amp;N2 HP5 (05.204) and HP7 (05.208) classrooms""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2017-12-13 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Elixir Belgium" ] ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "KU Leuven" ;
    schema1:keywords " R-programming",
        "tidyverse" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Leuven" ;
                    schema1:addressRegion "Vlaams-Brabant" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "UCLL Campus Gasthuisberg, 49, Herestraat" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "50.880679" ;
            schema1:longitude "4.674543" ] ;
    schema1:name "Handle your data the tidy way: Data science using R and the tidyverse" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Stijn Wittouck and Sander Wuyts" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2017-12-13 14:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://bbc2017blog.wordpress.com/workshops/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/hands-on-introduction-to-chip-seq-and-atac-seq-analysis> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The aim is to:\r
\r
\r
	have an understanding of the nature of ChIP-seq and ATAC-seq data\r
	perform a complete analysis workflow including QC, read mapping, visualization in a genome browser and peak-calling using GenePattern\r
	have an overview of possible downstream analyses\r
	perform a motif analysis with online web programs\r
\r
 This training does not cover all methods available today. It does not aim at bringing users to a professional NGS analyst level but provides enough information to allow biologists understand what DNA sequencing practically is and to communicate with NGS experts for more in-depth needs.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2020-02-17 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
