<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/how-to-connect-on-and-off-the-conference-stage> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "As a researcher, you attend conferences to stay up to date, connect with peers and share your work. But how do you make sure this investment pays off?Let's be honest: walking up to someone you don't know can feel awkward. Delivering your message in a clear and memorable way, even more so. And once you've made an interesting connection, how do you follow up effectively?In this hands-on workshop, we'll tackle both the research pitch you give on stage and  the informal conversations that happen during coffee breaks. You'll learn how to engage others with confidence, communicate your key message and turn spontaneous moments in to valuable opportunities.Participants will receive exclusive access to:A short video course on how to pitch your research clearly and effectively.The Pitch your research AI assistant (developed by The Floor is Yours), to help you craft and refine strong pitches faster.A practical handout summarizing the key insights from the workshop." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2026-02-12 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Zwijnaarde" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "9052" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Ghent - VIB/UGent FSVM II, Technologiepark 75" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "51.000392" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.71677" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2026-01-29 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "communication skills",
        "personal effectiveness" ;
    schema1:name "How to connect on and off the conference stage" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/how-connect-and-conference-stage> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/how-to-describe-openrisknet-services-and-their-functionality-by-semantic-annotation> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0089> ;
    schema1:description """Providing the data and software services within OpenRiskNet virtual environment and make the functionality available via application programming interfaces (APIs) already allows for the development of advanced workflows providing solutions to complex risk assessment tasks (demonstrated in the case studies webinars). However, identification and selection of the most appropriate service, understanding their functionality and format the input and output still need manual investigation and programming/scripting skills. To be able to automate these tasks, OpenRiskNet is exploring semantic annotation on different levels. \r
\r
After a short introduction to ontologies applicable to service annotation, we will first demonstrate high-level annotation, which gives the information for the OpenRiskNet registry service to index the service and to make it findable for users and gives basic categorisation like dataset, algorithm and model. Then we will show low level annotation, which will be used for dataset and input/output harmonisation so that the user but more important other services can understand the information provided/required and how to access it. Finally, first approaches to define the middle layer providing metadata describing the service in more detail like available toxicity domains, available endpoint as well as gene/pathway/AOP associations will be shown.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-05-13 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-05-13 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "How to describe OpenRiskNet services and their functionality by semantic annotation" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "OpenRiskNet" ;
            schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://openrisknet.org/events/64/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/how-to-design-and-deliver-pathogen-genomics-training-for-health-and-research-professionals> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Develop training and facilitation skills in genomics for healthcare and pathogen surveillance" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "public health bioinformaticians" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Healthcare professionals " ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "medical scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "clinical microbiologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "clinical bioinformaticians" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "public health epidemiologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "clinical scientists" ] ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """We invite applicants from research, healthcare and training professionals who are involved in any aspect of genomics or data science, and who are interested in building their capabilities to effectively share their knowledge and skills with others focused on pathogen genomic analysis, application and interpretation in management of infectious disease. This includes clinical, biomedical, and public health scientists, epidemiologists, bioinformaticians.\r
\r
Applicants may also be involved in genomics technologies and computational biology training programmes, and interested in developing training resources for potential application of pathogen genomics in healthcare and public health.""" ;
    schema1:description """Watch the [promo video] (https://vimeo.com/779188141)\r
\r
A collaboration between the *Centre for Genomic Pathogen Surveillance* and *Wellcome Connecting Science*, the programme will apply the concept of forward training and equip professionals who interface with pathogen genomics data science with the skills needed to train others.\r
\r
This *FREE* course will provide participants with methods and techniques for training adult learners in analysis of genomics as applied in clinical management and public health. It integrates pedagogical aspects with pathogen genomic analysis, application and interpretation for management and surveillance of infectious disease. Participants will develop deeper knowledge of teaching and facilitation methodologies that they can later apply in their respective fields and work environments. Using a project-based learning approach, participants will be mentored on how to build their own training materials and design their own training. A combination of online training resources and in-person sessions will be followed by ongoing mentorship and network building activities.\r
\r
Read more [here] (https://coursesandconferences.wellcomeconnectingscience.org/news_item/expanding-pathogen-genomics-expertise-across-the-uk/)""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-06-29 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "UKRI – UK Research and Innovation" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Hinxton" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Cambridgeshire" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "CB10 1SA" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Wellcome Genome Campus" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "52.07889" ;
                    schema1:longitude "0.187629" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Centre for Genomic Pathogen Surveillance (University of Oxford) and Wellcome Connecting Science" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-06-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords " data science",
        "Bioinformatics",
        "Pedagogy",
        "epidemiology",
        "pathogen genomics",
        "public health",
        "surveillance",
        "train-the-trainer" ;
    schema1:name "How to Design and Deliver Pathogen Genomics Training for Health and Research Professionals" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Wellcome Connecting Science" ] ;
    schema1:teaches """At the end of the course, trainees will be able to:\r
* Explain the flow of pathogen genomic data/information from generation to interpretation in healthcare and public health settings.\r
* Design training and communication formats by applying evidence-based learning science methodologies.\r
* Identify appropriate training resources for use in training pathogen genomics and surveillance tools.\r
* Deliver pathogen genomic data science training to professionals working in genomic epidemiology, surveillance and outbreak investigation.\r
* Evaluate the self-developed training and knowledge sharing of pathogen genomic data science.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://coursesandconferences.wellcomeconnectingscience.org/event/how-to-design-and-deliver-pathogen-genomics-training-for-health-and-research-professionals-20230626/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/how-to-efficiently-access-the-data-of-bacdive-brenda-lpsn-silva-and-more> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educator(s)\r
The DSMZ Digital Diversity Team\r
\r
Date\r
11 November 2024, 14:00-16:00 CET\r
\r
Location\r
Online\r
\r
Contents\r
This workshop will give a short introduction to the new DSMZ Digital Diversity infrastructure and the Digital Diversity Hub as the gateway to the connected ressources, before splitting into breakout rooms to talk about the individual databases. In four parallel sessions you will learn how to effectively use BacDive (incl. StrainInfo and MediaDive), BRENDA, LPSN or SILVA to advance your research.\r
\r
Learning goals\r
Efficiently use the DSMZ Digital Diversity resources.\r
\r
Prerequisites: \r
None\r
\r
Keywords\r
Databases, Bioinformatics, Bacteria, Enzymes, Nomenclature, Taxonomy, Cultivation, Strain identity \r
\r
Tools\r
BacDive, BRENDA, LPSN, SILVA, StrainInfo, MediaDive""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-11-11 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
