<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/integrating-gene-expression-and-biological-knowledge-for-drug-discovery-and-repurposing> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0204>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3336>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3373> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformaticians and wet-lab biologists who can program" ] ;
    schema1:description """Screening for potential cancer therapies using existing large datasets of drug perturbations requires expertise and resources not available to all. This is often a barrier for lab scientists to tap into these valuable resources. To address these issues, one can take advantage of prior knowledge especially those coded in standard formats such as causal biological networks (CBN). Large datasets can be converted into appropriate structures, analyzed once and the results made freely available in easy-to-use formats. **In this three parts tutorial, we will give a full description of one large scale analysis of using this approach, one case study of building a network of metastasis suppressors from scratch, and a walkthrough example code to perform and adapt these tools for different use cases.**\r
\r
**Detailed description:**\r
\r
- Part One: a talk describing the construction of a database for cancer-cell-specific perturbations of biological networks (LINPS). We pre-computed cancer-cell-specific perturbation amplitudes of several biological networks and made the output available in a database with an interactive web interface.\r
- Part Two: a talk describing the building of a functional network model of the metastasis suppressor RKIP and its regulators in breast cancer cells. In this case study, we applied text mining and a manual literature search to extract known interactions between several metastasis suppressors and their regulators. Then we adopted a reverse causal reasoning approach to evaluate and prioritize pathways that are most consistent and responsive to drugs that inhibit cell growth. We further validated some of the predicted regulatory links in the breast cancer cell line MCF7 experimentally and highlighted the points of uncertainty in our model.\r
- Part Three: a code walkthrough encoding directed interactions into the biological expression language (BEL), computing the network perturbation amplitudes (NPA), and interpreting the output.\r
\r
**Pre-requisites:**\r
\r
● Knowledge of the mechanisms of transcriptional regulation\r
● Familiarity with high-throughput gene expression data\r
● Basic knowledge of R and Bioconductor\r
● Familiarity with Docker and Git (This is only required to execute the code on a local machine)""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-04-22 11:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 50 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "International Society for Computational Biology (ISCB)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2022-04-22 08:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Data Integration",
        "Genome",
        "Genomic data",
        "Sequence Analysis",
        "Workshop" ;
    schema1:name "Integrating gene expression and biological knowledge for drug discovery and repurposing" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.iscb.org/cms_addon/academy/events#65> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/integrating-knowledge-of-proteins-and-small-molecules-with-uniprotkb> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0078>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0154>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3172> ;
    schema1:description """A complete understanding of biological systems requires integration of knowledge of the regulation and function of small molecule metabolites – the metabolome – with that of genes, transcripts, and proteins (the genome, transcriptome, and metabolome).

The UniProt knowledgebase [UniProtKB](https://www.uniprot.org) supports biomedical research by providing a comprehensive, high quality and freely accessible resource of protein sequences and functional information. UniProtKB integrates, interprets and standardizes data from a range of sources including the scientific literature, tools for protein sequence analysis, other knowledge resources and databases, and automatic annotation systems, to provide a detailed overview of the available protein knowledge.

This webinar will provide an introduction into how UniProtKB captures and represents knowledge of small molecule metabolites using the [Rhea knowledgebase of biochemical reactions](https://www.rhea-db.org/), based on the [chemical ontology ChEBI](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/), and how UniProt users can access and exploit that knowledge.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2021-03-25 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 500 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2021-03-25 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Metabolites",
        "Proteins (proteins)",
        "UniProt: The Universal Protein Resource",
        "UniProtKB" ;
    schema1:name "Integrating knowledge of proteins and small molecules with UniProtKB" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
