<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/introduction-to-qualitative-modelling-with-maboss> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/operation_3660>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_2259> ;
    schema1:description """Boolean modelling uses a simple representation of biological entities as either active or inactive, and describes their relations with logical formulas. [MaBoSS](https://maboss.curie.fr/) extends Boolean modelling by adding a notion of continuous time, with the introduction of rates of (in)activation. This enable the representation of physical time, and of processes with different time scales. MaBoSS simulate multiple stochastic trajectories, and produces trajectories of the probabilities of the system state. This webinar will introduce the concepts of boolean modelling, and MaBoSS’ continuous time boolean modelling, and will then demonstrate the use of WebMaBoSS, a web interface designed for easily simulating MaBoSS models.  

### About the speaker

[Dr Vincent Noël](https://www.linkedin.com/in/vincent-no%C3%ABl-13390055/) (M) is a postdoctoral student at the Computational Systems Biology of Cancer team of the U900 in [Institut Curie](https://institut-curie.org/). His research interests concern modelling of biological systems, where he has experience in both construction and analysis of these models. He did his PhD in applied mathematics studying simplifications of ODE models, and then did his first postdoc within a molecular biology unit building a model of Ras-Mapk signaling in Y1 tumor cells. He also developed tools to simplify the construction and analysis of models, and the reproducibility of this work. He has a good experience in the optimisation of numerical simulation workflows, and has been working on MaBoSS for more than three years.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2021-11-18 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 500 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2021-11-18 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Cell-level simulations",
        "PerMedCoE",
        "Personalised medicine" ;
    schema1:name "Introduction to qualitative modelling with MaBoSS" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/introduction-qualitative-modelling-maboss> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/introduction-to-quantum-computing> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "On-demand (start any time) with regular tutor facilitation." ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Industry professionals, and people with a keen interest in the subject." ] ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "There are no entry requirements for this course and no previous knowledge of quantum computing is required. " ;
    schema1:description """The course is facilitated by world-leading experts from UCL’s Quantum Science and Technology institute. You'll be guided through the fundamentals of quantum theory without the need for any in-depth mathematical knowledge.\r
\r
You’ll be introduced to the definitions and properties of qubits, before comparing the characteristics of classical bits and quantum bits. There are no entry requirements for this course and no previous knowledge of quantum computing is required. """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-08-31 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "University College London" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-03-21 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Quantum" ;
    schema1:name "Introduction to Quantum Computing" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University College London" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "UCL" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "UCL Engineering" ;
            schema1:url <https://bit.ly/3OzH0Vu> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR UK" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixiruknode.org/activities/training> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """After completing the course, you should be able to:\r
\r
- explain the key differences in non-mathematical terms between classical and quantum computers \r
- identify quantum computing hardware platforms by the technology they are based on \r
- outline the most promising applications of quantum computers and their limitations.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://bit.ly/40nj1yV> .

