<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/licensing-research-outputs> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """While doing scientific research, you handle different kinds of research outputs, whether they are produced by you or by somebody else. So, how can you ensure that other people will give you credit when using your produced materials? And how will you know how exactly you can use the resources produced by others? This is a topic that copyright laws handle. \r
\r
This lecture will cover what copyright laws do, who has the rights to the materials, and how you can use them. Most of this information is protected with licenses, whether they are meant for figures, data, or software. This lecture will discuss what licenses to use for different materials and what each license covers (this means how people can use the materials). We will also show how exactly you can apply a license to your research outputs. """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-09-27 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Estonia" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Tartu" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "58.380121" ;
                    schema1:longitude "26.72245" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2022-09-27 14:15:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "CONVERGE",
        "Data management",
        "ELIXIR-CONVERGE",
        "licensing" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs course" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7118427> ] ;
    schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University of Tartu, Institute of Computer Science" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <http://elixir.ut.ee> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """Knows what items need a license\r
Knows the restrictions on licensing a dataset\r
Knows what licenses to use for what research output\r
Knows how to apply a license to a research output practically""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/node/450> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/licensing-research-outputs-72bf4211-b212-4674-9cfa-294a82d09dc3> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Data Steward" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD student" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Data Manager" ] ;
    schema1:description """In scientific research, you work with many types of outputs, whether created by you or by others. How can you ensure that others give you proper credit when using your materials? And how can you know exactly what you are allowed to do with resources created by others? These questions fall under the scope of copyright law.

This lecture will explain what copyright laws do, who holds the rights to research materials, and how those rights affect their use. We will cover the role of licenses in protecting and sharing different types of outputs—such as figures, datasets, and software—and explain what each license permits. Finally, we will demonstrate how to choose and apply an appropriate license to your own research outputs.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-09-05 14:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 20 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-09-05 12:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Data Management",
        "licensing" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs slides" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17076073> ] ;
    schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/news/2025/08/18/Licensing_05-09/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/licensing-research-outputs-ffbe278d-be66-498c-a6bc-4df1115dce9e> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate students" ] ;
    schema1:description """On the 25th of April 2023, ELIXIR-Estonia will hold a data management lecture: Licensing Research Outputs. The course will be held in English and will be held online. \r
\r
While doing scientific research, you handle different kinds of research outputs, whether they are produced by you or by somebody else. So, how can you ensure that other people will give you credit when using your produced materials? And how will you know how exactly you can use the resources produced by others? This is a topic that copyright laws handle. \r
\r
This lecture will cover what copyright laws do, who has the rights to the materials, and how you can use them. Most of this information is protected with licenses, whether they are meant for figures, data, or software. This lecture will discuss what licenses to use for different materials and what each license covers (this means how people can use the materials). We will also show how exactly you can apply a license to your research outputs. """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-04-25 12:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-04-25 10:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "CONVERGE",
        "Data management",
        "ELIXIR-CONVERGE",
        "licensing" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7863005> ] ;
    schema1:name "Licensing Research Outputs" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <http://elixir.ut.ee> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University of Tartu, Institute of Computer Science" ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* Know what items need a license\r
* Know the restrictions on licensing a dataset\r
* Know what licenses to use for what research output\r
* Know how to apply a license to a research output practically""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/node/486> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/life-cell-by-cell-introduction-to-single-cell-expression-atlas> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:contact "Anna Swan" ;
    schema1:description "Explore gene expression profiles across various species and conditions at single cell resolution." ;
    schema1:endDate "2019-11-13 16:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:keywords "HDRUK" ;
    schema1:name "Life cell by cell: Introduction to Single Cell Expression Atlas" ;
    schema1:startDate "2019-11-13 15:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/2019/life-cell-cell-introduction-single-cell-expression-atlas> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/life-cell-by-cell-introduction-to-single-cell-expression-atlas-876a06b9-f713-4e7c-8a01-916701b30b21> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203> ;
    schema1:description """Explore gene expression profiles across various species and conditions at single cell resolution.

Single Cell Expression Atlas (SCEA) is an open resource committed to making the information from single cell sequencing datasets readily accessible to the research community and beyond. At SCEA we accumulate, curate and re-analyse available raw single-cell sequencing data from multiple species and across experimental conditions to make them cross-comparable and we present the analysis results in a user-friendly interface for public consumption. This allows researchers to gain a quick insight into the expression pattern of their gene of interest at the level of individual cells across different species from human to Saccharomyces.

At SCEA we aim to keep pace with the rapidly growing single cell transcriptomics research and to make the knowledge thus obtained truly open and widely available so that it can serve as basis for further studies.

The webinar will provide a quick overview of:

- Different single cell sequencing methods
- Various ways of searching SCEA
- How to filter and interpret the analysis results on the experiment page
- Additional features and resources available on the experiment page""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-11-13 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 200 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-11-13 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Expression Atlas",
        "Gene expression (gene-expression)",
        "Introduction to single cell expression atlas",
        "Life cell by cell",
        "SCEA",
        "Single Cell Expression Atlas",
        "Single cell RNA-seq" ;
    schema1:name "Life cell by cell: Introduction to Single Cell Expression Atlas" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/life-cell-cell-introduction-single-cell-expression-atlas> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/life-science-data-management-planning-workshop> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Senior scientist/ Principal investigator" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Life Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD candidates" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Professors" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "All postgraduates" ] ;
    schema1:contact "contact@bioinfo.no" ;
    schema1:description """We are pleased to announce the Data Management planning workshop for Life Scientists in Norway by ELIXIR Norway and Digital Life Norway.\r
\r
All Norwegian universities require a DMP for all projects. All projects that receive funding from the Norwegian RCN are required to submit a data management plan as soon as possible after the contract with the RCN has been signed. This workshop focuses on how-to generate a data management plan for a Life Science research project that will meet both requirements.\r
 \r
\r
We would like to invite researchers (PhD candidates, Post Doctoral Fellows, Researchers, Associate Professors and Professors) in existing and upcoming Life Science projects to develop their DMP in this workshop.\r
\r
Maximum number of participants: 45\r
\r
The workshop will take place if there is at least 10 participants\r
\r
26.09.2022\r
\r
0830 - 0900 Login support\r
\r
0900 - 0915 Introduction\r
\r
0915 - 0930 Requirements from the RCN\r
\r
0930 - 1000 Parallel session: Local recommendations &amp; storage infrastructures Institutional DMP guidelines\r
\r
1000 - 1015 Short break\r
\r
1015 - 1100 Introduction DSW\r
\r
1100 - 1120 Information on national storage infrastructures\r
\r
\r
	NeLS/StoreBioInfo\r
	NIRD\r
	TSD\r
\r
\r
1120 - 1135 Short break\r
\r
1135 - 1230 Persistent identifiers, Controlled Vocabularies &amp; Ontologies\r
\r
 \r
\r
27.09.2022\r
\r
0900 - 1000 Key legal requirements for research in the GDPR and Health research act\r
\r
\r
	Personal Data Act\r
	Health Research Act\r
\r
\r
1000 - 1015 Short break\r
\r
1015 - 1045 Relevant deposition repositories/ data archives\r
\r
\r
	domain specific archives (ELIXIR/EMBL-EBI, NCBI)\r
	Dataverse NO\r
	EGA\r
	NIRD archive\r
	NSD\r
\r
\r
1045 - 1100 Short break\r
\r
1100 - 1115 Licensing of data, biological material and software\r
\r
1115 - 1300 Hands on training and assistance to generate a data management plan\r
\r
Requirements: Join us online and bring your project proposal . You will get the opportunity to set up a data management plan for your project with help of the Data Stewardship Wizard tool using a structured and detailed questionnaire with references to relevant resources.\r
\r
Looking forward to see you at the workshop.\r
\r
 \r
\r
Register here or below by 21 September 2022\r
\r
 """ ;
    schema1:eligibility "first_come_first_served" ;
    schema1:endDate "2022-09-27 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Converge" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Centre for Digital Life Norway" ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Compliance",
