<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/managing-single-cell-transcriptomics-data-e280715f-b963-4497-ae4f-cdd45718ab76> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "This course is aimed at researchers who are generating, planning on generating, or working with single cell RNA sequencing or image-based transcriptomics data.&nbsp;This course will not cover any aspects of data analysis, therefore no prior computational knowledge is required." ] ;
    schema1:description "This course aims to provide researchers generating and working with single-cell RNA sequencing or image-based transcriptomics data with the tools and knowledge for generating high-quality metadata alongside their data and analyses. Participants will learn what it means to make their data FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) and what tools and standards are available to help them with good data management and FAIRification. Finally, participants will learn about the Human Cell Atlas project and how to ensure their data reaches the wider research community." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-07-05 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Cambridge" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "CB10 1SD" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "European Bioinformatics Institute, Hinxton" ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 30 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-07-03 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "DNA & RNA (dna-rna)" ;
    schema1:name "Managing single cell transcriptomics data" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/managing-single-cell-transcriptomics-data> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/managing-stress-and-burnout-advanced> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "These sessions are part of a learning path. You first need to participate in the introductory session before you can attend these in-depth sessions. More information on the introductory session can be found here.Session 1: Training your brain to get into relaxation status and tackle stress in daily lifeYou can automate the methods of getting into deep relaxation so that you can drop in your brain frequency in shorter and shorter periods of time. By doing these exercises, you learn to drop to low alpha and thêta, the levels of relaxation where you have much more self-control and you can recondition your brain itself in about 2 minutes. In this session, we will also learn several short methods that can be used at any time of the day. These methods help to drop slightly in brain frequency. So, if you use them quickly enough (before you are completely stressed), they will help you calm down quickly in acute moments and keep your self-control. By using these methods, you also prevent the stress from building up and leaving you exhausted and tired in the evening. Thanks to the application of these methods you can prevent accumulation of stress.Session 2: The power of beliefs, the missing link to achieve lasting changeOur beliefs are the way we look at the world. We think it is the only truth, and as a result, we come into conflict with ourselves and those around us. After all, a belief is only a partial truth. Working first and then playing (= relaxing) is a good thought in itself, but limiting if you have been raised with it. If I relax first, then I will work more efficiently afterward, which is equally correct and thus complementary. It is AND - AND. When both beliefs are imprinted 100% in your brain, then you have a choice and can adapt according to the situation and need. We are not aware of which beliefs we have and which ones we lack. This is 80-90% of the cause of our stress in all areas of life. In this module, you will reflect on your thoughts, why you think this way, and what complementary thoughts you need to reduce your stress." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2026-03-05 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Leuven" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "3000" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Leuven - Campus Gasthuisberg, Herestraat 49" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "50.879202" ;
                    schema1:longitude "4.701168" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2026-02-13 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "wellbeing" ;
    schema1:name "Managing stress and burnout - advanced" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/managing-stress-and-burnout-advanced-0> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/managing-stress-and-burnout-advanced-2ef2e6fa-4134-46e5-b149-f88ebf53c28d> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "These sessions are part of a learning path. You first need to participate in the introductory session before you can attend these in-depth sessions. More information on the introductory session can be found here.Session 1: Training your brain to get into relaxation status and tackle stress in daily lifeYou can automate the methods of getting into deep relaxation so that you can drop in your brain frequency in shorter and shorter periods of time. By doing these exercises, you learn to drop to low alpha and thêta, the levels of relaxation where you have much more self-control and you can recondition your brain itself in about 2 minutes. In this session, we will also learn several short methods that can be used at any time of the day. These methods help to drop slightly in brain frequency. So, if you use them quickly enough (before you are completely stressed), they will help you calm down quickly in acute moments and keep your self-control. By using these methods, you also prevent the stress from building up and leaving you exhausted and tired in the evening. Thanks to the application of these methods you can prevent accumulation of stress.Session 2: The power of beliefs, the missing link to achieve lasting changeOur beliefs are the way we look at the world. We think it is the only truth, and as a result, we come into conflict with ourselves and those around us. After all, a belief is only a partial truth. Working first and then playing (= relaxing) is a good thought in itself, but limiting if you have been raised with it. If I relax first, then I will work more efficiently afterward, which is equally correct and thus complementary. It is AND - AND. When both beliefs are imprinted 100% in your brain, then you have a choice and can adapt according to the situation and need. We are not aware of which beliefs we have and which ones we lack. This is 80-90% of the cause of our stress in all areas of life. In this module, you will reflect on your thoughts, why you think this way, and what complementary thoughts you need to reduce your stress." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
