<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/metagenomics-bioinformatics-virtual-b1ac31e1-806d-4ebe-97f8-9134d8044751> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3174> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType """This course is aimed at life scientists who are working in the field of metagenomics and are currently in the early stages of data analysis. Participants should have some prior experience of using bioinformatics in their research.\r
\r
The practical sessions in the course require a basic understanding of the Unix command line and the R statistics package. Participants should ensure that they&nbsp;complete these free tutorials before attending the course:\r
\r
\r
	Basic introduction to the Unix environment:&nbsp;www.ee.surrey.ac.uk/Teaching/Unix\r
	Basic R concept tutorials:&nbsp;www.r-tutor.com/r-introduction""" ] ;
    schema1:description """Gain knowledge of the tools, processes and analysis approaches used in the field of metagenomics.

This course will cover the metagenomics data analysis workflow from the point of newly generated sequence data. Participants will explore the use of publicly available resources and tools to manage, share, analyse and interpret metagenomics data. The content will include issues of data quality control and how to submit to public repositories. While sessions will detail marker-gene and whole-genome shotgun (WGS) approaches; the primary focus will be on assembly-based approaches. Discussions will also explore considerations when assembling genome data, the analysis that can be carried out by MGnify on such datasets, and what downstream analysis options and tools are available.

#### Virtual course

The course content will involve participants learning via pre-recorded lectures, live presentations and trainer Q&amp;A sessions. Practical experience will be developed through group activities and computational exercises. Sessions will be delivered over [Zoom](https://zoom.us/) with additional text communication over [Slack](https://slack.com/intl/en-gb/). Trainers will be available to assist, answer questions and further explain the analysis during these times. Computational practicals will be run in [Docker containers](https://www.docker.com/) operating on EMBL-EBI's virtual training infrastructure; this means there is no need to have a powerful computer to run exercises or a requirement to install complex software before the course.

Participants will need to be available between the hours of 09:00-17:00 GMT each day of the course.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2020-11-06 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 30 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2020-11-02 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Metagenomics bioinformatics (Virtual)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/metagenomics-bioinformatics-virtual> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/metagenomics-data-analysis-and-interpretation> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """This course will provide an overview of the main aspects involved in metagenomics data analysis and discussion around the interpretation and actual examples of the impact and applications of metagenomics derived research. A substantial part of the course will be devoted to hands-on experience with bioinformatics resources and tools relevant in metagenomics data analysis.\r
\r
Participants will start with an overview of NGS technologies, a look at experimental approaches and emerging technologies, including a tour of Earlham Institute’s Genomic Pipelines laboratories. Then the remainder of the course will be spent in front of the computers learning how to produce metagenomic assemblies, and taking participants from data to publication-ready figures.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-09-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Norwich" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Norfolk" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "NR4 7UZ" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Earlham Institute (EI), Colney Lane" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "52.621741" ;
                    schema1:longitude "1.218987" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Earlham Institute" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-09-16 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Metagenomics: Data Analysis and Interpretation" ;
    schema1:url <http://www.earlham.ac.uk/metagenomics-data-analysis-and-interpretation> .

