<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/methods-for-infectious-disease-modelling-using-genomics> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/operation_0324>,
        <http://edamontology.org/operation_2426>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3305>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3324> ;
    schema1:description "This EMBO Practical course will introduce concepts, methods, and tools for carrying out infectious disease modelling, as well as for developing models further by leveraging the power of genomics. Modelling infectious diseases can help to understand the mechanisms of pathogen evolution and predict the development of epidemics. This had an immense impact on the COVID-19 pandemic and is crucial for the general surveillance of pathogens. In this course, participants will learn about compartmental models, such as Susceptible-Infectious-Recovered (SIR) models, how to adapt them to a pathogen of their interest, and the differences between stochastic and deterministic models. Taking this further, participants will apply these models to epidemic settings in order to predict changes in case numbers and disease severity. Additionally, the participants will learn how to implement and adapt genomics-informed models for pathogen evolution, and they will learn how to make use of genomics to create timed phylogenies and reconstruct ancestral states. Finally, bringing together transmission modelling and genomic data, participants will learn about phylodynamic models and how this can help to detect changes in case numbers and pathogenicity earlier and more reliably than with transmission models alone. " ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-09-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Cambridge" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "CB10 1SD" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "European Bioinformatics Institute, Hinxton" ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 30 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-09-14 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "genomics",
        "infectious diseases",
        "mathematical modelling",
        "phylodynamics",
        "public health" ;
    schema1:name "Methods for infectious disease modelling using genomics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/infectious-disease-modelling-using-genomics-2025> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/methods-for-rare-variant-association-analysis> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/operation_3227>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622> ;
    schema1:description """Rare variants contribute to development of familial cancer.  Genes carrying rare variants may contribute to molecular mechanisms of sporadic cancers.  Historically, many of the rare variants were discovered by co-segregation with the disease in cancer families.  However, recent progress in population scale sequencing opens new opportunities for using association analysis for detection of rare variants.  

The standard methods of association analysis were developed for common variants.  While the standard regression framework and population stratification approaches hold in the rare-variant analysis, it may additionally require (i) aggregating of variants per gene (or pathway), (ii) weighting by biological significance and allelic frequency, and (iii) applying permutation-style tests for estimating statistical significance.  

The webinar will discuss these features of rare-variant analysis and illustrate their implementation using SKAT R library. 

 """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-04-06 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:streetAddress ", " ] ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 1000 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2022-04-06 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "DNA & RNA (dna-rna)",
        "Rare-variant",
        "SKAT",
        "Variant association" ;
    schema1:name "Methods for rare-variant association analysis" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/methods-rare-variant-association-analysis> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/methods-in-bioimage-analysis> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Microscopy is a key technology driving biological discovery. Nowadays, microscopy based scientific findings must be substantiated by quantitative image analysis. The discipline concerned with such quantification of biological microscopy images is called bioimage analysis.

This webinar will walk us through the main concepts of a typical bioimage analysis workflow, including how to quantitatively interpret the content of microscopy images, how to automatically detect objects in images and derive object-based measurements. It will emphasise the importance of visual inspection and quality control of automated image analysis.

Finally, this webinar will also provide an overview of current bioimage analysis tools and communities.

 """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2022-03-23 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
