<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/negotiating-with-success> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "Negotiation is an essential skill in research and professional collaboration. Whether you’re discussing project timelines, resources, authorship, or team responsibilities, effective negotiation helps build trust and long-term partnerships.This workshop focuses on both the mindset and the tools required to negotiate with confidence and integrity. Participants learn to recognize their own negotiation style, understand the dynamics of influence, and develop strategies to reach sustainable, win–win outcomes. Through interactive exercises and real-world simulations, the course helps participants move from positional bargaining to constructive, interest-based negotiation." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2026-03-16 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2026-01-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "leadership & management",
        "online",
        "personal effectiveness" ;
    schema1:name "Negotiating with success" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/negotiating-success> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/neof-bacterial-16s-metabarcoding> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene is a well established method of determining the bacterial taxonomic composition of microbiomes. This has been used for human and animal body sites, soil, sewage, clouds, deserts, permafrost and many other environments. This course will give you the ability to describe the advantages and disadvantages of 16S rRNA sequencing, and analyse 16S rRNA datasets with the QIIME2 bioinformatics platform.\r
\r
Places are restricted to UK-based researchers and only 30 places are available so please only register if you intend on attending.\r
\r
The workshop will be held over two days on Tuesday 16 and Thursday 18, 10am-4pm, with registration opening 9.30am on Tuesday.\r
\r
The deadline to register is Monday 18th December. You will hear later that week whether you have been invited and will need to confirm your attendance.\r
\r
Register to NEOF mailing list to hear first about future NEOF free bioinformatics courses: https://neof.org.uk/news/""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-01-18 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Liverpool" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "43.590472" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.859513" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2024-01-16 09:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "NEOF - Bacterial 16S metabarcoding" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR UK" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixiruknode.org/activities/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeabT5JvYuAEs7w8y7kG3ox0AKsoHuc9KrnqXoYDlHt3HP6_w/viewform> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/neof-bacterial-16s-metabarcoding-08a9a07d-9476-4fc2-96a3-e8c7a848d0e7> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """This course will introduce participants to a variety of population genomics analyses for large next generation sequencing (NGS) datasets. It will include discussion on single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) calling and filtering options, an introduction to population genomics statistics, examining genetic structure, and Fst-based analyses such as outlier detection/genomic islands of divergence.\r
\r
Places are restricted to UK-based researchers and only 30 places are available so please only register if you intend on attending.\r
\r
The workshop will be held over two days on Tuesday 11th and Thursday 13th March 2025, 10am-4pm, with registration opening 9.30am on Tuesday.\r
\r
The deadline to register is Monday 24/2 noon, 2 weeks before the course. You will hear later that week whether you have been invited and will need to confirm your attendance.\r
\r
Register to NEOF mailing list to hear first about future NEOF free bioinformatics courses: https://neof.org.uk/news/""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-03-13 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NERC" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "43.590472" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.859513" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NEOF" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-03-11 09:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:name "NEOF - Population Genomics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR UK" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixiruknode.org/activities/training> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """At the end of the course learners will be able to:\r
\r
- Describe and understand the steps involved in generating and assessing short-read sequencing data for population genomics studies.\r
- Perform read mapping and SNP calling and filtering.\r
- Examine population structure.\r
- Carry out analyses of genetic differentiation across the genome, and perform Genome Wide Association Studies using the R package GEMMA.\r
""" ;
    schema1:url <https://forms.gle/j88yoSLPafkdEWnR6> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/neof-bacterial-16s-metabarcoding-3797f055-e9e2-499e-a402-07d55f616828> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Sequencing of the 16S rRNA gene is a well established method of determining the bacterial taxonomic composition of microbiomes. This has been used for human and animal body sites, soil, sewage, clouds, deserts, permafrost and many other environments. This course will give you the ability to describe the advantages and disadvantages of 16S rRNA sequencing, and analyse 16S rRNA datasets with the QIIME2 bioinformatics platform.\r
\r
Places are restricted to UK-based researchers and only 30 places are available so please only register if you intend on attending.\r
\r
The workshop will be held over two days on Tuesday 13 and Thursday 15 January 2026, 10am-4pm, with registration opening 9.30am on Tuesday.\r
\r
The deadline to register is Monday 15 December 2025 noon. You will hear later that week whether you have been invited and will need to confirm your attendance.\r
\r
Register to NEOF mailing list to hear first about future NEOF free bioinformatics courses: https://neof.org.uk/news/""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2026-01-15 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NERC" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "43.590472" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.859513" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NEOF" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2026-01-13 09:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:name "NEOF - Bacterial 16S metabarcoding" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR UK" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixiruknode.org/activities/training> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """At the end of the course learners will be able to:\r
\r
- Utilise and understand the use of QIIME2 with 16S rRNA data.\r
- Import sequencing data and metadata into QIIME2 artifacts.\r
- Cluster and denoise 16S sequences with DADA2.\r
- Assign taxonomies to ASVs and produce a phylogenetic tree.\r
- Evaluate and normalise sequencing depth across their samples.\r
- Produce alpha and beta diversity metrics and statistics.\r
- Carry out differential abundance analysis.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSd7FhOC4DyVwFk0vAuV4LEAp4pokB2_TyT4aH4MlhpNn2JfuQ/viewform> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/neof-community-analysis-in-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """A lot of different analysis and visualisations can be carried out with community data. This includes taxonomy and functional abundance tables from 16S rRNA and Shotgun metagenomics analysis. This workshop will introduce you to the phyloseq R object; a specialised object containing an abundance, taxonomy, and metadata table. You will learn how to import your data into a phyloseq object, analyse your data and produce bespoke visualisations in R with the packages qiime2R, phyloseq, and microbiome.\r
\r
Places are restricted to UK-based researchers and only 30 places are available so please only register if you intend on attending.\r
\r
The workshop will be held over two days on Tuesday 16th and Thursday 18th of April, 10am-4pm, with registration opening 9.30am on Tuesday.\r
\r
The deadline to register is Monday 1st April, 2 weeks before the course. You will hear later that week whether you have been invited and will need to confirm your attendance.\r
\r
Register to NEOF mailing list to hear first about future NEOF free bioinformatics courses: https://neof.org.uk/news/""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-04-18 16:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "NERC" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
