<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/nextflow-2019> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Courses@CRG presents the event bringing together the Nextflow community, big data scientists and cloud experts" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:description """\r
**Create. Deploy. Share.** \r
\r
Nextflow is a popular workflow management solution that allows scientists and engineers to create data-driven applications which can be effortlessly scaled across clusters and clouds. It simplifies the writing of complex distributed computational workflows in a portable and replicable manner. Nextflow allows the seamless parallelization and deployment of any existing application with minimal development and maintenance overhead, irrespective of the original programming language. Containerisation technologies and inbuilt executors for the most popular cluster systems (SLURM, PBS, UGE, LSF, etc) and cloud infrastructure (AWS Batch &amp; Google Cloud Platform) ensures unrivalled deployment. The built-in support for code repositories provides code sharing for the collaborations that matter most and enable your applications to touch the world. But most of all, the active community of that has formed around Nextflow provides inspirational, best-in-class technical examples from global leaders in workflow deployment as well as lasting relationships in a growing community.\r
\r
 \r
\r
__Nextflow Training - 17th and 18th September 2019__\r
The week kicks off with a two-day intensive workshop intended for Nextflow beginners and intermediate users. In these practical sessions, participants learn about Nextflow technology starting from basic through to advanced concepts, with the expectation they will acquire the proficiency to develop and deploy their own workflows. The workshop will be provided across 14 topics including processes, operators, executors, containers and best practices. A GitHub repository will be provided with all the necessary material and software as well as AWS cloud instances for running the deployments in the relevant practical sessions. The audience is expected to have at least some prior basic command line experience.\r
\r
 \r
\r
__Nextflow Camp - 19th and 20th September 2019__\r
Nextflow Camp brings together the Nextflow community of developers and users to discuss the state of Nextflow technology, the latest developments and tackle the open questions in a collaborative manner. Each day is split between talks from Nextflow experts and tutorial breakout sessions where participants get a flavour for the possibilities for their own applications.\r
\r
We hope to see you in Barcelona in September!""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2019-09-20 18:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "AWS" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Spain" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Barcelona" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Barcelona" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "08003" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "PRBB Parc de Recerca Biomèdica de Barcelona, 88, Carrer del Doctor Aiguader" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "41.385233" ;
                    schema1:longitude "2.194245" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 50 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Paolo Di Tommaso" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2019-09-17 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Cloud Computing",
        "Containers",
        "HPC",
        "Nextflow",
        "Workflows" ;
    schema1:name "Nextflow 2019" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Centre for Genomic Regulation" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Spain" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Seqera" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.seqera.io> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.crg.eu/en/event/coursescrg-nextflow-2019> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/nextflow-for-beginners-3-day-course> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0605> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Beginner" ] ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """* Applicants should be comfortable working with the CLI (command-line interface) in a Linux-based environment. If you feel uncomfortable with UNIX commands, refresh your knowledge with the UNIX Shell course materials here.\r
* Participants must be able to connect during the course to a remote server via the “SSH” protocol. You can learn how ssh works here.\r
* Instructor will be using VS Code with SSH extension; it is recommended to use the same text editor for ease of comparison.\r
* Participants need to bring their laptop. They will work in their local machine to learn the basics and develop their very first pipeline. They will also work in a dedicated UT HPC environment to learn how to run pipelines in different computation environments.\r
""" ;
    schema1:description """This 3-day Nextflow course by ELIXIR Estonia, in collaboration with the University of Tartu HPC Center, comprehensively introduces the powerful workflow language. Nextflow is renowned for its robust, scalable, and reproducible methods of running computational pipelines. Through efficient, interactive lessons, participants will gain a solid understanding of Nextflow technology, from fundamental to advanced concepts.\r
\r
The course ensures that participants receive frequent hands-on experience while minimising theoretical sessions. By the end of this course, participants are expected to possess the necessary proficiency to develop, use and deploy their own workflows using Nextflow. Additionally, participants will receive an introduction to nf-core pipelines, gaining insights into how these ready-to-use pipelines can be integrated into their workflows for enhanced bioinformatics analysis. This component of the course aims to provide a comprehensive understanding of both Nextflow and nf-core, ensuring participants can effectively utilize these tools in their projects. All essential course materials and software installation guidelines will be available on a GitHub repository. This course will equip participants with the skills to effectively build and execute Nextflow pipelines.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-04-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Estonia" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Tartu" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "51009" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Narva mnt 18, 18 Narva maantee" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "58.385253" ;
                    schema1:longitude "26.724993" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 20 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2024-04-19 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "Command line",
        "Data management",
        "HPC",
        "Nextflow" ;
    schema1:name "Nextflow for beginners - 3-day course" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University of Tartu, Institute of Computer Science" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <http://elixir.ut.ee> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* Develop a Nextflow pipeline from scratch.\r
* Describe and explain Nextflow’s basic concepts.\r
* Implement short blocks of code into a Nextflow pipeline.\r
* Execute/Run a Nextflow pipeline.\r
* Locate and fetch Nextflow pipelines from dedicated repositories.\r
* Run a pipeline in diverse computational environments (local, HPC, cloud).\r
* Get acquainted with nf-core pipelines and their usage.\r
* Share a pipeline.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/news/2024/02/07/Nextflow/> .

