<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/norwich-single-cell-symposium-2021-ea8bb8e5-5253-440f-9572-afaff08e39ff> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Now in its fourth year, the annual Norwich Single-Cell Symposium at Earlham Institute covers single-cell genomics technologies and their application in microbial, plant, animal and human health and disease." ;
    schema1:contact "training@earlham.ac.uk" ;
    schema1:description """*This event has been postponed from 2020 due to the COVID-19 (coronavirus) pandemic. The speakers were confirmed for our 2020 event, and we are hopeful they will be able to attend in 2021.**\r
\r
Single-cell genomic technologies continue to develop at pace, generating new insights into cellular diversity within living systems. This event aims to bring together researchers applying single-cell technologies across a wide range of species.\r
\r
The symposium covers single-cell genomics technologies and their application in microbial, plant, animal and human health and disease. Offering a forum for researchers to discuss the latest developments in single-cell genomics, whilst networking with other researchers with the intention of catalysing future development and application of single-cell genomics across the UK.\r
\r
The event will feature talks from invited speakers and selected abstracts, and we are keen to capture as broad a range of single-cell applications as possible. Topics to be covered include:\r
- Single-cell genomics in plant and microbial research\r
- Single-cell genomics in health, disease and development\r
- Single-cell informatics\r
- Single-cell technology development\r
\r
Abstracts\r
\r
We are offering the opportunity to present your research at the symposium. This will either be as a 15-20 minute talk, or as a poster (you can select your preference during the registration). Submission deadline for abstracts is 1 August 2021.\r
\r
All abstracts must be submitted electronically as part of your registration. Submissions via email will not be accepted\r
Abstract limits are 250 words (excluding title, authors and affiliations)\r
Presenting author should be highlighted in bold\r
Abstracts will be reviewed within 14 days of the submission deadline and you will be informed of the outcome shortly thereafter""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-09-16 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:event_types "meetings_and_conferences" ;
    schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Molecular Machines & Industries (MMI)" ] ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "Earlham Institute" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Norwich" ;
                    schema1:postalCode "NR4 7UZ" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Earlham institute" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "52.628606" ;
            schema1:longitude "1.29227" ] ;
    schema1:name "Norwich Single-Cell Symposium 2021" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Earlham Institute" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-09-15 10:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.earlham.ac.uk/single-cell-symposium-2021> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/norwich-single-cell-symposium-2024> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Now in its seventh year, and extended to a two-day event, the Norwich Single-Cell Symposium at Earlham Institute covers single-cell genomics technologies and their application in microbial, plant, animal and human health and disease." ;
    schema1:description """Single-cell genomic technologies continue to develop at pace, generating new insights into cellular diversity within living systems. \r
\r
This event aims to bring together researchers who are curious about applying single-cell technologies with those who are experts working at the forefront of the field and across a wide range of species. \r
\r
Held in partnership with the UEA School of Biological Sciences, the symposium covers single-cell genomics technologies and their application in microbial, plant, animal and human health and disease. \r
\r
Offering a forum for researchers working on similar challenges in diverse systems to discuss the latest developments in single-cell genomics, while networking with other researchers with the intention of catalysing future development and application of single-cell genomics across the UK. \r
\r
The event will feature talks from keynotes, invited speakers and selected abstracts, and we are keen to capture as broad a range of single-cell applications as possible.\r
\r
Topics to be covered include:\r
\r
Single-cell genomics in plant and microbial research\r
Single-cell genomics in health, disease and development\r
Single-cell informatics\r
Single-cell technology development\r
We will also be opening up doors to Earlham Institute’s single-cell labs, so you will be able to see our technology and capability as well as access our expertise through our researchers.""" ;
