<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/qupath-quantitative-pathology-bioimage-analysis-software-e835b8f0-f010-415f-a625-a939c11b1a42> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "This introductory QuPath course is designed for early-stage researchers—particularly 1st-year PhD students, postdocs, and research support staff—who are new to digital pathology and image analysis. As high-throughput imaging becomes increasingly central to biomedical research, the ability to efficiently analyze whole slide images (fluorescent and histochemical) is essential. This training provides a hands-on introduction to QuPath, a powerful open-source platform for bioimage analysis and specifically large 2D images such as slide scanner images. Participants will learn how to navigate the software, annotate images, perform cell segmentation, classify pixels and objects, and extract quantitative features. The course also introduces basic scripting for batch processing, enabling scalable workflows. By the end of the session, attendees will be equipped to independently analyze their own slide scanner images (histochemical and fluorescent) and integrate image analysis into their research pipelines." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-10-17 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Belgium" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Zwijnaarde" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "9052" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Ghent - VIB/UGent FSVM II, Technologiepark 75" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "51.000392" ;
                    schema1:longitude "3.71677" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "VIB" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-10-17 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "hands-on",
        "imaging",
        "software" ;
    schema1:name "QuPath: quantitative pathology & bioimage analysis software" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "VIB Training" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches "“Apply cell segmentation techniques to detect and quantify cellular structures in whole slide images”",
        "“Classify tissue regions using pixel classification tools based on staining patterns and morphological features”",
        "“Differentiate between object classification strategies and implement them to label biological structures”",
        "“Execute basic QuPath scripts to automate repetitive tasks and batch process multiple images”",
        "“Extract quantitative features from annotated regions to support downstream statistical analysis”",
        "“Identify the core functionalities of QuPath including image management annotation tools and project organization”" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.vib.be/all-trainings/qupath-quantitative-pathology-bioimage-analysis-software-9> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/r-basics> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0605>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Beginners" ] ;
    schema1:description """The aim of the first sessions is to give you the very basic knowledge of R, about the building blocks of R. This should be almost enough for you to be able to start working in R on your own. It should cover the basics that is assumed to be known by help pages and specific tutorials on the internet – I expect you to be able to more or less follow them after the first couple of session of our course.\r
We will then continue towards the modern workflow in R, using some more recent add-ons (or packages, as they are called in R). We will cover data wran- gling, convenient aggregation, filtering, slicing. We will look at data visualisa- tion with grammar of graphics and how to create professional looking reports and articles straight from R1.\r
R will feel different at first if you have never used a scripting language, and it does have a learning curve. We will ascend the steepest part of it during the first sessions. The worse will be over by then and we can continue with stuff that is much more fun, reaching the elegant features of modern R that have made R the language of choice in many fields.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2024-06-11 16:45:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Estonia" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Tallinn" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "12616" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress " Akadeemia tee 3, room SOC-408, Tallinn, Estonia" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "59.396797" ;
                    schema1:longitude "24.66999" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Department of Chemistry and Biotechnology; Tallinn University of Technology" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2024-06-03 09:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Quarto",
        "R",
        "R Studio",
        "Rmarkdown",
        "data visualisation",
        "dplyr",
        "ggplot2" ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "R basics" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.12165576> ] ;
    schema1:name "R basics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Tallinn University of Technology" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <http://elixir.ut.ee> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* work in R and RStudio\r
* write functions in R\r
* understand the concept of grammar of graphics and work with ggplot2\r
* understand the concept of tidy data and convert data between long and wide format\r
* data filtering and summarizing by groups with dplyr\r
* minimum necessary understanding of RMarkdown/Quarto""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee/news/2024/06/03/R_basics_taltech/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/r-for-beginners> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_2269>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307> ;
    schema1:alternateName "Registration closes 03 March 2020" ;
    schema1:description """R is a programming language and free software environment for statistical computing and graphics supported by the R Foundation for Statistical Computing.\r
\r
This course covers:\r
\r
    Day 1: Foundations of R: get familiar with R, R studio, operators, variables, functions, directories and the script editor\r
    Day 2: Visualisation in basic R (boxplots, scatterplots, line graphs and histograms) and ggplot2 (same types of plots and also manipulation of data for use within this package as well as simple linear regression)\r
    Day 3: Introduction to statistical analyses in R: univariate statistics (choosing the right test, checking data assumptions, calculating and extracting the values to report in publications) and Principal Component Analysis (calculation and visualisation with ggplot2)\r
    Extra materials: Introduction to the Tidyverse, Introduction to for loops\r
\r
All materials have been built using relevant life sciences/clinical examples. The course has been designed to introduce R from the very basics. Therefore applicants do not need any prior experience to attend, just the desire to learn R. Real life examples with bioinformatic applications are included.""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2020-04-03 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "United Kingdom" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Liverpool" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Merseyside" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "L69 3GH" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "University of Liverpool" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "53.404782" ;
                    schema1:longitude "-2.965299" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Computational Biology Facility" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2020-04-01 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords " R programming",
        " R-programming",
        " Statistics",
        "Clinical Bioinformatics",
        "biostatistics",
        "life sciences, bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:name "R for beginners" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "University of Liverpool" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR UK" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixiruknode.org/activities/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/computational-biology-facility/events/r-beginners/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/r-for-beginners-82bbe281-71af-4c79-8180-01e7a69e1ac2> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:alternateName "March 2025" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Government" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researcher" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Post Docs" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformatician" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PI" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Statistician" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Industry" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Student" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Academic" ] ;
    schema1:contact "Computational Biology Facility: cbf@liverpool.ac.uk Dr Euan McDonnell: euan2mcd@liverpool.ac.uk Dr Jordan Tzvetkov: jordan.tzvetkov@liverpool.ac.uk John Heap: johnheap@liverpool.ac.uk" ;
    schema1:description """This **intensive 3-day course** introduces the statistical software and **programming language R** with specific focus on its applications in **life sciences and clinical research**. \r
 \r
Each day of the course focuses on a specific topic, as follows: \r
\r
**Day 1. R fundamentals:** An introduction to the language and its interface. Get familiar with R, R studio, operators, variables, functions, directories and the script editor. \r
\r
**Day 2. Data visualisation:** Providing you with the tools to make publication quality plots using R. \r
\r
**Day 3. Statistical analysis in R:** We will cover statistical methods ranging from simple univariate statistics to dealing with large volumes of variables using Principal Component Analysis. \r
\r
The course is designed to be **undertaken at the student’s own pace** with **1 on 1 targeted support** from **our team of experts. Our team comprises computational biologists, data scientists, and bioinformaticians** with a strong background in both wet and dry lab projects. Empowering you on a self-learning path, you will **gain essential skills to apply R to your research projects** and to generate publication-ready outputs. \r
\r
We will also be available to **assist with your learning up to two months after the final day** of the course. Moreover, upon completion of the course, all delegates will be **invited to join a peer-peer support community “RClub”** to enhance their research/analytical work. \r
\r
This course runs twice a year – in **March and in October**. The next cohort will be held online on **11th-13 March**. \r
\r
**Sign up using the [link provided](https://forms.office.com/Pages/ResponsePage.aspx?id=MVElUymxEECG4UdL_X6AdhWSPTMZ0-JPrmodQZMqA-xUNDE0WkIwTEVPRkdDRDM2U1ZNRE1IWE1TSC4u) before the 25th February**. Places are limited and registration may close earlier if places are full. \r
\r
The course has a cost of **£300 for all academic delegates; £500 for all delegates from public institutions (not academic)**. If you are a delegate from the **University of Liverpool you can access student bursaries**, that you can apply to using this link. Note the delegate **bursaries applications close on the 11th February**. You must submit both a registration form and a bursary application to be considered. \r
\r
For more advanced data analysis (e.g. omics) and data science projects, we strongly recommend you also sign up for our R for Data Science course. \r
\r
**Applications close on the 25th February**, and spaces are limited. \r
\r
**To sign up, please use [this form](https://forms.office.com/Pages/ResponsePage.aspx?id=MVElUymxEECG4UdL_X6AdhWSPTMZ0-JPrmodQZMqA-xUNDE0WkIwTEVPRkdDRDM2U1ZNRE1IWE1TSC4u).**""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-03-13 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "University of Liverpool" ;
    schema1:keywords "Coding",
        "Programming",
        "R",
        "R Programming",
        "RStudio",
        "Statistics" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Liverpool" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "53.407199" ;
            schema1:longitude "-2.99168" ] ;
    schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 60 ;
    schema1:name "R for Beginners" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Computational Biology Facility, University of Liverpool" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-03-11 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/computational-biology-facility/events/r-beginners/> .

