<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/training-course-in-computational-methods-for-epitrascriptomics> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Early/mid-career stage researchers with a solid understanding of molecular biology and RNA sequencing, but no prior experience with Nanopore sequencing is required." ] ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "Lectures are intended for early/mid-career stage researchers with a solid understanding of molecular biology and RNA sequencing, but no prior experience with Nanopore sequencing is required. In order to follow and independently carry out the practical exercises participants will need basic bioinformatics skills (e.g. quality check of reads and their alignment against the reference genome) and a certain degree of familiarity using command-line tools in a *Unix environment (e.g. Linux or MacOS)." ;
    schema1:description """Epitranscriptome refers to all chemical modifications affecting cellular RNAs. Although more than 170 different types have been identified up to now, their punctual detection is yet challenging. Profiling epitranscriptome modifications require ad hoc sequencing technologies as well as dedicated computational workflows. While Illumina RNAseq is the de facto sequencing methodology for unveiling RNA editing events (not transient modifications), Nanopore direct RNA Sequencing has recently become one of the most powerful techniques to profile the whole repertoire of RNA modifications. Its capacity to directly sequence full-length, native RNA molecules without the need for retrotranscription or amplification offers remarkable advantages compared to other technologies based on short-read sequencing. However, at the same time it poses various experimental and analytical challenges. This course will provide a foundation on the experimental planning, analytical strategies and computational approaches for applying Nanopore direct RNA Sequencing to profile RNA modifications. Through a combination of theoretical lectures and practical exercises, participants will learn the fundamental concepts and bioinformatics skills that will allow them to start using Nanopore sequencing data for epitranscriptome analysis. Additionally, the course will also cover theoretical aspects of RNA editing as well as practical sessions focused on its detection in human/mouse transcriptomes by Illumina RNAseq data. Participants will acquire specific skills in command-line tools (REDItools) to call, annotate (REDIportal) and filter RNA variants. \r
\r
Speakers\r
Eva Novoa (CRG, ES)\r
Mattia Pelizzola (IIT, IT)\r
\r
Instructors\r
Adriano Fonzino (UNIBA, IT)\r
Claudio Lo Giudice (UNIBA, IT)\r
Alessandro Silvestris (UNIBA, IT)\r
Francesco Nicassio (IIT, IT)\r
Ernesto Picardi (UNIBA, IT)\r
Tommaso Leonardi (IIT, IT)\r
Camilla Ugolini (IIT, IT)\r
Logan Mulroney (IIT, IT)\r
\r
Helpers\r
Pietro D’Addabbo (UNIBA, IT)\r
Chiara Bruno (CNR, IT)\r
\r
""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-04-28 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Italy" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Bari" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Città Metropolitana di Bari" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "70126" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Via Giovanni Amendola, 122o, 122o Via Giovanni Amendola" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "41.112625" ;
                    schema1:longitude "16.883023" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 15 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Ernesto Picardi (UNIBA, IT) Francesca De Leo (CNR-IBIOM, IT) Francesco Nicassio (IIT, IT) Tommaso Leonardi (IIT, IT) Bruno Fosso (UNIBA, IT) Graziano Pesole (UNIBA, IT) Allegra Via (Sapienza, IT)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-04-26 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords " Nanopore sequencing",
        "Epitrascriptomics",
        "RNA sequencing" ;
    schema1:name "Training Course in Computational Methods for Epitrascriptomics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Italy" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """By the end of this course, the participants will be able to:\r
\r
- acquire theoretical knowledge about methods to profile RNA modifications\r
- use bioinformatics tools to analyze Nanopore reads\r
- use bioinformatics tools to pre-process RNAseq data\r
- use command-line tools (REDItools) for calling, annotating (REDIportal), and filtering RNA variants""" ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir-iib-training.github.io/website/2023/04/26/Computational_Methods_for_Epitrascriptomics_Bari.html> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/training-course-on-best-practices-for-rna-seq-data-analysis> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3168>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3308>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3320> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students and young researchers in the life science and computational biology field who are planning to use RNA-seq data and are looking for the best practices to analyze these types of data" ] ;
    schema1:description "Next-generation sequencing (NGS) of RNA libraries (RNA-Seq) has become increasingly common and it largely replaced microarray technology for gene expression profiling. The aim of this course is to get a deeper understanding of RNA-Seq experiments, providing a theoretical introduction to the data processing steps, together with practical sessions illustrating the use of the most popular data analysis tools. The classroom size is limited to 25 participants to optimize the learning and the interaction with the instructors. Starting from the raw sequenced data coming from different phenotypical samples (e.g disease vs healthy control samples), genes which are differentially expressed between the two conditions are determine. Some strategies are illustrated for detecting alternative splicing products, predicting novel isoforms and gene fusion events. Some methods for downstream analysis are described to give insight into how biological knowledge can be generated from RNA-Seq experiments. A lecture on single-cell RNA-Seq will provide an idea of how transcriptome data from individual cells is now emerging as a powerful tool, allowing the study of cell-to-cell gene expression heterogeneity in the same tissue." ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2017-09-29 16:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR Italy" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Italy" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Fisciano" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Provincia di Salerno" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "84084" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Campus di Fisciano, Università degli Studi di Salerno, Via Giovanni Paolo II, n. 132" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "40.774464" ;
                    schema1:longitude "14.789698" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Anna Marabotti (University of Salerno and ELIXIR-IIB Training Team, Italy), Roberto Tagliaferri (University of Salerno, Italy), Alessandro Weisz (University of Salerno, Italy), Loredana Le Pera (ELIXIR-IIB Training Team and IIT, Italy), Allegra Via (ELIXIR-IIB Training Coordinator, CNR-IBPM, Italy) " ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2017-09-27 09:15:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "Data analysis",
        "Gene Expression",
        "High Throughput Sequencing Analysis",
        "NGS",
        "RNA-Seq" ;
    schema1:name "Training Course on Best practices for RNA-Seq data analysis" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Campus di Fisciano, University of Salerno, Italy" ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Italy" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://elixir-iib-training.github.io/website/2017/09/27/RNA-seq-Salerno.html> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/training-course-on-galaxy-for-bioinformatics-tool-developers> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students, computer scientists, bioinformaticians and developers who need to maintain/extend existing tools or develop new tools for Galaxy. " ] ;
    schema1:description "Galaxy is an open source, web-based platform for data intensive biomedical analysis. It is used in a broad range of biomedical research domains to make  accessible bioinformatics applications to users lacking programming skills, and enabling them to easily build analysis workflows for NGS data.  The aim of this course is to make participants familiarize with the Galaxy platform and prepare them to work independently, using best practices guidelines for building, maintaining and installing Galaxy tools.  The course will be delivered using a mixture of lectures and computer based hands-on practical sessions. Lectures will provide an up-to-date overview of the practices for the most successful Galaxy tool development. During this 2-day course participants will learn a modern approach to create, configure and install their own tools into a Galaxy server. " ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2017-07-06 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:funder [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "ELIXIR-IIB" ] ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Italy" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Cagliari" ;
                            schema1:addressRegion "Provincia di Cagliari" ;
                            schema1:postalCode "09134" ;
                            schema1:streetAddress "Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering (DIEE) of University of Cagliari" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "39.228863" ;
                    schema1:longitude "9.11043" ] ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 25 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Allegra Via, Gianmauro Cuccuru, Loredana Le Pera" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2017-07-03 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "Training Course on Galaxy for Bioinformatics tool developers" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
