<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/user-experience-design-for-more-user-friendly-applications> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:contact "Melissa Burke" ;
    schema1:description """Interested in learning how User Experience (UX) design can help you meet your goals? What are the quick wins for knowing your users, designing with your team and getting feedback on your designs? \r
This webinar is for you if are in a role where it is key to understand your user.\r
\r
During this webinar we will introduce you to UX design, show how it can be applied and demonstrate how it can make a positive difference. This is a joint webinar between EMBL-EBI and CORBEL.""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2017-06-27 15:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:keywords "HDRUK" ;
    schema1:name "User experience design for more user-friendly applications" ;
    schema1:startDate "2017-06-27 14:30:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/2017/user-experience-design-more-user-friendly-applications> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/user-experience-design-for-more-user-friendly-applications-c247a31f-a814-4b4c-8d6b-5961bbfdfafa> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/operation_0337>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3572> ;
    schema1:description """Interested in learning how User Experience (UX) design can help you meet your goals? What are the quick wins for knowing your users, designing with your team and getting feedback on your designs? This webinar is for you if you are involved in maintaining or designing a website, tool or app or alternatively, are in a role where it is key to understand your user (e.g. training or capacity building).

User experience design places the user at the centre of development. Our speakers will discuss tools to profile the user (e.g. personas), to create a user journey, and using sketches and prototypes for usability testing.

We will focus on lean UX tools, and will discuss quick wins for knowing your users, designing with your team and getting feedback on your designs.

During this webinar we introduce you to UX design using the [Open Targets platform](https://targetvalidation.org/) as a case study, showing how UX can be applied and demonstrate how it can make a positive difference.

This webinar was given by Michele Ide-Smith and Nikiforos Karamanis and is a joint webinar between EMBL-EBI and [CORBEL](http://www.corbel-project.eu/home.html). 

CORBEL is an initiative of eleven new biological and medical research infrastructures (BMS RIs), which together will create a platform for harmonised user access to biological and medical technologies, biological samples and data services required by cutting-edge biomedical research. CORBEL will boost the efficiency, productivity and impact of European biomedical research. """ ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2017-06-27 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 200 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2017-06-27 00:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:name "User experience design for more user-friendly applications" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/user-experience-design-more-user-friendly-applications> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/using-asap-for-single-cell-analysis-38ac648b-15a9-4977-b8e1-754d2aff1814> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """**This course will take place on consecutive Wednesdays, November 8 and  15, 2023, in the afternoon.**
# Overview
[ASAP](https://asap.epfl.ch/) (Automated Single-cell Analysis Portal) is a web-based, collaborative portal aimed at democratizing single-cell omics data analyses and rendering it more accessible to researchers. ASAP does not require any installation and enables standardized analyses that can be run in minutes by any user without requiring significant computing power. The entire single-cell analysis pipeline is available in ASAP, allowing users to choose from a panel of tools, and guiding them through tutorials.

This 6 h course introduces participants to using ASAP for single-cell analysis, through lectures and hands-on exercises.

# Audience
This course is addressed to students, postdocs, and researchers, from any scientific environment (academia, facilities, companies, etc.) interested in analyzing single-cell data.

# Learning outcomes
At the end of the course, the participants are expected to:
* run a single-cell RNA-seq analysis pipeline using ASAP
* choose the best tools and parameters for each analysis step
* carry out cell-type annotation and downstream analyses


# Prerequisites
##### Knowledge / competencies
This course is designed for beginners. Basic knowledge of single-cell analysis (equivalent to the SIB courses [Single-cell Transcriptomics](https://www.sib.swiss/training/course/20230703_ISCTR) or NGS - Single-cell RNA-Seq Analysis) is required.
##### Technical
This webinar will be streamed, you are thus required to have your own computer with an Internet connection.

# Schedule - CET time zone
**Wednesday November 8, 2023**

15:00-18:00 Introduction to single-cell analysis using ASAP (lecture)

**Wednesday November 15, 2023**

15:00-18:00 Practical exercises

# Application
Attendance is free-of-charge; however, registration is mandatory (use the Apply button below).

You will be informed by email of your registration confirmation.

Applications close on *01/11/2023*. Deadline for free-of-charge cancellation is set to *01/11/2023*. Please note that participation in SIB courses is subject to our [general conditions](https://www.sib.swiss/training/terms-and-conditions).

# Venue and Time
This webinar will be streamed using Zoom. It will start at 15:00 CET and end around 18:00 CET.

All registered participants will receive the information for remotely connecting to the course by email one week before the course.

#  Additional information
Coordination: Monique Zahn, SIB training group.

You are welcome to register to the SIB courses mailing list to be informed of all future courses and workshops, as well as all important deadlines using the form [here](https://lists.sib.swiss/mailman/listinfo/courses).

Please note that participation in SIB courses is subject to our [general conditions](https://www.sib.swiss/training/terms-and-conditions).

SIB abides by the [ELIXIR Code of Conduct](https://elixir-europe.org/events/code-of-conduct). Participants of SIB courses are also required to abide by the same code.

For more information, please contact [training@sib.swiss](mailto://training@sib.swiss).""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-11-15 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Switzerland" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Streamed" ] ] ;
            schema1:organizer <https://ror.org/002n09z45> ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-11-08 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "bart deplancke group",
        "biomarkers",
        "experimental biology",
        "functional genomics",
        "genes and genomes",
        "next generation sequencing",
        "population genomics",
        "single-cell biology",
        "training",
        "transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:name "Using ASAP for Single-Cell Analysis" ;
    schema1:provider <https://ror.org/002n09z45> ;
