<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/using-the-human-protein-atlas-tips-and-tricks> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0108>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_2225> ;
    schema1:alternateName "http://www.proteinatlas.org/" ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Life Science Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """Organizer: Tove Alm, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
This practical workshop will give you knowledge on why and how to use the Human Protein Atlas database (www.proteinatlas.org).\r
\r
The Human Protein Atlas portal is a publicly available database with millions of high-resolution images showing the spatial distribution of proteins in normal human tissues, cancer tissues, and different human cell lines. Antibody-based methods have been used together with transcriptomic analysis to explore the human proteome. The data are publicly available and include application-specific validations for each antibody.\r
\r
During the workshop different areas of the Protein Atlas will be explored, with a focus on the new Cell Atlas launching Dec. 4, 2016. Practical questions will be handed out that are solved by hands-on use of the Human Protein Atlas.\r
\r
Presentations:\r
\r
1:30-1:35 pm – Welcome. Tove Alm, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
1:35-1:55 pm – Introduction to the Human Protein Atlas. Mathias Uhlén, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
1:55-2:15 pm – Spatial localization of proteins in the Tissue Atlas. Cecilia Lindskog, Uppsala University\r
\r
2:15-2:35 pm – All about the Pathology Atlas. Fredrik Pontén, Uppsala University\r
\r
2:35-2:45 pm – Break\r
\r
2:45-3:05 pm – High-resolution: The Cell Atlas. Emma Lundberg, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
3:05-3:25 pm – Antibody validation for bioimaging. Lars Björk, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
3:25-3:45 pm – Working with the downloadable Human Protein Atlas data. Kalle von Feilitzen, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
3:45-3:55 pm – Concluding remarks and future prespective. Mathias Uhlén, KTH Royal Institute of Technology\r
\r
3:55-4:10 pm – Break\r
\r
4:10-5:30 pm – Practical session with General Questions and Answers""" ;
