<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/visualization-of-proteomics-data-using-r> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Educators:\r
Karin Schork, Nils Hoffmann (BioInfra.Prot / LIFS)\r
\r
Date:\r
6th Oct + 7th Oct 2025, 1:00 pm – 5:00 pm, UTC+1\r
\r
Location:\r
Online training\r
\r
Contents:\r
In this course you will learn how to use the ggplot2 package in R to create informative and beautiful figures to communicate your (prote-)omics data and analysis results.\r
\r
We will cover the following topics:\r
- Usage of the tidyverse for data preprocessing\r
- Usage of the ggplot2 R package\r
- Presentation of different types of graphics and when to use them\r
- Customization of graphics\r
\r
We will explain the concepts in general, while the hands-on exercises will be focused on visualizing proteomics data.\r
\r
Learning goals:\r
- Using the ggplot2 R package to create graphics for omics data\r
- Decide which type of graph is appropriate for the given data\r
- Ability to customize the graphics using ggplot2\r
\r
Prerequisites:\r
- Basic understanding of high-dimensional data sets from quantitative proteomics or other life sciences;\r
- Basic knowledge of R (e.g. data import, basic plots. This can for example be gained from our course “Differential analysis of quantitative proteomics data” (16th and 17thSeptember 2025, https://forms.gle/wCpP3kJqjLGMyosZ9).\r
- Computer with stable internet connection, headset and camera\r
\r
Keywords:\r
R; tidyverse; ggplot2; high-throughput data; omics; proteomics; data analysis, graphics, data preprocessing\r
\r
Tools: \r
Download and more information on R here:\r
https://cran.r-project.org/\r
\r
We recommend using an editor such as RStudio, see\r
www.rstudio.com""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-10-07 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Germany" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ;
                    schema1:latitude "50.56912" ;
                    schema1:longitude "10.449144" ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "de.NBI & ELIXIR-DE" ] ;
