<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-a-practical-guide-to-ai-tools-for-life-scientists> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3474> ;
    schema1:contact "training@biocommons.org.au" ;
    schema1:description """The widespread availability and application of AI tools like ChatGPT have fundamentally transformed our approach to work, creativity, learning, and communication. In the realm of scientific research, the impact of AI extends far beyond mere promises, already catalysing significant advances and discoveries.\r
\r
This talk will explore how AI is reshaping scientific exploration and innovation. We explore how AI can accelerate research processes, from data analysis and code writing to hypothesis development. We will present some of the available and emerging AI and how we might effectively leverage these tools while acknowledging their limitations.\r
\r
**Speakers:** Dr Michael Kupier, Principal Research Scientist in Computational Biology and acting Group Leader of the Computational Modelling (CM) group at Data61 of CSIRO. \r
\r
**Date/Time:** 8 May 2024,  12 - 1pm AEST / 11:30am - 12:30 pm ACST / 10 - 11am AWST\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
This webinar is for life scientists and bioinformaticians who are curious about how to use AI tools to accelerate their research. No prior experience of AI or machine learning is required.\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
This webinar is free to join but you must[ register for a place in advance](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_FEGNpYqSTbSHORoEk0ojMQ).\r
\r
A recording of this webinar will be shared on the Australian BioCommons YouTube channel.""" ;
    schema1:eligibility "first_come_first_served" ;
    schema1:endDate "2024-05-08 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:keywords "AI",
        "Artificial Intelligence",
        "ChatGPT" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Australia" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ] ;
    schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 500 ;
    schema1:name "WEBINAR: A practical guide to AI tools for life scientists" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2024-05-08 12:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/ai4lifescientists> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-alphafold-what-s-in-it-for-me> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0082>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_1317>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3474> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "None" ;
    schema1:description """[AlphaFold](https://www.deepmind.com/research/highlighted-research/alphafold) has taken the scientific world by storm with the ability to accurately predict the structure of any protein in minutes using artificial intelligence (AI). From drug discovery to enzymes that degrade plastics, this promises to speed up and fundamentally change the way that protein structures are used in biological research.  \r
\r
Beyond the hype, what does this mean for structural biology as a field (and as a career)?\r
\r
\r
Dr Craig Morton, Drug Discovery Lead at the CSIRO, is an early adopter of AlphaFold and has decades of expertise in protein structure / function, protein modelling, protein – ligand interactions and computational small molecule drug discovery, with particular interest in anti-infective agents for the treatment of bacterial and viral diseases.\r
\r
\r
Craig joins this webinar to share his perspective on the implications of AlphaFold for science and structural biology. He will give an overview of how AlphaFold works, ways to access AlphaFold, and some examples of how it can be used for protein structure/function analysis.\r
\r
\r
**Speaker:**\r
\r
\r
Dr Craig Morton, Principal Research Scientist and Drug Discovery Lead at the CSIRO\r
\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
\r
Researchers, computational biologists and bioinformaticians with an interest in structural biology or applications of deep learning and artificial intelligence to life science research.\r
\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
\r
This webinar takes place online via Zoom Webinar.\r
\r
\r
The webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance \r
**[Register here](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_LwOIIh3UQemKPgcJnfAvzA)**""" ;
