<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-getting-started-with-rnaseq-transforming-raw-reads-into-biological-insights> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3308> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "None" ;
    schema1:description """RNA sequencing (RNAseq) is a powerful technique for investigating gene expression in biological samples. Processing and analysing RNAseq data involves multiple steps to align raw sequence reads to a reference genome, count the number of reads mapped to each gene, and perform statistical analyses to identify differentially expressed genes and functionally annotate them. RNAseq experiments have many different applications as we apply them to a variety of research questions and organisms. This diversity of applications can make it challenging to appreciate all the design considerations, processing requirements, and limitations of RNAseq experiments as they apply to you.\r
\r
In this webinar, you will gain an understanding of the key considerations for designing and performing your own successful experiments with bulk RNA. We’ll start at the lab bench with RNA extraction, quality control, and library preparation, then move to the sequencing machine where you will make essential decisions about sequencing platforms, optimal sequencing depth, and the importance of replicates. We’ll talk about bioinformatics workflows for RNAseq data processing and the computational requirements of transforming raw sequencing reads to analysis-ready count data. Finally, we’ll discuss how to apply differential expression and functional enrichment analyses to gain biological insights from differentially expressed genes.\r
\r
**Speaker:** \r
\r
Dr Nandan Deshpande, Senior Research Bioinformatician, Sydney Informatics Hub\r
\r
\r
**Date/Time:** 06 September 2023,  1 - 2pm AEST/ 12:30 - 1:30 pm ACST / 11 am - 1 pm AWST\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
\r
This webinar is targeted towards those interested in applying RNAseq datasets to their research questions and who wish to gain a deeper understanding of the design considerations, processing requirements and limitations of RNAseq experiments.\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
This webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance.\r
\r
**[Register here](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_Tza2ns06SUqfVQb0QVOmtA)**""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "online" ;
            schema1:endDate "2023-09-06 14:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 1000 ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2023-09-06 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "RNA sequencing",
        "RNAseq",
        "Transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:name "WEBINAR: Getting started with RNAseq: Transforming raw reads into biological insights" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Describe the key considerations for designing and performing successful experiments with bulk RNA\r
- Outline bioinformatics workflows for RNAseq data processing\r
- Outline the computational requirements of transforming raw sequencing reads to analysis-ready count data. \r
- Describe how to apply differential expression and functional enrichment analyses""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/rnaseqwebinar> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-getting-started-with-spatial-omics> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3391> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "None" ;
    schema1:description """Spatial omics provides unprecedented opportunities for the understanding of cells, tissues and systems by combining omics and imaging technologies. This webinar provides a starting point from which to navigate the evolving field of spatial omics and the numerous methods available. We’ll begin with an overview of spatial omics and how it differs from other omics methods. We then explore considerations for analysis of spatial omics data, compare and contrast available tools and delve into some of the theory behind the bioinformatics. \r
\r
**Speakers:** \r
\r
Dr Michael Geaghan, Senior Bioinformatician (Australian BioCommons), Sydney Informatics Hub, University of Sydney.\r
\r
Dr Sarah Williams, Senior Bioinformatician, QCIF Ltd.\r
\r
**Date/Time:** 10 September 2025,  12 \\- 1 pm AEST / 11:30 am  \\- 12:30 pm ACST / 10 \\- 11 am AWST\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
This webinar is for life scientists and bioinformaticians who are curious about spatial omics and how it can be applied in their work.\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
This webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance.\r
\r
[Register here](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_ziitC4cbQg2NzJZT_kpYGQ)""" ;
