    schema1:name "WEBINAR: Global data resources for human genomics and health" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """By the end of the webinar you should be able to:\r
\r
* List some of the human genomics resources available at EMBL-EBI\r
* Outline the features of Ensembl, Federated EGA, DECIPHER and Perturbation Catalogue""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/ebi-human-genomics> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-harmonised-analysis-of-childhood-cancer-data-across-international-borders> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """The development of personalised treatments that target rare paediatric cancer subtypes can be enhanced through global collaboration. But geography and rules to protect personal data in different jurisdictions can make the sharing and comparing of essential data difficult or even impossible.\r
\r
The Australian BioCommons Zero Childhood Cancer Flagship project is a collaborative partnership between ZERO Childhood Cancer, D3b at Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, Seven Bridges (SB), Australian Research Data Commons and the Australian BioCommons to unite and harmonise the analysis of paediatric cancer data sets from Australia (ZERO) and the US (Kids First Data Resource) using the cloud-based Cavatica analysis platform. The project allows ZERO and Kids First workflows and analysis tools to be used interchangeably and seamlessly across both datasets, effectively aggregating the separate datasets into a single virtual pan-continental dataset.\r
\r
This webinar will highlight the outcomes of the project and how the platform can be used to enhance human genomics studies and patient outcomes. Future plans will also be discussed.\r
\r
**Presenters**: Mark Cowley (Children's Cancer Institute), Jack DiGiovanna (Seven Bridges), Allison Heath (Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia)\r
\r
**Date/time**: 21 Jan 2021 - 11:00-12:00 AEDT / 10:00-11:00 AEST / 10:30 - 11:30 ACST / 8:00-9:00 AWST\r
""" ;
    schema1:eligibility "first_come_first_served" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-01-21 12:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:keywords "Cancer",
        "Cloud computing",
        "genomics" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Australia" ;
                    schema1:streetAddress "Australia" ] ;
            schema1:latitude "-25.274398" ;
            schema1:longitude "133.775136" ] ;
    schema1:maximumAttendeeCapacity 500 ;
    schema1:name "WEBINAR: Harmonised analysis of childhood cancer data across international borders" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2021-01-21 11:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/kids-cancer> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-high-performance-bioinformatics-submitting-your-best-ncmas-application> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0769> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:contact "Melissa Burke melissa@biocommons.org.au" ;
    schema1:description """Bioinformaticians are increasingly turning to specialised compute infrastructure and efficient, scalable workflows as their research becomes more data intensive. Australian researchers that require extensive compute resources to process large datasets can apply for access to national high performance computing facilities (e.g. Pawsey and NCI) to power their research through the National Computational Merit Allocation Scheme (NCMAS). NCMAS is a competitive, merit-based scheme and requires applicants to carefully consider how the compute infrastructure and workflows will be applied. \r
\r
This webinar provides life science researchers with insights into what makes a strong NCMAS application, with a focus on the technical assessment, and how to design and present effective and efficient bioinformatic workflows for the various national compute facilities. It will be followed by a short Q&amp;A session.\r
\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for**\r
\r
This webinar is intended for biologists and bioinformaticians who wish to apply for the NCMAS 2022 allocation. Familiarity with HPC is recommended.\r
\r
\r
For an overview of compute options available to Australian researchers and advice on how to match your bioinformatics needs to compute join our other webinar on[ Where to go when your bioinformatics outgrows your compute](https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/compute-resources).\r
\r
\r
**Presenters:** Georgina Samaha and Dr Tracy Chew, Sydney Informatics Hub, University of Sydney\r
\r
\r
**Date/time:** 20 August 2021 - 12:00-13:00 AEST / 11:30 - 12:30 ACST / 10:00-11:00 AWST\r
\r
\r
**How to join:** This webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance.\r
\r
\r
**[Register here](https://unimelb.zoom.us/meeting/register/tZYscuigrDMjEtbGTLGmc3LZooB6zn16vKWv)**\r
""" ;
    schema1:eligibility "first_come_first_served" ;
    schema1:endDate "2021-08-20 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "Australian Biocommons" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "HPC",
        "High performance computing",
        "NCMAS",
        "Workflows" ;
