            schema1:startDate "2024-03-06 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:keywords "Multiomics",
        "Multiomics data integration" ;
    schema1:name "WEBINAR: Multivariate integration of multi-omics data with mixOmics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* Outline the different methods implemented in mixOmics and how statistical data integration is defined in this context. \r
* Outline how these approaches are applied to analysis of different multi-omics studies and the latest methodological developments in this area. \r
* Describe how mixOmics is used to perform variable selection and identify a signature of omics markers that characterise a specific phenotype or disease status. """ ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/mixomics> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-new-era-of-structure-abundance-insights-into-protein-function> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:description """You are invited to join the webinar from the 3D-BioInfo Community\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
				Dr. Joana Pereira, \r
\r
				Biozentrum, University of Basel \r
\r
				(joana.pereira@unibas.ch)\r
			\r
			  \r
		\r
	\r
\r
\r
"Uncovering new families and folds in the natural protein universe"\r
\r
Abstract:\r
	“Thanks to the deep learning revolution in bioinformatics, we are now entering a new era in protein structural biology. There are hundreds of millions of predicted protein structures made available through the AlphaFold database, which cover nearly all proteins that are known. This include not only those already covered by the PDB but also those that are challenging to computationally annotate for function or putative biological role. We examined the extent to which the AlphaFold database has structurally illuminated this “dark matter” of the natural protein universe at high predicted accuracy and described the protein diversity that they cover as an annotated interactive sequence similarity network. By searching for novelties from sequence, structure, and annotation perspectives, we uncovered new novel folds, found a new superfamily of translation-targeting toxin-antitoxin systems, and added multiple protein families to Pfam. Our work underscores the value of large-scale efforts in identifying, annotating, and prioritising novel protein families. By leveraging this revolution, we can now shed light into uncharted areas of the protein universe at an unprecedented scale.”\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
				 \r
\r
				Dr. Manjeet Kumar,\r
\r
				European Molecular Biology Laboratory\r
\r
				(manjeet.kumar@embl.de)\r
			\r
			\r
				\r
		\r
	\r
\r
\r
“Eukaryotic Linear Motif (ELM) resource - updates, annotation challenges, and impact of AlphaFold2 and AlphaFold DB on motif-mediated PPIs”\r
	Abstract:\r
	The talk will cover recent updates on the Eukaryotic Linear Motif (ELM) resource, a hub for exploring the Short Linear Motifs (SLiMs). Other than sharing the latest biological insights from the database, I will also discuss the challenges encountered during the annotation process, including distinguishing functionally relevant motifs and the missing cellular context. The last part of the talk will focus on the impact of AlphaFold2 (AF2)/AlphaFold predicted models (AFDB) in advancing our understanding of motif-mediated protein-protein interactions (PPIs) and their functional relevance.\r
\r
\r
	\r
		\r
			\r
				 \r
\r
				This webinar will be chaired by \r
\r
				Dr. Mihaly Varadi,\r
\r
				European Bioinformatics Institute\r
\r
				(mvaradi@ebi.ac.uk)\r
			\r
			\r
		\r
	\r
\r
\r
All previous webinars from the 3d-BioInfo Community are available here""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2023-10-10 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:name "Webinar: New Era of Structure Abundance - Insights into Protein Function" ;
    schema1:startDate "2023-10-10 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-new-era-structure-abundance-insights-protein-function> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-no-code-no-problem-data-analysis-for-biologists-with-galaxy-australia> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites "None" ;
    schema1:description """Life science research is all about data. Turning that data into useful biological knowledge and publication-worthy figures requires specialised bioinformatics resources and compute. This is where Galaxy comes in.\r
\r
Galaxy is an accessible, online platform for reproducible and transparent data analysis. You don’t need command line skills to use it and it includes 1000’s of popular, community backed bioinformatics tools which is why it has become the go-to platform for thousands of life scientists worldwide.\r
\r
Join us to learn how Galaxy is being used in a wide range of research including genome assembly and annotation, metagenomics, proteomics, transcriptomics, data visualisation and more. We’ll show you the features that make this such a versatile platform whether you are new to data analysis or are looking to take your workflows to the next level\\!\r
\r
[*Galaxy Australia*](https://usegalaxy.org.au/) *is a fully subsidised service provided by Australian BioCommons.*  \r
*The service receives NCRIS funding through Bioplatforms Australia, as well as The University of Melbourne and Queensland Government RICF funding.*\r
\r
**Speakers:** \r
\r
Dr Tiffanie Nelson, Genomics Research Community Engagement Lead, Australian BioCommons\r
\r
Dr Tristan Reynolds, Galaxy Outreach Specialist, The University of Melbourne\r
\r
**Date/Time:** 29 April 2024,  12 \\- 1 pm AEST / 11:30am \\- 12:30 pm ACST / 10 \\- 11 am AWST\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
This webinar is for life scientists and bioinformaticians who need to analyse and interpret biological data. New and experienced data analysts are welcome.\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
This webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance.\r
\r
[**Register here**](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_vTkLCeraQLuzRh4U7PtEYQ)""" ;
