    schema1:url <https://bioexcel.eu/webinar-using-interactive-jupyter-notebooks-and-bioconda-for-fair-and-reproducible-biomolecular-simulation-workflows-2024-05-28/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/webinar-variant-interpretation-from-the-clinic-to-the-lab-and-back-again> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0199>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0622> ;
    schema1:description """The use of genomic testing is increasing rapidly as the cost of genome sequencing decreases. Many areas of the health workforce are upskilling in genomics to help meet the increased demand. From clinicians learning how to use the right test, for the right patient, at the right time, to medical scientists learning how to interpret and classify variants, and data scientists to learning how to better create and continuously refine the pipelines and software to handle and curate big data.\r
\r
In this webinar, we’ll hear from two people working at the coalface of variant interpretation – one in a diagnostic laboratory and the other in a cancer research laboratory.\r
\r
Naomi Baker is Medical Scientist at[ Victorian Clinical Genetics Services](https://www.vcgs.org.au/). She helps process hundreds of genomic tests per year to find the variants that cause rare diseases. She’ll explain the clinical variant interpretation processes she uses, the pipelines, professions and people involved.\r
\r
Joep Vissers is a Curation Team Leader, at the[ University of Melbourne Centre for Cancer Research](https://mdhs.unimelb.edu.au/centre-for-cancer-research), Department of Clinical Pathology. Joep, who also teaches cancer biology at the University, will describe how he uses variant interpretation in his work at the research/clinical interface, and the shift in mindset required when working with data for these different purposes.\r
\r
Amy Nisselle, Genomics Workforce Lead at[ Melbourne Genomics](https://www.melbournegenomics.org.au/), will then briefly outline some of the education programs available in clinical variant interpretation.\r
\r
**Speakers:**\r
\r
Naomi Baker, Medical Scientist, Victorian Clinical Genetics Services\r
\r
Joep Vissers, Curation Team Leader, The University of Melbourne Centre for Cancer Research, Department of Clinical Pathology\r
\r
Amy Nisselle, Genomics Workforce Lead, Melbourne Genomics\r
\r
**Who the webinar is for:**\r
\r
This webinar is for anyone interested in how genomics technologies and workflows are used to inform clinical practice.\r
\r
Whether you’re wanting to get started in translational research, curating big data in a clinical setting or just curious about careers and opportunities in clinical genomics there is something in this webinar for you.\r
\r
**How to join:**\r
\r
This webinar is free to join but you must register for a place in advance.\r
\r
[ Register here](https://unimelb.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_VcXpNbdNSOC8XGqUuyOYlg)\r
\r
This webinar is co-hosted by Australian BioCommons and Melbourne Genomics\r
""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
