<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/workshop-nextflow-for-the-life-sciences> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:coursePrerequisites """- You must be associated with an Australian organisation for your application to be considered\r
- Command line / linux skills are essential. The workshop will be conducted in a Unix environment.\r
- Experience developing reproducible workflows (e.g. bash, CWL, WDL, or Snakemake) \r
- Experience with Github and a Github account """ ;
    schema1:description """The rise of big data has made it essential to be able to analyse and perform experiments on large datasets in a portable and reproducible manner. Nextflow is a popular bioinformatics workflow orchestrator that makes it easy to run data-intensive computational pipelines. It enables scalable and reproducible scientific workflows using software containers on any infrastructure. It allows the adaptation of workflows written in most languages and provides the ability to customise and optimise workflows for different computational environments, types and sizes of data.\r
\r
Learn to build reproducible and scalable scientific workflows with Nextflow in this two-part workshop. Part one covers the fundamental principles of Nextflow pipeline development using the “Hello Nextflow” materials. Part two offers practical, hands-on experience creating a multi-sample Nextflow workflow for RNAseq data preparation.\r
\r
## Format\r
\r
**Lead Trainers:**\r
Fred Jaya, Senior Bioinformatician (Australian BioCommons), Sydney Informatics Hub, University of Sydney.\r
Dr Michael Geaghan, Senior Bioinformatician (Australian BioCommons), Sydney Informatics Hub, University of Sydney.\r
\r
**Date/Time:** 22 - 23 July 2025, 1-5pm AEST/ 12:30-4:30pm ACST / 11-3pm AWST\r
\r
This workshop will take place over **two sessions** via a [distributed training methodology](https://journals.plos.org/ploscompbiol/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pcbi.1008715).   \r
\r
Participants will join in person satellite sites at host universities and research institutes across Australia which will connect via Zoom with lead trainers Fred Jaya and Michael Geaghan.  All sites will be supported by experienced local facilitators, who will guide you through hands-on activities and help you put your new skills into action\r
\r
Satellite sites will be located in central locations in:\r
\r
- Brisbane\r
- Sydney\r
- Perth\r
- Adelaide\r
- Melbourne\r
- Canberra  \r
\r
## Who the workshop is for:\r
\r
This is a workshop for people developing reproducible bioinformatics workflows.\r
\r
## How to apply:\r
\r
This workshop is free but participation is subject to application with selection. During the application process you must choose the satellite site you would like to join.\r
\r
Applications close at **11:59pm AEST, Friday 27 June 2025**.\r
\r
Applications are reviewed by the organising committee and all applicants will be informed of the status of their application (successful, waiting list, unsuccessful). More information on the selection process is provided in our [Advice on applying for Australian BioCommons workshops](https://www.biocommons.org.au/workshop-applications).\r
\r
[Apply here](https://www.eventbrite.com.au/e/workshop-hello-nextflow-tickets-1355997327509?aff=oddtdtcreator).""" ;
    schema1:hasCourseInstance [ a schema1:CourseInstance ;
            dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/CourseInstance/1.0-RELEASE> ;
            schema1:courseMode "onsite" ;
            schema1:endDate "2025-07-23 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
            schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
                    schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                            schema1:addressCountry "Australia" ;
                            schema1:addressLocality "- Brisbane - Sydney - Perth - Adelaide - Melbourne - Canberra  " ] ] ;
            schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
                    schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
            schema1:startDate "2025-07-22 13:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ] ;
    schema1:inLanguage "en" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "Nextflow" ;
    schema1:name "WORKSHOP: Nextflow for the life sciences" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """By the end of the workshop you should be able to:\r
\r
- Describe and use core Nextflow components sufficient to build a simple multi-step workflow \r
- Describe next-step concepts such as operations and channels \r
- Launch a Nextflow workflow locally \r
- Find and interpret outputs and log files generated by Nextflow \r
- Troubleshoot basic issues \r
- Develop a scalable workflow that processes multiple samples and incorporates advanced features\r
- Assess the effectiveness of a workflow in handling multiple samples in parallel execution""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/nextflow-life-sciences> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/workshop-nextflow-on-hpc> a schema1:Event ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Event/0.2-DRAFT-2019_06_14> ;
    schema1:contact "training@biocommons.org.au" ;
    schema1:description """Outgrown your laptop? Learn how to take your bioinformatics pipelines to the next level by scaling them on Australia’s national high performance computing (HPC) clusters.\r
\r
In collaboration with the National Computational Infrastructure (NCI) and the Pawsey Supercomputing Research Centre, this hands-on workshop will equip you with the practical skills to run and optimise efficient Nextflow workflows on HPC systems.\r
\r
Attendees will be guided through a series of hands-on exercises to configure nf-core workflows on NCI Gadi (PBS) and Pawsey Setonix (SLURM), and will iteratively develop a bioinformatics workflow to process larger files, and more samples.\r
\r
**Lead Trainers**:\r
- *Dr Michael Geaghan*, Senior Bioinformatician (Australian BioCommons), Sydney Informatics Hub (SIH), University of Sydney\r
- *Fred Jaya*, Senior Bioinformatician (Australian BioCommons), Sydney Informatics Hub (SIH), University of Sydney\r
\r
**Date/Time**: 1\r
8-19 November 2025, 1 - 4 pm AEDT /12:30 - 3:30 pm ACDT / 11 - 2 pm AWST \r
\r
**Format**:\r
This online workshop will take place over two sessions of three hours each. You must attend both sessions in order to get the most out of the workshop. Expert trainers will introduce new topics and guide you through hands-on activities to help you put your new skills into action.\r
\r
**Who the workshop is for**\r
\r
This workshop is for Australian researchers and technical users (bioinformaticians, life scientists, and data scientists) who:\r
\r
- Have access to HPC systems (e.g., institutional, national, or cloud-based clusters)\r
- Have run simple Nextflow pipelines, typically in a one-sample-per-run or manual scripting context\r
- Are now looking to scale up their workflows, optimise performance, and apply best practices for HPC environments\r
\r
You must be associated with an Australian organisation for your application to be considered. Please note that participants will be supported with access to Gadi and Setonix computing resources for the duration of this workshop. \r
\r
**[Apply here](https://www.eventbrite.com.au/e/nextflow-on-hpc-tickets-1698529981899?aff=oddtdtcreator)**\r
\r
This workshop is free but participation is subject to application with selection. \r
\r
Applications close at **11:59 pm AEDT, Monday 3 November 2025**.\r
\r
Applications will be reviewed by the organising committee and all applicants will be informed of the status of their application (successful, waiting list, unsuccessful). Successful applicants will be provided with a Zoom meeting link closer to the date. More information on the selection process is provided in our [Advice on applying for Australian BioCommons workshops](https://www.biocommons.org.au/workshop-applications).\r
\r
This workshop is presented by [Australian BioCommons](https://www.biocommons.org.au/) with the assistance of a network of facilitators from the [National Bioinformatics Training Cooperative](https://www.biocommons.org.au/training-cooperative). It is underpinned by computational resources provided by NCI and Pawsey Supercomputing Research Centre.\r
\r
This event is part of a series of [bioinformatics training events](https://www.biocommons.org.au/webinars-workshops). If you'd like to hear when registrations open for other events, please subscribe to the [Australian BioCommons newsletter](https://www.biocommons.org.au/subscribe).""" ;
    schema1:endDate "2025-11-19 17:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:hostInstitution "Australian Biocommons" ;
    schema1:keywords "HPC",
        "Nextflow",
        "nf-core",
        "workflows" ;
    schema1:location [ a schema1:Place ;
            schema1:address [ a schema1:PostalAddress ;
                    schema1:addressCountry "Australia" ;
                    schema1:addressLocality "Online" ] ] ;
    schema1:name "WORKSHOP: Nextflow on HPC" ;
    schema1:organizer [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Australian BioCommons" ] ;
    schema1:startDate "2025-11-18 09:00:00 UTC"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:url <https://www.biocommons.org.au/events/nextflow-hpc-workshop-2025> .

