<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/events/workshop-online-data-analysis-for-biologists> a schema1:Course ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/Course/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """**Get a hands-on introduction to Galaxy, an online platform for data analysis**\r
\r
**About this workshop**\r
\r
[Galaxy](https://usegalaxy.org.au/) is an online platform for biological research that allows people to use computational data analysis tools and workflows without the need for programming experience.\r
\r
It is an open source, web-based platform for accessible, reproducible, and transparent computational biomedical research. It also captures run information so that workflows can be saved, repeated and shared efficiently via the web.\r
\r
This interactive beginners workshop will provide an introduction to the Galaxy interface, histories and available tools. The material covered in this workshop is freely available through the [Galaxy Training Network](https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/introduction/tutorials/galaxy-intro-101-everyone/tutorial.html).\r
\r
The workshop will be held via Zoom and involve a combination of presentations by the lead trainer and smaller breakout groups supported by experienced facilitators.\r
\r
**Lead trainer:** Grace Hall, Melbourne Bioinformatics.\r
\r
\r
**Learning objectives**\r
\r
\r
By the end of the workshop you should be able to:\r
\r
1. Work with data on Galaxy\r
    1. Login to a Galaxy server\r
    2. Upload data to a Galaxy server from:\r
        1. A file on your local computer\r
        2. A file on a remote datastore with an accessible URL\r
2. Use tools in Galaxy\r
    3. Access tools via the tool menu\r
    4. Run tools using the tool interface including:\r
        3. Data transformation and filtering\r
        4. Data visualisation\r
    5. View/access tool output\r
3. Identify sources of support and further training\r
\r
**Who the workshop is for**\r
\r
\r
This workshop is suitable for Australian researchers with no prior knowledge of Galaxy and no programming experience.\r
\r
\r
**Date/Time:** 9 September 1:00pm -  4:00pm AEST\r
\r
\r
**How to join**\r
\r
This workshop is free but participation is subject to application with selection. **_Applications close at 5pm AEST Tuesday 31 August 2021._**\r
\r
\r
You must be associated with an Australian organisation and provide an appropriate organisational email address for your application to be considered.\r
\r
Applications will be reviewed by the organising committee and all applicants will be informed of the status of their application (successful, waiting list, unsuccessful) by Friday 3 September 2021. Successful applicants will be provided with a Zoom meeting link closer to the date.\r
\r
**[Apply here](https://galaxy-intro-2021.eventbrite.com.au/)**\r
\r
This [Galaxy Australia](https://usegalaxy.org.au/) workshop is presented by the [Australian BioCommons](https://www.biocommons.org.au/),[Melbourne Bioinformatics](https://www.melbournebioinformatics.org.au/) and [QCIF](https://www.qcif.edu.au/) with the assistance of a national network of facilitators.\r
\r
\r
To hear when registrations open for other events, please [subscribe](https://www.biocommons.org.au/subscribe) to the Australian BioCommons’ eNewsletter.""" ;
