<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-and-evaluating-macromolecular-models-model-quality-assessment> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0081>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_1317> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Clinicians" ] ;
    schema1:description "Understanding the 3D structures of proteins, nucleic acids, and their complexes is key to interpreting their functions. These models, whether from experiments or computational predictions, vary in accuracy and reliability. This tutorial introduces essential tools and metrics to assess macromolecular model quality, a crucial step for research using structural data." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Analysing and evaluating macromolecular models: Model quality assessment" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/courses/analysing-evaluating-macromolecular-models> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-and-visualising-microbiome-derived-datasets-using-the-mgnify-web-api> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """MGnify (formerly EBI Metagenomics) is a freely available hub for the analysis and exploration of metagenomic, metatranscriptomic, amplicon and assembly data. The resource provides rich functional and taxonomic analyses of user-submitted sequences, as well as analysis of publicly available metagenomic datasets drawn from the European Nucleotide Archive (ENA). \r
\r
This webinar will provide an overview of the MGnify API and its use, including how to execute API calls from the browser; where to find documentation; how to use filtering and pagination; available output formats; and scripting examples in Python and R. The webinar will assume a degree of familiarity with the microbiome-derived data in MGnify.\r
\r
This webinar is aimed at individuals who wish to learn more about the MGNify API. Some familiarity with MGNify and programmatic access methods is recommended.\r
\r
This webinar was recorded on 28 November 2018.""" ;
    schema1:identifier "10.6019/TOL.MGnify-w.2018.00001.1" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "MGnify (was EBI Metagenomics)" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/ebi_metagenomics> ] ;
    schema1:name "Analysing and visualising microbiome-derived datasets using the MGnify Web API" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/course/analysing-and-visualising-microbiome-derived-datasets-using-mgnify-web-api> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-bisulfite-methylation-sequencing-data> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3171> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Simon Andrews" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Felix Krueger" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course builds on the core skills introduced in the Introduction to R, Introduction to Unix and Introduction to SeqMonk courses to provide a more in depth look at the analysis of bisulphite sequencing data. The course is a mix of theoretical lectures and hands on practicals which go through the whole analysis pipeline, starting from raw sequence data and covering QC, visualisation, quantitation and differential methylation analysis.\r
\r
**Course Content**\r
\r
* The theoretical basis for BS-Seq\r
* Processing raw sequencing data with Bismark\r
* Visualisation and exploration of methylation calls with SeqMonk\r
* The theory of differential methylation calling\r
* Differential methylation analysis practical\r
\r
**Course Material**\r
\r
* [BS-Seq data processing lecture (pptx)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/BS-Seq%20theory%20and%20QC%20lecture.pptx)\r
* [BS-Seq data processing lecture (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/BS-Seq%20theory%20and%20QC%20lecture.pdf)\r
* [BS-Seq data processing exercises (docx)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/Basic%20BS-Seq%20processing%20Exercises.docx)\r
* [BS-Seq data processing exercises (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/Basic%20BS-Seq%20processing%20Exercises.pdf)\r
* [Visualisation and exploration lecture (pptx)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/Visualising%20and%20Exploring%20Lecture.pptx)\r
* [Visualisation and exploration lecture (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Methylation_Course/Visualising%20and%20Exploring%20Lecture.pdf)\r
