<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-linkage-disequilibrium-with-ensembl> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3053> ;
    schema1:description """The Ensembl project offers integrated genome, variation, gene regulation and comparative genomics data of mainly vertebrate genomes on an open access web browser platform.\r
\r
This webinar will introduce you to the analysis of Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) between variants with Ensembl. We will look at different ways to explore and visualise LD in the Ensembl genome browser website. We will discuss the new LD Calculator tool which has been released with Ensembl 91 and show how to use it.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Analysing linkage disequilibrium with Ensembl" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/analysing-linkage-disequilibrium-ensembl> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-mapped-sequence-data-with-seqmonk> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0182> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Simon Andrews" ] ;
    schema1:description """[SeqMonk](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/projects/seqmonk/) is a program which can analyse large data sets of mapped genomic positions. It is most commonly used to work with data coming from high-throughput sequencing pipelines.\r
\r
The program allows you to view your reads against an annotated genome and to quantitate and filter your data to let you identify regions of interest. It is a friendly way to explore and analysis very large datasets.\r
\r
This course provides an introduction to the main features of SeqMonk and will run through the anlaysis of a couple of different datasets to show what sort of analysis options it provides.\r
\r
**Course content**\r
\r
* What is SeqMonk\r
* Starting and configuring the program\r
* Creating a project and importing data\r
* Using the chromosome viewer\r
* Quantitating and Filtering Data\r
* Creating Reports\r
* Using Quantitation Pipelines\r
* Correcting and Normalising Quantitations\r
* Scaling analysis to larger studies\r
* Running statistical tests\r
* Interacting with external programs\r
* Exporting text and graphics\r
\r
**Course Material**\r
\r
* [Course Manual (PDF)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/SeqMonk_Introduction/SeqMonk%20Course%20Manual.pdf)\r
* [Course Manual (doc)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/SeqMonk_Introduction/SeqMonk%20Course%20Manual.doc)\r
* [Course Exercises (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/SeqMonk_Introduction/SeqMonk%20Exercises.pdf)\r
* [Course Exercises (doc)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/SeqMonk_Introduction/SeqMonk%20Exercises.doc)\r
* [Course Data (zip, 2.6GB)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/SeqMonk_Introduction/SeqMonk_Course_Data.zip)""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Babraham Institute" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/other-nc> ;
    schema1:name "Analysing Mapped Sequence Data with SeqMonk" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Bioinformatics and Biomathematics Training Hub" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.earlham.ac.uk/bbsrc-bioinformatics-and-biomathematics-training-hub> ] ;
    schema1:url <http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training.html#seqmonk> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysing-publications-and-funding-with-the-europe-pmc-rest-api> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """Europe PMC is a database of life science publications and preprints. Articles in Europe PMC are enriched with additional resources, such as author, funding and affiliation information, citations and links to data, peer reviews and more. \r
\r
In this webinar, we will explain how to programmatically access publications and related information using the Europe PMC Articles RESTful API. Michael Parkin, Data Scientist at Europe PMC, will demonstrate how to access the API using the browser and provide some scripting examples in Python/R. He will outline different API methods, rules for filtering and pagination, as well as output formats. Following on, Antonio Campello, Senior Data Scientist at the Wellcome Trust, will explain how Wellcome uses Europe PMC APIs to gain insights into the outputs of the research they fund. He will discuss several use cases, including charting Wellcome’s research portfolio, developing novel technologies to link grants to academic publications, and tracking which grants have been influential to the COVID-19 literature.\r
\r
Additional reading:\r
\r
\r
	Linking academic publications to grants with machine learning\r
	Which research fields have emerged in the last years? A machine learning approach""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Analysing publications and funding with the Europe PMC REST API" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/analysing-publications-and-funding-europe-pmc-rest-api> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/analysis-of-huge-metabolic-models-with-cobrexa-jl-6a5d2934-1460-4b79-a0c6-6957c7994c1a> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_2259>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3524> ;
    schema1:description "COBREXA.jl is a new software package to enable the analysis and simulation of large metabolic models by taking advantages of modern HPC infrastructure. Mainly, it advanced the scalability of constraint-based method, using the Julia programing language and its mathematic ecosystem as a base building block. We will talk about the problems that motivated the development of COBREXA.jl and show several of its main applications. We will show the COBREXA.jl interface that allows biologists and bioinformaticians to easily specify large amounts of analyses and execute it on supercomputers. Later, we will use COBREXA for creating a model of a small microorganism community, and demonstrate how the software helps to answer questions about the viability and chemical activity of microbe ecosystems. About the speakerMiroslav Kratochvíl has recently finished his PhD in computer science at Charles University in Prague; currently he is a researcher focusing on bioinformatics-oriented topics at Luxembourg Centre for Systems Biomedicine. His research concerns mainly the high-performance algorithms for processing large datasets from life sciences." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Analysis of huge metabolic models with COBREXA.jl" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/analysis-huge-metabolic-models-cobrexajl> .

