    schema1:competencyRequired """Participants must have:\r
\r
- an understanding of fundamental genomics and epigenomics concepts\r
- the ability to demonstrate basic laboratory technique, including safe laboratory practices\r
\r
Participants should have:\r
- an understanding of their local permit and safety requirements for processing and handling samples and reagents for HiC.""" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Kristen Schroeder" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Hampus Pehrsson Ternström" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Jill Jaworski" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:dateCreated "2025-06-09"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-08-31"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """This course aims to provide open, sustainable training in laboratory preparation of Hi-C libraries for reference genome sequencing of non-model organisms for BGE partner sequencing centers.\r
\r
There are three course streams available:\r
\r
1. Introductory HiC Technique Course Stream - designed for those without previous experience in the HiC technique, this stream follows a group of researchers performing HiC for the first time.\r
\r
2. Challenging Samples HiC Course Stream - designed for those with some or advanced experience in the HiC technique, this stream follows a group of expert researchers as they troubleshoot and prepare challenging sample types.\r
\r
3. AssemblyLink HiC Course Stream - designed for those with some or advanced experience in the HiC technique, this stream follows a group of experienced researchers as they attempt and troubleshoot the AssemblyLink technique with a variety of sample types.""" ;
