    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Teresa Müller" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Elifsu Filiz" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Anne Fouilloux" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-05-10"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-04-03"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

Welcome to the JupyterGIS collaborative features tutorial. JupyterGIS enables the seamless sharing of notebooks and GIS files, allowing teams—including GIS specialists, data analysts,


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - What features does JupyterGIS have to help me collaborate with others on GIS projects in real time?


## Learning Objectives

- Launch a collaborative session in JupyterGIS.
- Generate shareable links to invite collaborators.
- Work together on GIS files and notebooks with live updates.
- Use follow mode to monitor collaborator activities.
- Add annotations and comments to provide context, ask questions, or share insights.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Climate" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Collaboration with JupyterGIS" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Launch a collaborative session in JupyterGIS.
- Generate shareable links to invite collaborators.
- Work together on GIS files and notebooks with live updates.
- Use follow mode to monitor collaborator activities.
- Add annotations and comments to provide context, ask questions, or share insights.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/climate/tutorials/jupytergis_collaboration/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "4" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/collaborative-lesson-development-training> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Mateusz Kuzak " ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Sarah Stevens" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Toby Hodges " ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Aleksandra Nenadic " ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired """Before joining Collaborative Lesson Development Training, participants should be able to:\r
- write formatted text - bold and italic, headings, links, bullet point and numbered lists - with Markdown.\r
- log into GitHub.com and create and edit files using the GitHub web interface.\r
""" ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:description """This is a training curriculum teaching good practices in lesson design and development, and open source collaboration skills, using The Carpentries Workbench. The curriculum was designed to be taught over three full days or six half-days. The target audience is Carpentries Instructors with an idea for a new lesson they would like to create, especially if that lesson is intended for short-format training (e.g. part or all of a two-day workshop).\r
\r
Lesson development is easier and more successful when it is a joint effort among collaborators, so the activities and examples used in this training are best suited to groups of trainees who want to collaborate on a lesson project. Efforts have been made to also cater to lesson developers working alone.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Training materials" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-2.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Collaborative Lesson Development Training" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "The Carpentries" ;
            schema1:url <https://carpentries.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """After attending this training, participants will be able to:\r
- collaboratively develop and publish lessons using The Carpentries lesson infrastructure (aka The Carpentries Workbench): lesson template, GitHub, GitHub Pages, etc.\r
- identify and characterise the target audience for a lesson.\r
- define SMART learning objectives.\r
- explain the pedagogical value of authentic tasks.\r
- create exercises for formative assessment.\r
- explain how considerations of cognitive load can influence the pacing, length, and organisation of a lesson.\r
- configure and maintain accessible and usable lesson repositories using best practices, readily available for collaboration.\r
- identify and correct accessibility issues in a Carpentries lesson.\r
- update and improve lesson material guided by feedback and reflection from teaching.\r
- review and provide constructive feedback on lessons.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://carpentries.github.io/lesson-development-training/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/combinations> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "You are already familiar with calculating permutation (\"How many ways could 7 different people sit in 4 different seats?\").  But what if you didn't care about which seat they sat in?  What if you just cared about which 4 people were in the car?  Or put another way, you want to know how many combinations of 4 people can you stick in the car from a pool of 7 candidates.  Or how many ways are there to choose 4 things from a pool of 7?  Look no further than this tutorial to answer your questions." ;
    schema1:keywords "Probability and combinatorics" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Combinations" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Khan Academy Statistics" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.khanacademy.org/math/probability> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://api.khanacademy.org/math/probability/probability-and-combinatorics-topic/combinations-combinatorics> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/community-analysis-of-amplicon-sequencing-data-16s-rrna> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Anu Mikkonen" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Eija Korpelainen" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Lehtivaara" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Jarno Tuimala" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course introduces community analysis of amplicon sequencing data (16S rRNA). It covers preprocessing, taxonomic classification, and statistical analysis for marker gene studies. The user-friendly Chipster software is used in the exercises, so no Unix or R experience is required and the course is thus suitable for everybody. The course takes one day. You will learn how to\r
\r
  * check the quality of reads with FastQC and PRINSEQ\r
  * remove bad quality data with Trimmomatic, PRINSEQ and Mothur\r
  * taxonomic classification with Mothur\r
  * statistical analysis for marker gene studies based on R packages vegan, rich, biodiversityR, pegas and labdsv\r
\r
Course material (2017) is available at [the course website](https://chipster.csc.fi/manual/courses.html#16S) and it includes\r
\r
  * slides\r
      1. Introduction to community analysis by Anu Mikkonen\r
      2. Community analysis with Chipster\r
      3. Statistical analysis of marker gene data by Jarno Tuimala\r
  * lecture videos\r
  * exercises (the data is available on Chipster server in the example session listed in the exercise sheet, and we also provide ready-made analysis sessions which you can use as a reference when doing exercises on your own.)""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Video",
        "course materials" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Chipster" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/chipster> ] ;
    schema1:name "Community analysis of amplicon sequencing data (16S rRNA)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "CSC - IT Center for Science" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.csc.fi> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Finland" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.csc.fi/web/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://chipster.csc.fi/manual/courses.html#16S> .

