<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0092> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Postdoctoral Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate Students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researcher" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Diana Pilvar" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:dateCreated "2023-11-09"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:dateModified "2024-04-10"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2023-11-10"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """In the course of their work, researchers encounter data in various forms. Yet, when data is presented in its raw, tabular form, it often appears as a complex puzzle. This can be especially overwhelming when dealing with large datasets. Fortunately, this is where the magic of data visualization comes into play. The main idea is to extract the key insights from the data and craft a narrative around it. However, it's important to note that hasty chart-making can lead to confusion. Poorly designed visuals risk misinterpretation and can be misleading. That's why it's crucial to approach data visualization thoughtfully and precisely.\r
\r
This webinar will discuss why data visualization is important and what we should consider when designing it. This means what chart type to pick, how much information to include, what (and how) to highlight, etc. The webinar will have a theoretical aspect, and we will assess real-life examples.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:identifier "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7819286" ;
    schema1:keywords "Data Visualization",
        "Data visualisation" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "lessons" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Data Visualisation" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Estonia" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixir.ut.ee> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """* Choose appropriate chart types\r
* Avoid common mistakes in data visualization\r
* Maximise data-ink ratio\r
""" ;
    schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7819286> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation-101-a-practical-introduction-to-designing-scientific-figures> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """In this webinar we will go through the basic principles of designing scientific figures to communicate our research findings. We will focus on how we can utilise colour effectively and help our audience make accurate quantitative judgements by taking advantage of fundamental principles of human perception. We will also discuss strategies for visualising complex datasets.\r
\r
The webinar is based on the articles in data visualisation which were published in the Points of View column in Nature Methods with additional material from publications by Edward Tufte, Tamara Munzner and other leading researchers on data visualisation.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Data visualisation 101: A practical introduction to designing scientific figures" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/data-visualisation-101-practical-introduction-designing-scientific-figures> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation-at-pdbe> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_2814> ;
    schema1:description """This webinar will introduce some of the data visualisations available from PDBe, how to add these to your own website and how to customise them for your own requirements. There will also be specific case studies that demonstrate how these visualisations can be used and implemented.\r
\r
This webinar is part of a 6-part PDBe API webinar series, introducing different levels of programmatic access at PDBe.The series will range from basic data retrieval and search using the PDBe API to more advanced features, including access and reuse of PDBe data visualisation components.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Data visualisation at PDBe" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/data-visualisation-pdbe> .

