<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation-olympics-visualization-in-r> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3372> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "The Carpentries" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helena Rasche" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2024-05-07"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2023-06-16"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

In this tutorial, you will learn how to produce scatter plots, boxplots, and time series plots using ggplot. You will also learn how to set universal plot settings, modify the aesthetics of an existing ggplot plots (including axis labels and color), and learn how to facet in ggplot.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How does plotting work in R?
 - How can I facet plots?
 - How do I produce a nice, publication ready plot with ggplot2?


## Learning Objectives

- Produce scatter plots, boxplots, and time series plots using ggplot.
- Set universal plot settings.
- Describe what faceting is and apply faceting in ggplot.
- Modify the aesthetics of an existing ggplot plot (including axis labels and color).
- Build complex and customized plots from data in a data frame.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Foundations of Data Science",
        "R",
        "cyoa",
        "jupyter-notebook",
        "rmarkdown-notebook" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "RStudio" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/rstudio> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Quarto/RMarkdown Notebook" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/data-manipulation-olympics-viz-r/data-science-data-manipulation-olympics-viz-r.Rmd> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/record/6803028> ] ;
    schema1:name "Data visualisation Olympics - Visualization in R" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Produce scatter plots, boxplots, and time series plots using ggplot.
- Set universal plot settings.
- Describe what faceting is and apply faceting in ggplot.
- Modify the aesthetics of an existing ggplot plot (including axis labels and color).
- Build complex and customized plots from data in a data frame.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/data-manipulation-olympics-viz-r/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "3" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation-principles-for-enhancing-effective-scientific-communication> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0092> ;
    schema1:description "This webinar will provide an introduction to data visualisation principles. It includes a review of basic concepts and showcases tools to improve your understanding and use of data visualisation for improved accessibility to your research. As we know, collecting data is just the first step to advancing scientific knowledge. Data presentation techniques are the key to communicating your findings in a concise manner to improve understanding across audiences and ultimately strengthening your contributions to your field.The webinar speaker, Isabel Romero Calvo, PhD, MS, Scientific Illustrator, will present information that will help you gain insights into:Gestalt principlesConcept of salience and graphic perceptionUse of colour in data visualisationAccessibility in data visualisationWe will conclude with a Q&amp;A session for the webinar attendees. " ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Data visualisation principles for enhancing effective scientific communication" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/data-visualisation-principles-enhancing-effective-scientific-communication> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/data-visualisation-with-r-and-ggplot2> a schema1:LearningResource ;
