<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/effective-literature-research-with-europe-pmc> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Scientists who want to optimise their literature searches" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Levchenko" ] ;
    schema1:description """Following the latest developments in your scientific field requires thorough and frequent literature research. In this webinar, we will show how to find and access relevant scientific literature using Europe PMC, an open biomedical literature database, part of PubMed Central International. We will cover searches by topic and author, searches for associated research data, as well as advanced options for building complex queries.\r
\r
This webinar was recorded on 30 May 2018. It is best viewed in full-screen mode using Google Chrome. The slides from this webinar can be downloaded here.\r
\r
To learn more about searching grant information and on managing, exporting and saving searches you can watch our previous webinar Europe PMC: get the most from literature searches. You will find more information about Europe PMCs API service in our webinar Europe PMC, programmatically. You can also try our Europe PMC: Quick Tour. \r
\r
See the EMBL-EBI training pages for a list of upcoming webinars.\r
\r
This webinar is aimed at scientists who want to optimise their literature searches. No prior knowledge of bioinformatics is required, but an undergraduate-level understanding of biology would be useful.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Biomedical Literature",
        "Europe PMC",
        "Literature search" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Effective Literature Research with Europe PMC" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/course/effective-literature-research-europe-pmc> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/ega-submitter-portal-tutorial> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """The *[European Genome-phenome Archive (EGA)](https://ega-archive.org/)* is a service for permanent and secure archiving and sharing of all types of personally identifiable genetic and phenotypic data resulting from biomedical research projects.\r
\r
The EGA accepts only de-identified data with a DAC approved access plan. The accepted data types include raw data formats from the array-based and new sequencing platforms as well as phenotype files describing study samples. A range of tools is offered for the file and metadata upload. EgaCryptor and Crypt4GH to encrypt your data, FTP and Aspera to upload it, and the *EGA Submitter Portal* for the metadata submission.\r
\r
**About this tutorial**\r
\r
The Submitter Portal provides the tools to facilitate the metadata submission of human data to the European Genome-phenome Archive. The aim of this tutorial is to show how to use the new EGA Submitter Portal, that is soon to be released. The portal will include new features such as adding collaborators (similar to data brokering), the possibility to link your data to external repositories, and the "on-the-fly" encryption of your files, among others.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "ELIXIR Converge",
        "FEGA",
        "Federated EGA",
        "Genomics" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Video" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "EGA Submitter Portal Tutorial" ;
            schema1:url <https://youtu.be/o81GDFwXiGE> ] ;
    schema1:name "EGA Submitter Portal Tutorial" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Centre for Genomic Regulation (CRG)" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.crg.eu> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://veis.bsc.es/en/events/ega-submitter-portal-tutorial/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/ejp-rd-mooc-diagnosing-rare-diseases-from-the-clinic-to-research-and-back> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3325> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType " rare disease patients representatives" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Patient Advocates " ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "healthcare professionals" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "postdoctoral researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """Topics covered:\r
- The diagnostic process and the types of genetic tests available for rare diseases\r
- The differences in rare genetic diseases patient pathways\r
- Technological advances for diagnostic research\r
- The role of collaborative studies and data sharing in rare diseases diagnosis\r
- The impact of having a diagnosis or lacking a diagnosis on patients’ lives\r
- The role and place of physiopathology approaches as well as social sciences research in the context of rare diseases diagnosis.\r
\r
Learners will gain insight into patients’ experiences, will discuss key issues relating to this topic and will also have the possibility to undertake their own internet-based investigations.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Rare Diseases & Research" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "EJP RD MOOC \"Diagnosing Rare Diseases: from the Clinic to Research and back\"" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Future Learn" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.futurelearn.com/courses/collections/genomics> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.futurelearn.com/courses/rare-genetic-disease> .

