<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/eurordis-online-trainning-modules> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3325> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biomedical researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Patient Advocates " ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Life Science Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """Through the Open Academy, EURORDIS empowers patient advocates to have the confidence and knowledge needed to bring their expertise to discussions on health care, research and medicines development. EURORDIS alumni engage in these discussions as equal partners, side by side with policy makers, industry and scientists.\r
The EURORDIS Open Academy currently encompasses the Winter School on Scientific Innovation and Translational Research, the Summer School on Medicines Research and Development, the Leadership School and the Digital School. The programmes of the EURORDIS Open Academy are taught in English, through a blended approach with online trainings (webinars and e-learning courses) and face-to-face components.\r
Although the EURORDIS Open Academy mainly targets rare disease patient advocates, up to 10 researchers attend the EURORDIS Summer School on Medicines Research &amp; Development every year. \r
Over 50 online courses are alsomade freely available via the Open Academy Website (https://openacademy.eurordis.org) to anyone interested in building their capacities independently.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Rare Diseases",
        "Rare Diseases & Research" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "course materials",
        "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Eurordis Open Academy" ;
    schema1:url <https://openacademy.eurordis.org> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/evaluating-reference-data-for-bulk-rna-deconvolution> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Morgan Howells" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Bulk RNA Deconvolution with MuSiC",
        "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Wendi Bacon" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Carlos Chee Mendonça" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Morgan Howells" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-03-11"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-02-02"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

There are various methods to estimate the proportions of cell types in bulk RNA data. Since the actual cell proportions of the data are unknown, how do we know if our tools are producing accurate results?\r


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How do I evaluate my reference data?
 - How do I compare different deconvolution tools?
 - What are the best metrics for determining tool accuracy?


## Learning Objectives

- Generate psuedo-bulk data from single-cell RNA data
- Process the single-cell and psuedo-bulk data using various deconvolution tools
- Evaluate and visualse the results of the different deconvolution methods""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Single Cell",
        "transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "annotatemyids" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/annotatemyids> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "MuSiC suite: PoPMuSiC, HoTMuSiC, and SNPMuSiC" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/popmusic> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/single-cell/tutorials/bulk-deconvolution-evaluate/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/records/5719228> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "ggplot2" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/ggplot2> ] ;
    schema1:name "Evaluating Reference Data for Bulk RNA Deconvolution" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Generate psuedo-bulk data from single-cell RNA data
- Process the single-cell and psuedo-bulk data using various deconvolution tools
- Evaluate and visualse the results of the different deconvolution methods""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/single-cell/tutorials/bulk-deconvolution-evaluate/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "3" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/evaluating-variant-effects-in-human-proteins-with-protvar> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_1775>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3120> ;
    schema1:description "ProtVar integrates human variant annotations and predictions to uncover the functional effects of missense variation in humans. Learn how our diverse data and intuitive visualisations can help you to contextualise your variant list or browse and prioritise variants from our expansive database.Genomic variation can perturb normal biological processes through diverse and context-dependent mechanisms, necessitating the integration of multi-dimensional datasets to accurately interpret their effects. Protein coding variation, in particular, offers unique opportunities beyond clinical diagnostics, such as the identification of therapeutic targets and engineering applications in synthetic biology. However, it also introduces significant interpretative complexity, contributing to the high proportion of missense variants that remain of uncertain significance.ProtVar is a human variant annotation and assessment web tool developed at EMBL-EBI, designed to offer users exceptional flexibility, accessibility, and seamless integration. It aggregates data and analyses from a wide range of resources to provide comprehensive genomic, protein sequence, functional, and structural insights, facilitating a deeper understanding of the potential impact of missense variation in humans. This webinar will provide practical guidance on how to explore and interact with ProtVar’s diverse annotations and predictive features to contextualise human missense variants. It will cover the types of annotations available, the visualisations offered, and the various methods for retrieving and exporting data." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Evaluating variant effects in human proteins with ProtVar" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/evaluating-variant-effects-human-proteins-protvar> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/evening-session-efficient-r> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Martin Morgan" ] ;
    schema1:description """Bioconductor provides tools for the analysis and comprehension of high-throughput genomic data.
                    Bioconductor uses the R statistical programming language, and is open source and open development. 
                    It has two releases each year, 1560 software packages, and an active user community. 
                    Bioconductor is also available as an AMI (Amazon Machine Image) and a series of Docker images.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Big data" ;
