<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/fair-principles-in-practice-for-health-data> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3063>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_4012> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biomedical Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Data Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Research Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Data Managers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Vasundra Touré" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Personalized Health Informatics Group" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Sabine Österle" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Janina Müller" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:description """FAIR principles in practice for health data was developed in the context of the [Swiss Personalized Health Network (SPHN)](https://sphn.ch) initiative and is part of a series of trainings centered around the [SPHN Interoperability Framework](https://sphn.ch/network/data-coordination-center/the-sphn-semantic-interoperability-framework/) developed by the SPHN Data Coordination Center (DCC). The framework aims at facilitating collaborative research by providing a decentralized infrastructure sustained by a strong semantic layer (SPHN Dataset) and graph technology, based on RDF, for the exchange and storage of data.\r
\r
The FAIR principles have been developed to enable a better data management and stewardship in research by [Wilkinson et. al.](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26978244/) in 2016. They consist of a list of necessary criteria for making data Findable, Accessible, Interoperable and Reusable. However, the understanding of these principles and their concrete implementation can sometimes be abstract and difficult.\r
\r
In this training we offer a detailed example of the implementation of FAIR principles, demonstrating how the different principles and criteria have been applied to the various components of the SPHN framework.\r
\r
__Prerequisites:__\r
\r
None\r
\r
__After the training you will be able to understand:__\r
\r
- why FAIR data does not necessarily mean “open data”;\r
- that there is not just one interpretation for each FAIR criterion, and they can be fulfilled in different ways;\r
- why FAIR is important in all stages of a project and not only for the data reuse;\r
- that data is not only either “FAIR” or “not FAIR”, but there are different levels in between.\r
\r
__Resources:__\r
\r
All resources are available on the [training's GitLab space](https://git.dcc.sib.swiss/sphn-semantic-framework/sphn-training-material/-/tree/master/FAIR%20principles%20in%20practice%20for%20health%20data)""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Accessibility",
        "Clinical data",
        "FAIR",
        "Findability",
        "Interoperability",
        "Knowledge graph",
        "RDF",
        "Reusability",
        "Semantic framework" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "E-learning",
        "Video" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-SA-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "FAIR principles in practice for health data" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Switzerland" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.sib.swiss/training/> ],
        <https://ror.org/002n09z45> ;
    schema1:url <https://sphn.ch/training/fair-principles-in-practice-for-health-data/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/fair-software-tools> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3372> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Marta Lloret Llinares" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Carlos Martinez Ortiz" ] ;
    schema1:description """In this talk, I will discuss the importance of the FAIR principles for the software tools we use to process data. Ranging from small analysis scripts to full fledged data processing pipelines, software needs to be FAIR to enable other researchers to reproduce our own experiments and reuse our software. However software and data are fundamentally different – software is executable in nature and may have intricate dependencies. FAIR principles apply differently to software than they do to data and we must be aware of these differences. Existing initiatives such as the RDA FAIR for Research Software (FAIR4RS) working group (https://www.rd-alliance.org/groups/fa...​) and http://fair-software.eu/​ are already focused on addressing these differences and raising awareness of the importance of FAIR for software.\r
\r
The “How FAIR are you” webinar series and hackathon aim at increasing and facilitating the uptake of FAIR approaches into software, training materials and cohort data, to facilitate responsible and ethical data and resource sharing and implementation of federated applications for data analysis.\r
\r
The CINECA webinar series aims to discuss ways to address common challenges and share best practices in the field of cohort data analysis, as well as distribute CINECA project results. All CINECA webinars include an audience Q&amp;A session during which attendees can ask questions and make suggestions. Please note that all webinars are recorded and available for posterior viewing.\r
\r
This webinar took place on 24th February 2021 and is part of the CINECA webinar series. It is best viewed in full screen mode using Google Chrome.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "FAIR",
        "Software",
        "software tools" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Video" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "FAIR software tools" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "CINECA PROJECT" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.cineca-project.eu/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VEvHBrJlE6M> .

