<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/genomic-data-science-and-clustering-bioinformatics-v> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """How do we infer which genes orchestrate various processes in the cell?  How did humans migrate out of Africa and spread around the world? In this class, we will see that these two seemingly different questions can be addressed using similar algorithmic and machine learning techniques arising from the general problem of dividing data points into distinct clusters.

In the first half of the course, we will introduce algorithms for clustering a group of objects into a collection of clusters based on their similarity, a classic problem in data science, and see how these algorithms can be applied to gene expression data.

In the second half of the course, we will introduce another classic tool in data science called principal components analysis that can be used to preprocess multidimensional data before clustering in an effort to greatly reduce the number dimensions without losing much of the "signal" in the data.

Finally, you will learn how to apply popular bioinformatics software tools to solve a real problem in clustering.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "algorithms",
        "computer-science",
        "health-informatics",
        "life-sciences" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Genomic Data Science and Clustering (Bioinformatics V)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Coursera" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.coursera.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.coursera.org/learn/genomic-data> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/genomic-features-that-regulate-gene-expression-with-ensembl> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3173>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3295> ;
    schema1:description """Gene expression is controlled by a complicated network of transcription factors and repressors, interacting with genomic features such as promoters and enhancers, dependant on epigenetic marks at those loci. Ensembl provides access to epigenomic data on histone modifications and protein binding across the genome, integrated with data on genes and genetic variation.\r
\r
Join Ensembl to investigate a genomic region which controls gene expression, determine its activity in different cell types and view the epigenomic markers at that locus, plus see how to access these data in bulk.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Genomic features that regulate gene expression, with Ensembl" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/genomic-features-regulate-gene-expression-ensembl> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/genomic-ranges-slides> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Sonali Arora" ] ;
    schema1:description """Bioconductor provides tools for the analysis and comprehension of high-throughput genomic data.
                    Bioconductor uses the R statistical programming language, and is open source and open development. 
                    It has two releases each year, 1560 software packages, and an active user community. 
                    Bioconductor is also available as an AMI (Amazon Machine Image) and a series of Docker images.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Data Representation" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Bioconductor" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/bioconductor> ] ;
    schema1:name "Working with DNA Sequences" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Bioconductor" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.bioconductor.org/help/course-materials/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://bioconductor.org/help/course-materials/2014/SeattleFeb2014/Bioconductor_sequences.pdf> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/genomic-technologies-in-clinical-diagnostics-molecular-techniques> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3325> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "healthcare professionals" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course focuses on molecular techniques. By the end, you will be able to demonstrate knowledge and applicability of the molecular principles behind:\r
- array comparative genomic hybridisation (array CGH);\r
- karyotyping;\r
- fluorescent in situ hybridisation (FISH);\r
- Southern blotting;\r
- multiplex ligation probe amplification (MLPA);\r
- polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and Sanger sequencing;\r
- quantitative fluorescent PCR (QF-PCR);\r
- single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) genotyping and genome wide association studies (GWAS);\r
- and the extraction and analysis of cell free fetal DNA, including non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT).""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "advanced" ;
    schema1:keywords "Rare Diseases & Research" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Genomic Technologies in Clinical Diagnostics: Molecular Techniques" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.futurelearn.com/courses/molecular-techniques> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/genomic-technologies-in-clinical-diagnostics-next-generation-sequencing> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3325> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Scientists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Healthcare" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "postgrad" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course focuses on genomic sequencing.\r
Topics covered:\r
- The changing landscape of genomics: From Sanger sequencing to Next Generation Sequencing\r
- Overview of Next Generation Sequencing platforms and their methodology\r
- Targeted resequencing\r
- Alignment, variant calling and annotation\r
- Other applications of Next Generation Sequencing beyond DNA sequencing\r
- Next Generation Sequencing in gene discovery\r
- Gene discovery in the research and diagnostics\r
- Next Generation Sequencing in clinical diagnostics: Single gene, gene panel sequencing, exome and genome sequencing\r
- How to design a gene panel\r
- The 100 000 Genomes project""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "advanced" ;
    schema1:keywords "Rare Diseases & Research" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Genomic Technologies in Clinical Diagnostics: Next Generation Sequencing" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.futurelearn.com/courses/next-generation-sequencing> .

