    schema1:description """## Abstract

We stumbled upon a paper called *"The histone acetyltransferase MOF is a key regulator of the embryonic stem cell core transcriptional network"*. The paper contains the analysis of possible target genes of an interesting protein called Mof. The targets were obtained by ChIP-seq in mice and the raw data is available through [GEO](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE37268).


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How to use Galaxy?
 - How to get from peak regions to a list of gene names?


## Learning Objectives

- Familiarize yourself with the basics of Galaxy
- Learn how to obtain data from external sources
- Learn how to run tools
- Learn how histories work
- Learn how to create a workflow
- Learn how to share your work""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
