    schema1:description """## Abstract

Try/except are a construct in Python used to catch a potential exception. Sometimes things go wrong in your code! Or in someone else's code in a module. Sometimes some errors might be expected like when you try and read a user supplied file, maybe it isn't available because they've specified the wrong path.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How do I try to execute code, knowing it might fail?
 - What are some situations where this is important?
 - How can I write my own exceptions.


## Learning Objectives

- catch an exception
- raise your own exception""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Foundations of Data Science",
        "jupyter-notebook" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Jupyter Notebook (with Solutions)" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/python-exceptions/data-science-python-exceptions.ipynb> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Jupyter Notebook (without Solutions)" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/python-exceptions/data-science-python-exceptions-course.ipynb> ] ;
    schema1:name "Python - Try & Except" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- catch an exception
- raise your own exception""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/python-exceptions/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "3" .

