    schema1:description """## Abstract

In this tutorial we are working with files from a long-term evolution study of an *E. coli* population (designated Ara-3). We will perform variant calling to see how the population changed over time. We care how this population changed relative to the original population, *E. coli* strain REL606. Therefore, we will align each of our samples to the *E. coli* REL606 reference genome, and see what differences exist in our reads versus the genome.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How do I find sequence variants between my sample and a reference genome?


## Learning Objectives

- Understand the steps involved in variant calling.
- Describe the types of data formats encountered during variant calling.
- Use command line tools to perform variant calling.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Foundations of Data Science" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Variant Calling Workflow" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Understand the steps involved in variant calling.
- Describe the types of data formats encountered during variant calling.
- Use command line tools to perform variant calling.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/data-science/tutorials/bash-variant-calling/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "7" .

