        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2024-10-15"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2020-01-10"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

Circos is a software package for visualizing data in a circular layout. This makes Circos ideal for exploring relationships between objects or positions. Circos plots have appeared in thousands of scientific publications. Although originally designed for visualizing genomic data, it can create figures from data in any field. In this tutorial we discuss the Galactic Circos tool which enables you to access Circos via the convenient Galaxy interface, and even produce plots as part of your workflows!


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - What can the Circos Galaxy tool be used for?
 - How can I visualise common genomic datasets using Circos?


## Learning Objectives

- Create a number of Circos plots using the Galaxy tool
- Familiarise yourself with the various different track types""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Visualisation" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galactic Circos" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galactic_circos> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/record/4494146> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/visualisation/tutorials/circos/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ] ;
    schema1:name "Visualisation with Circos" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Create a number of Circos plots using the Galaxy tool
- Familiarise yourself with the various different track types""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/visualisation/tutorials/circos/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "22" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/hands-on-for-visualization-of-climate-data-using-netcdf-xarray-map-plotting-tutorial> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0610> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Soumya Jha" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Getting your hands-on climate data",
        "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helena Rasche" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2024-06-27"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2022-10-26"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

The data used in this tutorial is [ECMWF Reanalysis 5 hourly data on single levels](https://cds.climate.copernicus.eu/cdsapp#!/dataset/reanalysis-era5-single-levels?tab=overview). We are interested in the following variables: air temperature at 2 metres, latitude, longitude and time. Our main objective is to plot the global air temperature at 2 metres with respect to time. For this we will be using the netCDF xarray tool available in the Galaxy Europe (or your favourite Galaxy Instance) server.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - What is xarray map plotting?
 - How to plot NetCDF data using xarray map plotting tool?
 - What are the different types of projections and colormap options available in the tool?


## Learning Objectives

- Learn about plotting Climate Data
- Learn about NetCDF xarray map plotting tool
- Learn about visualizing the climate data using NetCDF xarray map plotting using the different kinds of projections and colormap options""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Ecology",
        "pangeo" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/ecology/tutorials/x-array-map-plot/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6621460> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ] ;
    schema1:name "Visualization of Climate Data using  NetCDF xarray Map Plotting" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Learn about plotting Climate Data
- Learn about NetCDF xarray map plotting tool
- Learn about visualizing the climate data using NetCDF xarray map plotting using the different kinds of projections and colormap options""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/ecology/tutorials/x-array-map-plot/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "9" .

