    schema1:dateModified "2024-06-14"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2022-06-02"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

Workflows are a powerful Galaxy feature that allows you to scale up your analysis by performing an end-to-end analysis with a single click of a button. In order to aid interpretation of workflow results, *workflow reports* may be configured to combine and display the most important analysis results in a single, customizable view.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - What are workflow reports?
 - How can I view a workflow report?
 - How can I customize a workflow report?
 - How can I share a workflow report with others?


## Learning Objectives

- Understanding, viewing and creating workflow reports""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Using Galaxy and Managing your Data",
        "workflows" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/galaxy-interface/tutorials/workflow-reports/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "ggplot2" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/ggplot2> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ] ;
    schema1:name "Workflow Reports" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches "- Understanding, viewing and creating workflow reports" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/galaxy-interface/tutorials/workflow-reports/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "9" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/hands-on-for-writing-automated-tests-for-galaxy-tutorial> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3372> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Galaxy Administrators" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "John Davis" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "John Chilton" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Basic knowledge of Python and JavaScript",
        "Familiarity with basic Git commands",
        "Galaxy Code Architecture",
        "Mac OS or Linux that can run Galaxy & your favorite IDE or editor" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "John Chilton" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "John Davis" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helena Rasche" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-11-05"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2022-07-15"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

The Galaxy code base contains thousands of tests that include tests of different types (unit vs. functional vs. end-to-end; client vs. backend, etc.) that are supported by a variety of testing frameworks and libraries. In this tutorial, we will offer a small, yet representative sample of the types of tests you might write, as well as the concepts and issues you may need to be familiar with when writing tests for Galaxy code, whether as part of a new feature you are implementing, or as a standalone contribution to Galaxy's testing code.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Learning Objectives

- Learn about the different types of automated tests in Galaxy
- Learn to write API tests
- Learn to write unit tests""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Development in Galaxy" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Writing Automated Tests for Galaxy" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Learn about the different types of automated tests in Galaxy
- Learn to write API tests
- Learn to write unit tests""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/dev/tutorials/writing_tests/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "6" .

