<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/importing-files-from-public-atlases> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Wendi Bacon" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Julia Jakiela" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Combining single cell datasets after pre-processing",
        "Generating a single cell matrix using Alevin",
        "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Mehmet Tekman" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Pavankumar Videm" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Julia Jakiela" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helena Rasche" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Wendi Bacon" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Pablo Moreno" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-03-11"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2023-11-14"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

Public single cell datasets seem to accumulate by the second. Well annotated, quality datasets are slightly trickier to find, which is why projects like the [Single Cell Expression Atlas](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/gxa/sc/home) (SCXA) exist - to curate datasets for public use. Here, we will guide you through transforming data imported from the SCXA repository into the input file required for the [Filter, Plot, Explore tutorial](http://localhost:4000/training-material/topics/single-cell/tutorials/scrna-case_basic-pipeline/tutorial.html) and we will also show how to use the public atlases for your own research.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How do I use the EBI Single Cell Expression Atlas and Human Cell Atlas?
 - How can I reformat and manipulate the downloaded files to create the correct input for downstream analysis?


## Learning Objectives

- You will retrieve raw data from the EBI Single Cell Expression Atlas and Human Cell Atlas.
- You will manipulate the metadata and matrix files.
- You will combine the metadata and matrix files into an AnnData or Seurat object for downstream analysis.""" ;
