<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/inferring-bacterial-pangenomes-with-gene-based-approaches> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3301> ;
    schema1:description "The advent of the whole-genome sequencing era in bacteria led to both the initial definition of the term 'pangenome' and the rapid realisation that pangenomic methods would be indispensable to understanding bacterial genomics. Due to the rapidly rising scale of bacterial genomics of the early 2010's coupled with the computational constraints of the time, many bacterial pangenome methods were developed focusing exclusively on protein-coding DNA sequences. This type of method remains widely used to this day, and they continue to be actively developed and extended beyond their initial constraints, including their limitation to protein-coding regions. In this webinar, we will discuss how these methods work and why they are particularly useful in understanding bacterial pangenomes, as well as survey recent and ongoing developments to these methods." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Inferring bacterial pangenomes with gene-based approaches" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/inferring-bacterial-pangenomes-gene-based-approaches> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/inferring-cell-cell-communication-through-spatial-transcriptomics-data> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_2229>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3308>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3391> ;
    schema1:description "By leveraging spatial coordinate information with expression levels of ligands and target receptors, computational methods developed for analysing spatial transcriptomics data can predict biologically relevant signalling activity. This activity is important for healthy tissue function and disease pathogenesis.This webinar will present an overview of published computational approaches and databases used to infer spatially confined secreted and/or cell-contact mediated signalling, with a particular focus on data generated from the 10X Genomics Visium assay. Furthermore, methods to link inferred cell signalling with specific cell types and downstream pathway activation will be briefly explored." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Inferring cell-cell communication through spatial transcriptomics data" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/inferring-cell-cell-communication-through-spatial-transcriptomics-data> .

