<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/integrating-knowledge-of-proteins-and-small-molecules-with-uniprotkb> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0078>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0154>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3172> ;
    schema1:description """A complete understanding of biological systems requires integration of knowledge of the regulation and function of small molecule metabolites – the metabolome – with that of genes, transcripts, and proteins (the genome, transcriptome, and metabolome). \r
\r
The UniProt knowledgebase UniProtKB supports biomedical research by providing a comprehensive, high quality and freely accessible resource of protein sequences and functional information. UniProtKB integrates, interprets and standardizes data from a range of sources including the scientific literature, tools for protein sequence analysis, other knowledge resources and databases, and automatic annotation systems, to provide a detailed overview of the available protein knowledge. \r
\r
This webinar will provide an introduction into how UniProtKB captures and represents knowledge of small molecule metabolites using the Rhea knowledgebase of biochemical reactions, based on the chemical ontology ChEBI, and how UniProt users can access and exploit that knowledge.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Integrating knowledge of proteins and small molecules with UniProtKB" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/integrating-knowledge-proteins-and-small-molecules-uniprotkb> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/integrating-publications-into-bioinformatics-analysis> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Levchenko" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Magnus Palmblad" ] ;
    schema1:description """Overview\r
\r
Research literature contains a wealth of information that can be extracted at scale using text-mining technologies. To make text-mining data available to a wider group of researchers, the publications database, Europe PMC, provides access to millions of text-mined annotations, such as genes and proteins, metabolites, species, mutations, phosphorylations, interactions, associations, and many more. \r
\r
This webinar will demonstrate how to access text-mined literature evidence programmatically, and how to supplement it with bibliographic information for advanced use cases. Two of such use cases, mapping of tropical disease research by author affiliation geoparsing and comparison of applicability of analytical chemistry techniques, will be introduced by invited speaker, Dr. Magnus Palmblad.\r
\r
This webinar was recorded on 2nd October 2019. It is best viewed in full-screen mode using Google Chrome. The slides from this webinar can be downloaded below.\r
\r
Audience\r
\r
This webinar is aimed at individuals who wish to learn about accessing text-mined literature evidence programmatically. No prior knowledge of bioinformatics is required, but undergraduate level knowledge of biology would be useful.\r
\r
See the EMBL-EBI training pages for a list of upcoming webinars.\r
\r
No prior knowledge of bioinformatics is required, but an undergraduate level knowledge of biology would be useful.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatic analysis",
        "Europe PMC",
        "REST API" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Integrating publications into bioinformatics analysis" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/course/integrating-publications-bioinformatics-analysis> .

