<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/introduction-to-mixed-models> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helen Brown" ] ;
    schema1:description """1. Introduction\r
\r
2. What Are Mixed Models?\r
\r
3. Potential Advantages of Mixed Models\r
\r
4. Historical Perspective\r
\r
5. Example 1: Biological &amp; Technical Replicates\r
\r
6. Example 2: Rabbit Inspiration Time – Sources of Variation\r
\r
7. Non-Normal Data\r
\r
8. Example 3: Non-normal data\r
\r
9. Trials Involving Comparisons Between Units\r
\r
10. Example 4: Crossover Trial\r
\r
11. Repeated Measures Data\r
\r
12. Practical considerations – Negative Variance Components\r
\r
13. Significance Testing for Fixed Effects\r
\r
14. Model Assumptions &amp; Model Checking Methods\r
\r
15. Sample Size Estimation\r
\r
16. Explaining A Mixed Model\r
\r
17. Statistical Packages""" ;
