<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/kew-tree-of-life-project-discovering-the-phylogeny-of-all-flowering-plants> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3168>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3673> ;
    schema1:description """High throughput sequencing has transformed approaches to phylogenetic analysis, allowing the construction of trees based on multiple loci and consideration of incongruence between nuclear and plastid data. This webinar will highlight how the Hyb-Seq method enriches whole-genome sequence data for target genes and can be used on preserved specimens, enabling a cost-effective and practicable strategy for the rigorous investigation of plant evolutionary relationships over a broad taxonomic scope.  \r
\r
The Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew, has already generated a phylogeny covering over half of all flowering plant genera and aims to complete the tree (at the generic level) by 2025.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Kew tree of life project: discovering the phylogeny of all flowering plants" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/kew-tree-life-project-discovering-phylogeny-all-flowering-plants> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/key-terms-a-learning-game-for-conceptual-consolidation> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Ontologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Trainers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Educators" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Pedro Fernandes" ] ;
    schema1:description """A short presentation describing a new participatory method that easily engages course participants in gamestorming and helps to consolidate new knowledge, by collectively agreeing on ways to place concepts in mind maps.
	"Key-terms" is a classroom game that can be played in a variety of contexts, from group discussions to several forms of Non-Formal learning (e.g. training) events. It helps a lot in engaging a small audience, following any wrap-up (recapitulation) session. Learners capture terms that they find conceptually important and contribute to a merged list. Then they are invited to pin these terms on a cork board in specified ways, such as placing related terms together (clustering), placing high order terms above lower order ones, etc. The instructor/facilitator can intervene at several levels in minimal, non-disturbing interventions, that help to contextualise what is happening. A depiction of how concepts are perceived in a class emerges quickly. This is, in practice, a visual concept map. The technique is being tested in the context of technical training provision, with the help of IT.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Education",
        "Instruction",
        "Training" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "\"Key-terms\", a learning game for conceptual consolidation" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GOBLET" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.mygoblet.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.mygoblet.org/training-portal/materials/key-terms-learning-game-conceptual-consolidation> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/kitematic-galaxy-rna-workbench> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """These videos show how to launch the Galaxy RNA workbench for RNA-Seq analysis through Kitematic and the how to use the Galaxy RNA workbench user interface through an interactive tour. Galaxy users can walk through the tour to get accustomed with the workbench's functionalities, interacting with it by uploading data, submitting analyses, and visualize results.\r
\r
This Galaxy instance is launched using Kitematic, and loading the Galaxy RNA workbench directly from Docker Hub. Kitematic enables end users run publicly available docker images through a user friendly interface, where containers can be searched, downloaded, and launched to try Docker, or to use the services provided within the desired containers.\r
\r
The Galaxy RNA workbench is developed by the RNA Bioinformatics Center (RBC). This center is one of the eight service units of the German Network for Bioinformatics Infrastructure, running the German ELIXIR Node.""" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Kitematic Galaxy-RNA-Workbench" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "de.NBI" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Germany" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.denbi.de/online-training-media-library/400-kitematic-galaxy-rna-workbench> .

