<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/making-sense-of-genomic-data-covid-19-web-based-bioinformatics> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "scientific researchers, public health professionals and clinicians " ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Our core educator team can be found on our course page." ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Our list of contributors can be found on our course page." ] ;
    schema1:description """**Develop your understanding of bioinformatics and SARS-CoV-2 data analysis:**\r
Although testing and sequencing capacity has improved, in low-and-middle-income countries (LMICs) bioinformatics analyses still represent a bottleneck in the implementation of effective viral surveillance programmes.\r
\r
This course will introduce you to SARS-CoV-2 bioinformatics pipelines. You will then learn how to use the web-based tool Galaxy for bioinformatics analyses, including how to input and process data, build a pipeline, and use existing workflows.\r
\r
**Explore the process of identifying SARS-CoV-2 variants across global networks:**\r
Using COVID-19 as an example, you’ll be introduced to genomic epidemiology through the lens of data sharing across global networks. You will examine best practices for data sharing in public databases.\r
\r
Then, you will move on to explore phylogenetics, the best tools to use, and the models developed for SARS-CoV-2 analyses. To tie it all together, you will learn how to create and share public health reports.\r
\r
**Learn from global experts in bioinformatics:**\r
The educators and developers of this course are experts in bioinformatics working on data analyses and SARS-CoV-2 bioinformatics in different parts of the world. With this experience, the team is uniquely positioned to help you understand and get a step up in web-based SARS-CoV-2 bioinformatics.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Galaxy",
        "Genomic data",
        "NGS bioinformatics",
        "Next generation sequencing data analysis",
        "bioinformatics",
        "coronavirus",
        "covid-19",
        "data visualization",
        "data-analysis",
        "genomics",
        "nexstrain",
        "pipelines",
        "sars-cov-2" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "FREE online course" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Making sense of genomic data: COVID-19 web-based bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "COG-Train" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.cogconsortium.uk/priority-areas/training/> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.futurelearn.com/courses/making-sense-of-genomic-data-covid-19-web-based-bioinformatics> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/making-sense-of-massive-genomic-data-by-indexing-at-scale> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "As genomics and metagenomics datasets continue to grow in size, and assembled genomes accumulate due to progress in sequencing and assembly, efficient indexing is critical for enabling discovery and analysis across studies. This webinar introduces approaches to organising and searching large-scale genomic data, exploring how indexing strategies improve data accessibility, reproducibility, and analytical power. We’ll discuss current challenges, practical frameworks, and future directions for handling genomics at the population scale." ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Making sense of massive genomic data by indexing at scale" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/making-sense-massive-genomic-data-indexing-scale> .

