<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/molecular-evolution-bioinformatics-iv> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """In the previous course in the Specialization, we learned how to compare genes, proteins, and genomes.  One way we can use these methods is in order to construct a "Tree of Life" showing how a large collection of related organisms have evolved over time.

In the first half of the course, we will discuss approaches for evolutionary tree construction that have been the subject of some of the most cited scientific papers of all time, and show how they can resolve quandaries from finding the origin of a deadly virus to locating the birthplace of modern humans.

In the second half of the course, we will shift gears and examine the old claim that birds evolved from dinosaurs.  How can we prove this?  In particular, we will examine a result that claimed that peptides harvested from a T. rex fossil closely matched peptides found in chickens. In particular, we will use methods from computational proteomics to ask how we could assess whether this result is valid or due to some form of contamination.

Finally, you will learn how to apply popular bioinformatics software tools to reconstruct an evolutionary tree of ebolaviruses and identify the source of the recent Ebola epidemic that caused global headlines.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "algorithms",
        "computer-science",
        "health-informatics",
        "life-sciences" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Molecular Evolution (Bioinformatics IV)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Coursera" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.coursera.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.coursera.org/learn/molecular-evolution> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/molecular-formula-assignment-and-mass-recalibration-with-mfassignr-package> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3172> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Simeon Schum" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helge Hecht" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Kristina Gomoryova" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses",
        "Introduction to Metabolomics",
        "Mass spectrometry: LC-MS data processing" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Kristina Gomoryova" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helge Hecht" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-05-19"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-05-19"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

This training covers the multi-element molecular formula (MF) assignment using the MFAssignR tool. It was originally developed by for the analysis of untargeted mass spectrometry data coming from complex environmental mixtures. The package contains several functions including noise assessment, isotope filtering, internal mass recalibration, and formula assignment. 


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - What are the main steps of untargeted metabolomics LC-MS data pre-processing?
 - How to annotate small molecules contained in mass spectrometry analyses of complex mixtures using the MFAssignR package?


## Learning Objectives

- To learn about the main steps in the pre-processing of untargeted metabolomics LC-MS data.
- To try hands-on analysis using the real data.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Metabolomics" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/metabolomics/tutorials/mfassignr/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/records/13768009> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "MFAssignR" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/mfassignr> ] ;
    schema1:name "Molecular formula assignment and mass recalibration with MFAssignR package" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- To learn about the main steps in the pre-processing of untargeted metabolomics LC-MS data.
- To try hands-on analysis using the real data.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/metabolomics/tutorials/mfassignr/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "1" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/molecular-profiling-in-oncology> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-07-09"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """The video “Molecular Profiling in Oncology” was created as part of the CGI-Clinics project. Aimed at a broad audience, it focuses on the clinical utility of tumor genomic profiling. It explains how molecular profiling extends traditional cancer classification by incorporating specific genetic and molecular alterations to better predict treatment response. \r
The video explores barriers such as cost, access, and variability in patient response, and introduces CGI-Clinics as a collaborative effort to democratize genomic interpretation across Europe. Emphasis is placed on equipping both patients and clinicians with the knowledge to make informed, equitable treatment decisions.\r
This project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon Europe programme under grant agreement CGI-Clinics 101057509.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:identifier "10.5281/zenodo.15846904" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Molecular Profiling in Oncology" ;
    schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/records/15846905> .

