<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/nanopublication-tutorial-at-eswc-2025> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_4012> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Developers" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Basic knowledge of semantic technologies." ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Piotr Sowiński" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Vincent Emonet" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Tobias Kuhn" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Barbara Magagna" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:dateCreated "2025-06-02"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-06-02"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description "Nanopublications are a rapidly growing technology, fully based on Semantic Web standards and principles. At this tutorial, participants are introduced to the concept and then immediately get hands-on experience in creating and publishing their own nanopublications as well as querying and using the available content. The tutorial covers how to create nanopublications manually via web forms and automatically via software libraries, how to retrieve published nanopublications from the decentralized network, how to craft own web forms via templates, how to query the network with SPARQL, and how trust and communities are handled. Moreover, participants get brief introductions into a selection of existing systems and approaches that are based on this technology. The attendees will get supervision and advice from the core people driving the nanopublication technology, and will have time during the tutorial to make some first concrete steps in applying it to their areas of interest. At the end, attendees will have a comprehensive basic foundation on the theory and practice of nanopublication technology." ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Semantic Web",
        "decentralization",
        "nanopublication" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Nanopublication Tutorial at ESWC 2025" ;
    schema1:teaches """The learning goals are the following:\r
\r
- Participants know the motivation and vision behind nanopublications\r
- Participants know the technical basis of nanopublications and how they relate to other semantic technologies\r
- Participants can create their own nanopublications, via the Nanodash user interface as well as programmatically\r
- Participants know how to publish and query nanopublications\r
- Participants know the basics of how to create their own nanopublication templates\r
- Participants understand how agents are identified and trusted on the decentralized nanopublication network\r
- Participants have made their first steps in using nanopublications for a small project of their choosing""" ;
    schema1:url <https://nanopub.net/docs/tutorials/eswc2025/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/nanoxtract-nanomaterials-image-analysis-tool-powered-by-the-enalos-cloud-platform> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "NovaMechanics Ltd" ] ;
    schema1:description """The Enalos Cloud platform, developed by NovaMechanics Ltd, is an online, freely available toxicity and drug discovery platform, that hosts predictive models released as web services, which aim to address the need to reduce the time and cost spent on experimental testing during the drug discovery and the risk assessment procedures for small molecules and NMs.\r
\r
One of the platform's recent features is a tool to analyze Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images depicting nanomaterials(NMs) and extract useful descriptors that can be used to provide a much more nuanced description of the sample composition and/or as inputs to toxicity prediction models. The image analysis tool allows the user to upload a TEM image (either the raw file or extracted from a publication) and in a short time with simple steps to obtain 18 additional nano-descriptors based on geometric indices. These can be used in predictive models already existing in the Enalos Cloud platform or with in-house models""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Cloud Computing",
        "nanomaterials" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "NanoXtract: Nanomaterials Image Analysis Tool Powered by the Enalos Cloud Platform" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "NanoCommons" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.nanocommons.eu/> ] ;
    schema1:url <http://enaloscloud.novamechanics.com/EnalosWebApps/NanoXtract/instructions.zul> .

