<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/pseudobulk-analysis-with-decoupler-and-edger> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Diana Chiang Jurado" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Clustering 3K PBMCs with Scanpy",
        "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Pavankumar Videm" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Diana Chiang Jurado" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-05-09"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-02-12"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

Pseudobulk analysis is a powerful technique that bridges the gap between single-cell and bulk RNA-seq data. It involves aggregating gene expression data from groups of cells within the same biological replicate, such as a mouse or patient, typically based on clustering or cell type annotations.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How does pseudobulk analysis help in understanding cell-type-specific gene expression changes?
 - What steps are required to prepare single-cell data (e.g., clustering, annotation, and metadata addition) for pseudobulk analysis?
 - How can we use pseudobulk data prepared with Decoupler to perform differential expression analysis using edgeR in Galaxy?


## Learning Objectives

- Understand the principles of pseudobulk analysis in single-cell data
- Understand and generate the pseudobulk expression matrix with Decoupler
- Perform differential expression analysis using edgeR""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Single Cell",
        "pseudobulk",
        "transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/single-cell/tutorials/pseudobulk-analysis/workflows/> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "edgeR" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/edger> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "SCANPY" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/scanpy> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "anndata" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/anndata> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://zenodo.org/records/15275834> ] ;
    schema1:name "Pseudobulk Analysis with Decoupler and EdgeR" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Understand the principles of pseudobulk analysis in single-cell data
- Understand and generate the pseudobulk expression matrix with Decoupler
- Perform differential expression analysis using edgeR""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/single-cell/tutorials/pseudobulk-analysis/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "8" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/psls20-practical-statistics-for-the-life-sciences-2020> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Healthcare" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Academia/ Research Institution" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Industry" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Non-Profit Organisation" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "The Gulbenkian Training Programme in Bioinformatics" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Jeroen Gilis" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Lieven Clement" ] ;
    schema1:description """This intermediate level course is one of our Foundations courses. It covers essential statistical concepts and methods for extracting insights from empirical data in the life sciences. The course positions applied statistics, starting from important aspects of experimental design and data exploration. We then move into statistical modeling and data analysis. We will focus on the link between linear regression and analysis of variance. Together, these methods contribute to the study of General Linear Models. The course also introduces the basics of non-parametric testing, and addresses categorical data analysis and logistic regression.\r
\r
The concepts and methods are exclusively introduced via case-studies in the life sciences. For every study we elaborate on a concrete research question and then provide a study design, which is followed by data exploration. Next, we will focus on how to model the data and elaborate on the link between model parameters and the subject matter research question.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:identifier "10.5281/zenodo.3813545" ;
    schema1:keywords " Statistics",
        "General Linear Models" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Documentation",
        "Exercise",
        "Handout",
        "Scripts" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "PSLS20 - Practical Statistics for the Life Sciences (2020)" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Portugal" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixir-portugal.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://gtpb.github.io/PSLS20> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/psls22-practical-statistics-for-the-life-sciences> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "All postgraduates" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Graduate students" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Milan Malfait" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Jeroen Gilis" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Lieven Clement" ] ;
    schema1:description """The concepts and methods are exclusively introduced via case-studies in the life sciences. For every study we elaborate on a concrete research question and then provide a study design, which is followed by data exploration. Next, we will focus on how to model the data and elaborate on the link between model parameters and the subject matter research question.\r
\r
Objectives\r
The case studies will enable the participants to build self-confidence in understanding, reading and communicating on data and data analysis selecting appropriate statistical methods and software tools for analysing different types of data interpreting the result of a statistical data analysis in terms of subject matter research questions and reporting them appropriately. There is a strong emphasis in reproducible research by extensively using R/Rmarkdown scripts. This approach will enable the participants to weave statistical analyses, code, results and interpretation in webpages and PDF documents so that their entire data analysis workflow is transparent and reproducible. The course materials are designed in R/Rmarkdown, kickstarting the course participants into developing their own scripts.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "PSLS22 Practical Statistics for the Life Sciences" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Portugal" ;
            schema1:url <https://elixir-portugal.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://gtpb.github.io/PSLS22/> .

