<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/r-for-beginners> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3316> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr Euan McDonnell" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "University of Liverpool" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Liverpool Computational Biology Facility (CBF)" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr Eva Caamano-Gutierrez" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr John Heap" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Some understanding of programming, what it can do and what it can be used for." ;
    schema1:description """This intensive 3-day course introduces the **statistical software and programming language R** with specific focus on its applications in **life sciences and clinical research**.  \r
\r
Each day of the course focuses on a specific topic, as follows: \r
\r
**Day 1. R fundamentals:** An introduction to the language and its interface. Get familiar with R, R studio, operators, variables, functions, directories and the script editor. \r
\r
**Day 2. Data visualisation:** Providing you with the tools to make publication quality plots using R. \r
\r
**Day 3. Statistical analysis in R:** We will cover statistical methods ranging from simple univariate statistics to dealing with large volumes of variables using Principal Component Analysis.  \r
\r
The course is designed to be undertaken at the student’s own pace with **1 on 1 targeted support** from our team of experts. Our team comprises **computational biologists, data scientists, and bioinformaticians** with a strong background in both wet and dry lab projects. Empowering you on a self-learning path, you will **gain essential skills to apply R to your research** projects and to **generate publication-ready outputs**.  \r
\r
We will also be available to assist with your learning up to two months after the final day of the course. Moreover, upon completion of the course, all delegates will be invited to join a peer-peer support community “RClub” to enhance their research/analytical work. \r
\r
This course runs twice a year – in **March and in October**. The next cohort will be held online on **6th- 8th March 2024**. \r
\r
**Sign up using the website link provided before the 20th February**. Places are limited and registration may close earlier if places are full. \r
\r
The course has a cost of £300 for all academic delegates; £500 for all delegates from public institutions (not academic). If you are a delegate from the University of Liverpool you can access student bursaries, that you can apply to using this link.  Note the delegate **bursaries applications close on the 12th February 2024**. You must submit both a registration form and a bursary application to be considered. \r
\r
For more advanced data analysis (e.g. omics) and data science projects, we strongly recommend you also sign up for our **R for Data Science** course.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "Coding",
        "Computational biology",
        "Computer science",
        "Programming" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Bioinformatics",
        "Coding",
        "Computational Biology",
        "Computer Science",
        "Education",
        "Programming" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "R for Beginners" ;
    schema1:teaches """* To understand R's basic functionality and begin to write your own code.\r
* To generate publication-reading plots with base R's plotting utility and the ggplot2 package.\r
* To perform routine statistical testing.\r
""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/computational-biology-facility/events/r-beginners/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/r-for-data-science> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3307>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3308>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3474> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr Jordan Tzvetkov" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr Euan McDonnell" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Liverpool Computational Biology Facility (CBF)" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "University of Liverpool" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr John Heap" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Dr Eva Caamano-Gutierrez" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Understanding of how to perform basic coding in R, including reading in, saving and formatting data, as well as plotting." ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:description """This 4-day intensive course **builds on the content from our [R for Beginners course](https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/computational-biology-facility/events/r-beginners/)**, providing an excellent toolbox for multidisciplinary scientists in areas of **life sciences and biomedicine (particularly oriented to omics datasets)**.  \r
\r
**Day 1. Recap of R fundamentals and introduction to loops in R:** Building on the R fundamentals from our R for beginners course, we will teach you how to use programmatic loops. Loops allow you to automate repetitive tasks and accelerate your analysis, making your scripts neater and saving you time. \r
\r
**Day 2. Applying loops with custom functions:** We will teach you how to apply loops to different data structures and how to create your very own functions in R, allowing you to customise your analysis based on your needs.  \r
\r
**Day 3. Introduction to Data Science and Data Analysis Pipelines:** We will cover the key steps in a data analysis pipeline including normalisation, batch correction, clustering, and how to deal with selecting variables in a multivariate dataset using supervised modelling (e.g. PLS-DA).  \r
\r
**Day 4. Introduction to machine learning and functional enrichment:** We will introduce the powerful machine learning approach Random forests as well as apply gene ontology functional enrichment analysis, finishing with applying your new skills to  two RNAseq case studies.  \r
\r
The course is designed to be undertaken at the student’s own pace with **1 on 1 targeted support** from our team of experts. Our team comprises **computational biologists, data scientists, and bioinformaticians** with a strong background in both wet and dry lab projects. Empowering you on a self-learning path, you will gain **essential skills to apply R to your research projects** and to **generate publication-ready outputs**.  \r
\r
We will also be available to assist with your learning up to two months after the final day of the course. Moreover, upon completion of the course, all delegates will be invited to join a peer-peer support community “RClub” to enhance their research/analytical work. \r
\r
 \r
\r
This course runs twice a year – in **April and in November**. The next cohort will be held online on **19th-22nd November 2024**. \r
\r
**Sign up using the link provided before the 5th November**. Places are limited and registration may close earlier if places are full. \r
\r
The course has a cost of £400 for all academic delegates; £600 for all delegates from public institutions (not academic). Industry delegates may submit an application and will be quoted on an individual basis. If you are a delegate from the University of Liverpool you can access student bursaries, that you can apply to using this link.  Note the **delegate bursaries applications close on the 22nd October  2024**. You must submit both a registration form and a bursary application to be considered.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Bioinformatics",
        "Coding",
        "Computational Biology",
        "Computer Science",
        "Data Analysis",
        "Data Science",
        "Machine Learning",
        "Programming",
        "Transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Bioinformatics",
        "Coding",
        "Computational Biology",
        "Computer Science",
        "Data Science",
        "Education",
        "Machine Learning",
        "Programming",
        "Transcriptomics" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "R for Data Science: November 2024" ;
    schema1:teaches """* Learn how to code common computational tools including loops and custom functions.\r
* Apply these tools to datasets in order to expediate your analysis.\r
* Perform common univariate and multivariate analysis, including PCA.\r
* Introduce machine-learning.\r
* Perform functional enrichment analysis.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/computational-biology-facility/events/r-data-science/> .

