<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/research-data-management-and-sharing> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Life Science Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course will provide learners with an introduction to research data management and sharing. After completing this course, learners will understand the diversity of data and their management needs across the research data lifecycle, be able to identify the components of good data management plans, and be familiar with best practices for working with data including the organization, documentation, and storage and security of data. Learners will also understand the impetus and importance of archiving and sharing data as well as how to assess the trustworthiness of repositories. \r
\r
Today, an increasing number of funding agencies, journals, and other stakeholders are requiring data producers to share, archive, and plan for the management of their data. In order to respond to these requirements, researchers and information professionals will need the data management and curation knowledge and skills that support the long-term preservation, access, and reuse of data. Effectively managing data can also help optimize research outputs, increase the impact of research, and support open scientific inquiry. After completing this course, learners will be better equipped to manage data throughout the entire research data lifecycle from project planning to the end of the project when data ideally are shared and made available within a trustworthy repository.\r
\r
This course was developed by the Curating Research Assets and Data Using Lifecycle Education (CRADLE) Project in collaboration with EDINA at the University of Edinburgh. \r
\r
This course was made possible in part by the Institute of Museum and Library Services under award #RE-06-13-0052-13. The views, findings, conclusions or recommendations expressed in this Research Data Management and Sharing MOOC do not necessarily represent those of the Institute of Museum and Library Services.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Research Data Management and Sharing" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Coursera" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.coursera.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.coursera.org/learn/data-management> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/research-data-management-and-sharing-mooc> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3071> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Life Science Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "University of Carolina" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "University of Edinburgh" ] ;
    schema1:description """Get the essentials about how to manage and share research data from this free introductory online course.\r
\r
The Research Data Management and Sharing MOOC (Massive Open Online Course) covers:\r
\r
Understanding research data – the diversity of data and managing it across the research lifecycle\r
Data management planning – the components of good data management plans\r
Working with data – good practices for data organisation, documentation, versioning, citation, storage and security\r
Sharing data – understanding confidentiality, data ownership and licensing\r
Archiving data – importance of archiving and how to assess repositories\r
The course materials are short, video-based lessons and peer assessments which you access online on the Coursera website. There is no formal qualification if you pass the course though you can pay a small charge to get a certificate of accomplishment.\r
\r
It’s designed to last five weeks but you can proceed at your own pace if you don’t want to do the graded assessments for the certificate. There are specific dates for enrolment.\r
\r
The course is run by the University of Edinburgh and the University of North Carolina.""" ;
