<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/rna-seq-data-analysis-with-chipster> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Lehtivaara" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Eija Korpelainen" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Eija Korpelainen" ] ;
    schema1:description """This course introduces RNA-seq data analysis methods, tools and file formats. It covers all the steps from quality control and alignment to quantification and differential expression analysis, and also experimental design is discussed. The user-friendly Chipster software is used in the exercises, so no Unix or R experience is required and the course is thus suitable for everybody. The course takes two days (or one long day if you omit exercise sheets 3 and 4). You will learn how to\r
\r
  * check the quality of reads with FastQC and PRINSEQ\r
  * remove bad quality data with Trimmomatic\r
  * infer strandedness with RseQC\r
  * align reads to the reference genome with HISAT2 and STAR\r
  * visualize aligned reads in genomic context using the Chipster genome browser\r
  * perform alignment level quality control using RseQC and SAMtools\r
  * quantify expression by counting reads per genes using HTSeq\r
  * check the experiment level quality with PCA plots and heatmaps\r
  * analyze differential expression with DESeq2 and edgeR\r
  * take multiple factors (including batch effects) into account in differential expression analysis\r
\r
Course material (2017) is available at [the course website](https://chipster.csc.fi/manual/courses.html#rna) and it includes\r
\r
  * slides\r
  * lecture videos\r
  * exercises (data is available on Chipster server in the example sessions listed in the exercise sheets, and we also provide ready-made analysis sessions which you can use as a reference when doing exercises on your own.)\r
   1. ENCODE data with two samples. These exercises cover the whole workflow.\r
   2. drosphila pasilla dataset. These exercises focus on differential expression analysis and how to take confounding factors into account.\r
    3. parathyroid dataset. As before, but even harder.\r
    4. lung and lymphnode comparison. Test your new skills with minimal instructions!""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Video",
        "course materials" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Chipster" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/chipster> ] ;
    schema1:name "RNA-seq data analysis" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Finland" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.csc.fi/web/training> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "CSC - IT Center for Science" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.csc.fi> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://chipster.csc.fi/manual/courses.html#rna> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/rna-seq-from-counts-2-biological-insights> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0203>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3170>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3308>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3321> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Researchers" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Isabel Duarte" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Basic R knowledge" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "rmagno@pattern.institute" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "active" ;
    schema1:dateCreated "2025-06-01"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-06-01"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-06-01"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Overview\r
\r
This hands-on tutorial introduces a reproducible and interpretable RNA‑Seq analysis workflow using R and R Markdown. Starting from a count matrix, participants will learn to assess data quality, perform differential expression analysis, and interpret biological results through visualization and enrichment analysis. The tutorial is designed for learners with basic R experience and provides a step-by-step guide to key concepts in transcriptomics data analysis.\r
\r
## Questions Addressed\r
\r
   - Which genes are significantly differentially expressed between experimental groups?\r
\r
   - How can we link differentially expressed genes to biological processes and functions?\r
\r
   - How to visualize the outcome of the various steps of the transcriptomics data analysis?\r
\r
## Learning Objectives\r
\r
   - Use R Markdown notebooks for literate programming, integrating code, results, and documentation in a single, reproducible workflow.\r
\r
   - Assess quality at the gene count level, using PCA, sample clustering, and diagnostic plots to identify outliers, batch effects, and validate experimental design.\r
\r
   - Perform differential expression analysis with DESeq2, identifying genes with statistically significant changes between conditions.\r
\r
   - Visualize and interpret gene expression results, producing heatmaps, volcano plots, and summary statistics to communicate findings.\r
\r
   - Conduct functional enrichment analysis, mapping gene-level results to biological processes, molecular functions, and pathways (e.g. Gene Ontology enrichment).""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Functional enrichment",
        "PCA",
        "Quality Control",
        "RNA-Seq",
        "Visualisation",
        "bulk",
        "mouse" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "E-Learning",
        "hands-on tutorial" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "ggplot2" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/ggplot2> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "DESeq2" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/DESeq2> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "RNA-seq data package" ;
            schema1:url <https://github.com/patterninstitute/OSD758> ] ;
    schema1:name "RNA-seq | From Counts 2 Biological Insights" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Pattern Institute" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.pattern.institute/training/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """1. Learn to use R markdown notebooks for literate programming.\r
2. Assess quality at the gene count level: Use exploratory analyses (e.g. PCA, clustering) to evaluate sample quality, detect batch effects, and validate the experimental design.\r
3. Conduct differential expression analysis: Identify differentially expressed genes using DESeq2.\r
4. Visualize and interpret results: Create informative plots (e.g. heatmaps, volcano plots) to explore gene expression changes.\r
5. Perform functional enrichment analysis: Link gene expression results to biological functions, pathways, and processes (e.g. GO BP enrichment).""" ;
    schema1:url <https://instructr.github.io/rnaseq_counts2bio/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "V1" .

