<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/sample-size-estimation> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name " Anne Segonds-Pichon" ] ;
    schema1:description """Power analysis is a method to estimate the samples sizes needed to detect statistical effects with high probability. This course provides an introduction to the basic principles of power analysis and goes through the variables for power calculation. It also addresses the issues associated with technical and biological replicates. The program is intended for people who have no prior knowledge of power. Basic statistical knowledge is extremely useful but prior attendance on a statistics course is not compulsary.\r
\r
**Course Content**\r
\r
* Definition of Power\r
* Variables in Power Analysis\r
* Power anlaysis for\r
* Comparing 2 proportions\r
* Comparing 2 means\r
* Comparing more than 2 means\r
* Correlation\r
\r
**Course Material**\r
\r
* [Course Manual (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Introduction_to_Power_Analysis/Power%20Analysis%20Manual.pdf)\r
* [Course Manual (docx)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Introduction_to_Power_Analysis/Power%20Analysis%20Manual.docx)\r
* [Course Slides (pdf)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Introduction_to_Power_Analysis/Power%20Analysis%20Slides.pdf)\r
* [Course Slides (pptx)](http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training/Introduction_to_Power_Analysis/Power%20Analysis%20Slides.pptx)""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:keywords "Babraham Institute",
        "Power analysis",
        "Sample size" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/other-nc> ;
    schema1:name "Sample size estimation" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Bioinformatics and Biomathematics Training Hub" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.earlham.ac.uk/bbsrc-bioinformatics-and-biomathematics-training-hub> ] ;
    schema1:url <http://www.bioinformatics.babraham.ac.uk/training.html#power> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/sampling-distribution> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """In this tutorial, we experience one of the most exciting ideas in statistics--the central limit theorem.  Without it, it would be a lot harder to make any inferences about population parameters given sample statistics.  It tells us that, regardless of what the population distribution looks like, the  distribution of the sample means (you'll learn what that is) can be normal.

Good idea to understand a bit about normal distributions before diving into this tutorial.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Inferential statistics" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Sampling distribution" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Khan Academy Statistics" ;
            schema1:url <https://www.khanacademy.org/math/probability> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.khanacademy.org/math/probability/statistics-inferential/sampling-distribution> .

