<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/sequence-analysis-tools-in-uniprot-blast-and-align> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description "UniProt provides the scientific community with a freely accessible collection of protein sequences and functional information and is one of the most widely used protein resources in the world. BLAST and the multiple sequence alignment are essential methods commonly used to find and compare protein or nucleotide sequences. In this webinar, we will demonstrate how you can access and run these sequence analysis tools in UniProt. We will also show you how to navigate the results and how you can exploit UniProt protein annotation to expand further the analysis of your results. " ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Sequence analysis tools in UniProt: BLAST and Align" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/sequence-analysis-tools-uniprot-blast-and-align-2025> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/sequence-comparison-undergraduate-lab> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Undergraduate students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Bruno Gaeta" ] ;
    schema1:description "This is lab 2 in a series of labs developed as part of a \"bioinformatics for biology undergraduates\" course and targeted at 2nd and 3rd year undergraduates. It is meant to be easily assessible. The lab includes some instructions that are specific to our computer lab environment but the content of the lab should be easy to adapt to other environments. This lab makes use of EMBOSS programs, prss in the FASTA package, as well as the NCBI BLAST website. I usually deliver this lab after a series of lectures discussing dotmatrix plots and sequence alignment. Students enter their answers to the questions into a quiz set up within moodle which allows for easier marking." ;
    schema1:keywords "Dotmatrix plots",
        "Scoring matrices",
        "Sequence alignment" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Sequence comparison - undergraduate lab" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GOBLET" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.mygoblet.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.mygoblet.org/training-portal/materials/sequence-comparison-undergraduate-lab> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/sequence-data-submission-to-ena> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Sonal Henson" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "A linux-based machine or linux emulator",
        "Read data in fastq format",
        "Web browser" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Gareth Price" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Munazah Andrabi" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Khaled Jum'ah" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Katarzyna Kamieniecka" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Krzysztof Poterlowicz" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Helena Rasche" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateCreated "2023-11-01"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:dateModified "2024-06-25"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2023-11-01"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

DNA sequencing has become one of the key technologies in molecular biology, with applications in diagnostics, evolutionary biology, drug discovery, forensics and much more. Drop in sequencing costs and breakthroughs in sequencing technologies has seen increasing utilization of sequencing as a research tool, featuring in thousands of life-science publications every year. \r


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - I am new to sequence submission to a repository.
 - How do I submit sequences and metadata to ENA using the Webin portal and cURL?


## Learning Objectives

- To populate ENA metadata objects through the Webin portal
- To submit raw reads to ENA using FTP""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "beginner" ;
    schema1:identifier "https://gxy.io/GTN:T00369" ;
    schema1:keywords "ENA",
        "FAIR",
        "FAIR Data, Workflows, and Research" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:name "Sequence data submission to ENA" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- To populate ENA metadata objects through the Webin portal
- To submit raw reads to ENA using FTP""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/fair/tutorials/fair-ena/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "5" .

