<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/text-mining-differences-in-chinese-newspaper-articles> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_2269> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Students" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Daniela Schneider" ] ;
    schema1:competencyRequired "Introduction to Galaxy Analyses" ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Teresa Müller" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Saskia Hiltemann" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Björn Grüning" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Daniela Schneider" ] ;
    schema1:creativeWorkStatus "Active" ;
    schema1:dateModified "2025-09-09"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:datePublished "2025-02-28"^^schema1:Date ;
    schema1:description """## Abstract

The British Hong Kong Government censored Chinese newspapers before their publication in the colony in the 1930s.


## About This Material

This is a Hands-on Tutorial from the GTN which is usable either for individual self-study, or as a teaching material in a classroom.


## Questions this  will address

 - How can I automatically compare two Chinese newspaper articles?
 - What characters were censored in a Chinese newspaper published in Hong Kong in the 1930s?


## Learning Objectives

- Learn to clean and compare two texts.
- Extract specific information from texts.
- Visualise your results in a word cloud.""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:keywords "Digital Humanities",
        "text mining" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "e-learning" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Training Datasets" ;
            schema1:url <https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14899614> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Galaxy" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/galaxy> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "Associated Workflows" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/digital-humanities/tutorials/text_mining_chinese/workflows/> ] ;
    schema1:name "Text-Mining Differences in Chinese Newspaper Articles" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GTN" ;
            schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/> ] ;
    schema1:teaches """- Learn to clean and compare two texts.
- Extract specific information from texts.
- Visualise your results in a word cloud.""" ;
    schema1:url <https://training.galaxyproject.org/training-material/topics/digital-humanities/tutorials/text_mining_chinese/tutorial.html> ;
    schema1:version "5" .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/text-mining-exercises> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0218>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3174>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3473>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3697> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Biologists" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "Bioinformaticians" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Lars Juhl Jensen" ],
        [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Evangelos Pafilis" ] ;
    schema1:contributor [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Manimozhiyan Arumugam" ] ;
    schema1:description """Hands-on exercises using a variety of text-mining tools and databases based on text mining, to interpret the results from microbiome studies.\r
\r
The exercises will teach you how to:\r
\r
* automatically highlight named entities in a web page\r
* use named entity recognition for synonym-aware information retrieval\r
* extract associations based on cooccurrence of entities in the literature\r
* discover novel, indirect associations between entities\r
* perform text-mining-based term enrichment analysis""" ;
    schema1:educationalLevel "intermediate" ;
    schema1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0.html> ;
    schema1:mentions [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "ORGANISMS" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/ORGANISMS> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "DISEASES" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/DISEASES> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "EXTRACT" ;
            schema1:url <https://bio.tools/tool/EXTRACT> ],
        [ a schema1:Thing ;
            schema1:name "ARROWSMITH" ;
            schema1:url <http://arrowsmith.psych.uic.edu/> ] ;
    schema1:name "Text-mining exercises" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "ELIXIR Denmark" ;
            schema1:url <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.cpr.ku.dk/> ] ;
    schema1:url <http://jensenlab.org/training/textmining/> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/text-mining-for-biocuration> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0218>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0219> ;
    schema1:description """This webinar, part of the biocuration series, will introduce the topic of text mining and discuss why text mining is used by life sciences data resources in part of their biocuration efforts. \r
\r
To illustrate its use in biocuration, this webinar will discuss how text mining has recently been used by two data resources, Open Targets and UniProt in their curation of biological data.\r
\r
This webinar is the second in our series of webinars on the topic of biocuration. In an ELIXIR survey in 2019 which aimed at identifying training needs in biocuration, the most commonly indicated were programming, development and use of ontologies and database management. In addition skills like programming and extracting data from literature were mentioned as decisive for a successful biocuration career. With our webinar series we want to help to close this gap: Bicourators from different backgrounds and institutes will share their experience on how they have acquired these relevant skills in their job, how they apply these in their works and why they benefit from it. The speakers will also point to helpful tools and resources they use.""" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Recorded webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Text mining for biocuration" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/events/text-mining-biocuration> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/text-mining-key-concepts-and-applications> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:description """EMBL-EBI presents this talk on an introduction to text mining and its applications in service provision. The 1st part of this talk focuses on what text mining is and some of the methods and available tools. The 2nd part looks at how to find articles on Europe PMC - a free literature resource for biomedical and health researchers - and how to build your own text mining pipeline.\r
The final part gives a nice case study showing how Europe PMC's pipeline was integrated into a new drug target validation platform called Open Targets (previously CTTV).""" ;
    schema1:keywords " Text mining",
        "Biomedical Literature",
        "Europe PMC",
        "Literature search" ;
    schema1:learningResourceType "Webinar" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "Text mining: Key concepts and applications" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "European Bioinformatics Institute (EBI)" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk> ],
        [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "EMBL-EBI" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.ebi.ac.uk/training> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.ebi.ac.uk/training/online/course/text-mining-key-concepts-and-applications> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/tgac-metagenomics-from-bench-to-data-analysis> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_3174> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "postdocs" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Victoria ..." ] ;
    schema1:description """This course took place at TGAC on 8-12 September 2014
What is the workshop about?
A substantial part of the course will be devoted to bioinformatics resources and tools relevant in metagenomics data analysis. Participants will start at the bench and end in front of the computers for a problem solving activity utilizing the bioinformatics training they will be receiving during the course.
What will it cover?
To provide an overview of the main aspects involved in metagenomics from the bench to data analysis and discussion around the interpretation and actual examples of the impact and applications of metagenomics derived research
Target Audience:
This course is aimed at advanced PhD students and post-doctoral researchers who are doing or planning to start research in the field of metagenomics.""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Metagenomics" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "TGAC Metagenomics: From bench to data analysis" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
            schema1:name "GOBLET" ;
            schema1:url <http://www.mygoblet.org> ] ;
    schema1:url <https://www.mygoblet.org/training-portal/materials/tgac-metagenomics-bench-data-analysis> .

<https://tess.elixir-europe.org/materials/tgac_seqahead-ngs-current-challenges-and-data-analysis-for-plant-researchers> a schema1:LearningResource ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://bioschemas.org/profiles/TrainingMaterial/1.0-RELEASE> ;
    schema1:about <http://edamontology.org/topic_0080>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0091>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_0780>,
        <http://edamontology.org/topic_3489> ;
    schema1:audience [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "PhD students" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "post-docs" ],
        [ a schema1:Audience ;
            schema1:audienceType "plant researchers" ] ;
    schema1:author [ a schema1:Person ;
            schema1:name "Maria Victoria ..." ] ;
    schema1:description """Hands on course held at TGAC (http://www.tgac.ac.uk/) on Monday 19 to Friday 23 May 2014
What is this workshop about?
The course will combine lectures and led discussions to identify key challenges, opportunities and bottlenecks, with practical session on:
    Automated and standardized data analysis for plant species data
    Data quality checks
    Estimation of reproducibility
    Batch effects
    Statistical concepts
    Importance of standards
    Data formats
    Data integration
What will I learn?
    To discuss and reflect on best practice of experimental design and control for using NGS technologies in their context of addressing biological questions
    Identify key challenges, opportunities and bottlenecks
    Offering a platform for discussion and exploration of the challenges in plant research with a particular emphasis for agri-bioinformatics""" ;
    schema1:keywords "Ngs",
        "Ngs bioinformatics",
        "Plants bioinformatics" ;
    schema1:license <file:///home/runner/work/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/ELIXIR-TrP-KG-training-metadata/notspecified> ;
    schema1:name "TGAC_SeqAhead: NGS current challenges and data analysis for plant researchers" ;
    schema1:provider [ a schema1:Organization ;
